('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000235','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which chemical element is a radioactive noble gas that can accumulate in buildings?','["Aluminium", "Calcium", "Radon", "Iron"]','"Radon"','Radon is produced naturally by radioactive decay and can enter indoor air.','Royal Society of Chemistry: Radon','https://www.rsc.org/periodic-table/element/86/radon','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000236','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which chemical element has the isotope radon-222 produced directly by radium-226 decay?','["Calcium", "Radon", "Iron", "Aluminium"]','"Radon"','Radium-226 emits an alpha particle to become radon-222.','Royal Society of Chemistry: Radon','https://www.rsc.org/periodic-table/element/86/radon','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000237','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which chemical element produces an orange-red glow in a classic gas-discharge sign?','["Neon", "Iron", "Aluminium", "Calcium"]','"Neon"','Pure neon discharge tubes glow reddish orange.','Royal Society of Chemistry: Neon','https://www.rsc.org/periodic-table/element/10/neon','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000238','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which chemical element has a name taken from the Greek word for new?','["Iron", "Aluminium", "Calcium", "Neon"]','"Neon"','Neon was named from neos, meaning new.','Royal Society of Chemistry: Neon','https://www.rsc.org/periodic-table/element/10/neon','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000239','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which chemical element was named from a Greek word meaning stranger?','["Aluminium", "Calcium", "Xenon", "Iron"]','"Xenon"','Xenon derives from xenos, referring to a stranger.','Royal Society of Chemistry: Xenon','https://www.rsc.org/periodic-table/element/54/xenon','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000240','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which chemical element is used as a propellant in some spacecraft ion engines?','["Calcium", "Xenon", "Iron", "Aluminium"]','"Xenon"','Xenon is readily ionized and commonly used in electric propulsion.','Royal Society of Chemistry: Xenon','https://www.rsc.org/periodic-table/element/54/xenon','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000241','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which chemical element has a name derived from a Greek word meaning inactive or lazy?','["Argon", "Iron", "Aluminium", "Calcium"]','"Argon"','Argon''s name reflects its chemical inertness.','Royal Society of Chemistry: Argon','https://www.rsc.org/periodic-table/element/18/argon','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000242','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which chemical element makes up close to 1% of dry air and is its most abundant noble gas?','["Iron", "Aluminium", "Calcium", "Argon"]','"Argon"','Argon accounts for about 0.93% of dry air by volume.','Royal Society of Chemistry: Argon','https://www.rsc.org/periodic-table/element/18/argon','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000243','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which chemical element is a reddish metal whose name is historically associated with Cyprus?','["Aluminium", "Calcium", "Copper", "Iron"]','"Copper"','The Latin name cuprum is associated with Cyprus.','Royal Society of Chemistry: Copper','https://www.rsc.org/periodic-table/element/29/copper','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000244','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which chemical element forms a greenish protective patina on weathered roofs and statues?','["Calcium", "Copper", "Iron", "Aluminium"]','"Copper"','Copper develops corrosion products that can form a green surface layer.','Royal Society of Chemistry: Copper','https://www.rsc.org/periodic-table/element/29/copper','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000245','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which chemical element is so malleable that it can be beaten into extremely thin leaf?','["Gold", "Iron", "Aluminium", "Calcium"]','"Gold"','Gold can be formed into very thin sheets known as gold leaf.','Royal Society of Chemistry: Gold','https://www.rsc.org/periodic-table/element/79/gold','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000246','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which chemical element has the chemical symbol Au, from the Latin aurum?','["Iron", "Aluminium", "Calcium", "Gold"]','"Gold"','Au comes from the Latin word for gold.','Royal Society of Chemistry: Gold','https://www.rsc.org/periodic-table/element/79/gold','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000247','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which chemical element was historically called quicksilver?','["Aluminium", "Calcium", "Mercury", "Iron"]','"Mercury"','Quicksilver is an old name for elemental mercury.','Royal Society of Chemistry: Mercury','https://www.rsc.org/periodic-table/element/80/mercury','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000248','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which chemical element has the chemical symbol Hg, derived from hydrargyrum, meaning water-silver?','["Calcium", "Mercury", "Iron", "Aluminium"]','"Mercury"','Hg comes from a Latinized Greek name referring to liquid silver.','Royal Society of Chemistry: Mercury','https://www.rsc.org/periodic-table/element/80/mercury','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000249','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which chemical element has the chemical symbol Pb, derived from the Latin plumbum?','["Lead", "Iron", "Aluminium", "Calcium"]','"Lead"','Pb preserves the Latin name plumbum.','Royal Society of Chemistry: Lead','https://www.rsc.org/periodic-table/element/82/lead','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000250','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which chemical element is used in dense shielding that attenuates X-rays and gamma rays?','["Iron", "Aluminium", "Calcium", "Lead"]','"Lead"','Lead''s high density and atomic number make it useful for radiation shielding.','Royal Society of Chemistry: Lead','https://www.rsc.org/periodic-table/element/82/lead','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000251','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which fastening system was inspired by burrs sticking to clothing and animal fur?','["Magnetic clasp", "Buttonhole", "Hook-and-loop fastener", "Zip fastener"]','"Hook-and-loop fastener"','George de Mestral developed hook-and-loop fastening after examining the hooks on burrs.','Smithsonian Magazine: The Invention of Velcro','https://www.smithsonianmag.com/innovation/the-invention-of-velcro-180991331/','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000252','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which packaging material began as an unsuccessful idea for textured wallpaper?','["Aluminium foil", "Bubble wrap", "Corrugated cardboard", "Packing peanuts"]','"Bubble wrap"','Alfred Fielding and Marc Chavannes first attempted a plastic textured wallpaper before the material became protective packaging.','Smithsonian Magazine: The Accidental Invention of Bubble Wrap','https://www.smithsonianmag.com/innovation/accidental-invention-bubble-wrap-180971325/','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000253','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What was Play-Doh''s original purpose before it became a children''s modeling compound?','["Cleaning wallpaper", "Sealing windows", "Polishing silver", "Repairing tyres"]','"Cleaning wallpaper"','The material was originally sold as a compound for removing soot from wallpaper.','Smithsonian Magazine: The Origins of Play-Doh','https://www.smithsonianmag.com/innovation/accidental-invention-play-doh-180973527/','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000254','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which office product uses the repositionable adhesive associated with Spencer Silver and Art Fry?','["Staples", "Paper clips", "Correction fluid", "Post-it Notes"]','"Post-it Notes"','Silver developed a weak pressure-sensitive adhesive; Fry helped turn it into repositionable notes.','3M: Post-it Brand History','https://www.post-it.com/3M/en_US/post-it/ideas/articles/history-of-post-it-notes/','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000255','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which cooking appliance was developed after Percy Spencer investigated the heating effects of a magnetron?','["Electric toaster", "Induction hob", "Microwave oven", "Pressure cooker"]','"Microwave oven"','Spencer recognized that microwave energy from radar equipment could heat food.','Encyclopaedia Britannica: Microwave oven','https://www.britannica.com/technology/microwave-oven','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000256','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which spring toy was developed by naval engineer Richard James?','["Spinning top", "Slinky", "Yo-yo", "Rubik''s Cube"]','"Slinky"','Richard James observed the motion of a spring and developed the Slinky toy.','The Strong National Museum of Play: Slinky','https://www.museumofplay.org/toys/slinky/','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000257','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which adhesive''s development is associated with Harry Coover and cyanoacrylates?','["Superglue", "Starch paste", "Hot-melt glue", "Rubber cement"]','"Superglue"','Harry Coover helped develop the practical use of cyanoacrylate adhesives, sold as superglue.','National Inventors Hall of Fame: Harry Coover','https://www.invent.org/inductees/harry-coover','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000258','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which inventor patented an early safety pin in 1849?','["Thomas Edison", "Alexander Graham Bell", "Samuel Morse", "Walter Hunt"]','"Walter Hunt"','Walter Hunt''s 1849 patent covered a safety pin with a clasp protecting the point.','National Inventors Hall of Fame: Walter Hunt','https://www.invent.org/inductees/walter-hunt','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000259','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which inventor''s surname is also used as a word for a ballpoint pen in British English?','["Kelvin", "Ampere", "Biro", "Diesel"]','"Biro"','Laszlo Biro developed a practical ballpoint pen, and biro became a common name for it.','Encyclopaedia Britannica: Laszlo Biro','https://www.britannica.com/biography/Laszlo-Biro','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000260','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','How many dot positions are in a standard Braille cell, excluding extended eight-dot Braille?','["Twelve", "Six", "Four", "Five"]','"Six"','A standard Braille cell contains two columns of three dot positions.','Encyclopaedia Britannica: Braille','https://www.britannica.com/topic/Braille','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000261','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What writing system represents letters using short and long signals popularly called dots and dashes?','["Morse code", "Braille", "Semaphore flags", "Cuneiform"]','"Morse code"','Morse code uses different sequences of short and long signals.','Encyclopaedia Britannica: Morse Code','https://www.britannica.com/topic/Morse-Code','weird',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8004-000000000262','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Who created the constructed international language Esperanto?','["J. R. R. Tolkien", "Noam Chomsky", "Jacob Grimm", "L. L. Zamenhof"]','"L. L. Zamenhof"','Zamenhof introduced Esperanto in 1887 as an auxiliary international language.','Encyclopaedia Britannica: Esperanto','https://www.britannica.com/topic/Esperanto','weird',60),
