('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000237','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is a living place that provides the conditions an organism needs called?','["Habitat", "Genome", "Taxon", "Trophic level"]','"Habitat"','A habitat is the environment where an organism lives.','Wikipedia: Habitat','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Habitat','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000238','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the variety of living organisms in an area called?','["Salinity", "Biodiversity", "Topography", "Humidity"]','"Biodiversity"','Biodiversity encompasses variation among living organisms and ecosystems.','Wikipedia: Biodiversity','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biodiversity','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000239','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What describes an animal that eats both plants and other animals?','["Herbivore", "Carnivore", "Omnivore", "Autotroph"]','"Omnivore"','Omnivores consume both plant and animal material.','Wikipedia: Omnivore','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omnivore','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000240','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What describes an animal that eats mainly plant material?','["Carnivore", "Parasite", "Scavenger", "Herbivore"]','"Herbivore"','Herbivores primarily feed on plants or algae.','Wikipedia: Herbivore','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herbivore','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000241','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What do earthworms typically use to grip the soil while moving?','["Setae", "Feathers", "Scales", "Suction disks"]','"Setae"','Earthworms have small bristles called setae that help anchor segments during movement.','Wikipedia: Earthworm','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earthworm','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000242','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is a diagram of interconnected feeding relationships called?','["Fault map", "Food web", "Climate graph", "Family pedigree"]','"Food web"','A food web represents multiple interacting food chains.','Wikipedia: Food web','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Food_web','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000243','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is an organism''s feeding position within a food chain called?','["Latitude", "Taxonomic rank", "Trophic level", "Growth ring"]','"Trophic level"','Trophic levels describe positions in the transfer of food energy.','Wikipedia: Trophic level','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trophic_level','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000244','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the first trophic level in most food chains composed of?','["Apex predators", "Secondary consumers", "Parasites", "Primary producers"]','"Primary producers"','Primary producers create organic material from inorganic resources.','Wikipedia: Primary producer','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primary_producer','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000245','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is a predator at the top of a food web with no routine natural predators called?','["Apex predator", "Primary producer", "Detritivore", "Pollinator"]','"Apex predator"','Apex predators occupy the highest predatory position in their food webs.','Wikipedia: Apex predator','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apex_predator','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000246','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What relationship benefits both participating species?','["Parasitism", "Mutualism", "Competition", "Predation"]','"Mutualism"','Mutualism is an interaction in which both partners benefit.','Wikipedia: Mutualism (biology)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mutualism_(biology)','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000247','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What relationship benefits one organism while harming its host?','["Mutualism", "Commensalism", "Parasitism", "Neutralism"]','"Parasitism"','Parasites gain resources from hosts at the hosts'' expense.','Wikipedia: Parasitism','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parasitism','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000248','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What relationship benefits one species while leaving the other largely unaffected?','["Predation", "Parasitism", "Competition", "Commensalism"]','"Commensalism"','Commensalism benefits one partner without a substantial effect on the other.','Wikipedia: Commensalism','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commensalism','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000249','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What process changes an ecological community over time after a disturbance?','["Ecological succession", "Plate tectonics", "Mitosis", "Condensation"]','"Ecological succession"','Succession is a change in community composition and structure over time.','Wikipedia: Ecological succession','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecological_succession','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000250','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is succession beginning on a surface with no developed soil called?','["Secondary succession", "Primary succession", "Seasonal migration", "Hibernation"]','"Primary succession"','Primary succession occurs where established soil and communities are absent.','Wikipedia: Primary succession','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primary_succession','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000251','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is succession following disturbance where soil remains called?','["Primary succession", "Continental drift", "Secondary succession", "Fertilization"]','"Secondary succession"','Secondary succession follows a disturbance in a place that retains soil.','Wikipedia: Secondary succession','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secondary_succession','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000252','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is camouflage that resembles surrounding colors and patterns called?','["Bioluminescence", "Metamorphosis", "Migration", "Crypsis"]','"Crypsis"','Crypsis reduces detection through blending with the surroundings.','Wikipedia: Crypsis','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crypsis','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000253','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is bright coloration advertising toxicity or another defense called?','["Aposematism", "Phototropism", "Hibernation", "Echolocation"]','"Aposematism"','Aposematic signals warn potential predators of defenses.','Wikipedia: Aposematism','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aposematism','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000254','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is a harmless species resembling a defended species called?','["Primary succession", "Batesian mimicry", "Mutual predation", "Photoperiodism"]','"Batesian mimicry"','Batesian mimics gain protection by resembling harmful or unpalatable models.','Wikipedia: Batesian mimicry','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Batesian_mimicry','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000255','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is resemblance between multiple genuinely defended species called?','["Batesian mimicry", "Commensalism", "Mullerian mimicry", "Geotropism"]','"Mullerian mimicry"','Mullerian mimicry involves defended species sharing warning signals.','Wikipedia: Mullerian mimicry','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mullerian_mimicry','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000256','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is seasonal movement between breeding and nonbreeding areas called?','["Molting", "Germination", "Abscission", "Migration"]','"Migration"','Migration is regular movement between areas, often tied to seasons.','Wikipedia: Animal migration','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animal_migration','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000257','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the shedding and replacement of an animal''s outer covering called?','["Molting", "Pollination", "Fermentation", "Germination"]','"Molting"','Molting replaces coverings such as feathers or arthropod exoskeletons.','Wikipedia: Moulting','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moulting','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000258','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What term describes animals active mainly during daylight?','["Nocturnal", "Diurnal", "Crepuscular", "Pelagic"]','"Diurnal"','Diurnal animals are chiefly active during the day.','Wikipedia: Diurnality','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diurnality','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000259','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What term describes animals active mainly at night?','["Diurnal", "Sessile", "Nocturnal", "Arboreal"]','"Nocturnal"','Nocturnal animals are chiefly active at night.','Wikipedia: Nocturnality','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nocturnality','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000260','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What term describes animals that live chiefly in trees?','["Aquatic", "Fossorial", "Pelagic", "Arboreal"]','"Arboreal"','Arboreal animals live and move primarily in trees.','Wikipedia: Arboreal locomotion','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arboreal_locomotion','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000261','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What term describes animals adapted to digging and living underground?','["Fossorial", "Arboreal", "Pelagic", "Aerial"]','"Fossorial"','Fossorial animals are adapted for burrowing.','Wikipedia: Fossorial','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fossorial','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000262','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What term describes an organism attached to a surface and unable to move freely?','["Migratory", "Sessile", "Nocturnal", "Cursorial"]','"Sessile"','Sessile organisms are fixed in place rather than freely mobile.','Wikipedia: Sessility (motility)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sessility_(motility)','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000263','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What term describes the open-water region away from the bottom of a body of water?','["Benthic zone", "Root zone", "Pelagic zone", "Intertidal zone"]','"Pelagic zone"','The pelagic zone is the water column away from the bottom.','Wikipedia: Pelagic zone','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pelagic_zone','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000264','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What term describes the ecological region at the bottom of a body of water?','["Pelagic zone", "Canopy", "Troposphere", "Benthic zone"]','"Benthic zone"','The benthic zone includes the bottom substrate and its organisms.','Wikipedia: Benthic zone','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benthic_zone','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000265','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What coastal zone lies between high-tide and low-tide levels?','["Intertidal zone", "Abyssal zone", "Pelagic zone", "Subalpine zone"]','"Intertidal zone"','The intertidal zone is exposed and submerged as tides change.','Wikipedia: Intertidal zone','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intertidal_zone','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000266','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is a body of coastal water where freshwater mixes with seawater called?','["Glacier", "Estuary", "Geyser", "Dune"]','"Estuary"','Estuaries connect river freshwater with coastal seawater.','Wikipedia: Estuary','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Estuary','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000267','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Which wetland vegetation is adapted to salty tropical coasts and often has exposed support roots?','["Cacti", "Spruces", "Mangroves", "Alpine mosses"]','"Mangroves"','Mangroves are salt-tolerant trees and shrubs of tropical coastal wetlands.','Wikipedia: Mangrove','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mangrove','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000268','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is a coral''s small individual animal unit called?','["Spore", "Seed", "Hypha", "Polyp"]','"Polyp"','Corals are animals formed from polyps, often living in colonies.','Wikipedia: Coral','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000269','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What do reef-building corals produce as the main material of their hard skeletons?','["Calcium carbonate", "Silicon dioxide", "Cellulose", "Keratin"]','"Calcium carbonate"','Hard coral skeletons are mainly calcium carbonate.','Wikipedia: Coral reef','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral_reef','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000270','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is coral bleaching most directly associated with losing?','["All ocean salt", "Symbiotic algae", "All skeletal calcium", "Their entire genome"]','"Symbiotic algae"','Stressed corals may expel symbiotic algae and lose much of their color.','Wikipedia: Coral bleaching','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coral_bleaching','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000271','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What collective term describes drifting organisms carried by water currents?','["Nekton", "Bark", "Plankton", "Humus"]','"Plankton"','Plankton drift with currents rather than swimming strongly against them.','Wikipedia: Plankton','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plankton','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000272','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What collective term describes aquatic animals that can swim against currents?','["Plankton", "Lichen", "Detritus", "Nekton"]','"Nekton"','Nekton actively swim sufficiently to move independently of currents.','Wikipedia: Nekton','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nekton','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000273','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is partly decomposed organic material in soil commonly called?','["Humus", "Quartz", "Basalt", "Saline"]','"Humus"','Humus is decomposed organic material that contributes to soil properties.','Wikipedia: Humus','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Humus','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000274','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the gradual wearing away and transport of soil by water or wind called?','["Pollination", "Erosion", "Photosynthesis", "Hibernation"]','"Erosion"','Erosion removes and transports soil or rock material.','Wikipedia: Erosion','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Erosion','nature',60),
('41000000-0000-4000-8000-050000000275','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the total living organic mass in a given area or trophic level called?','["Salinity", "Pressure", "Biomass", "Latitude"]','"Biomass"','Biomass measures the mass of living biological material.','Wikipedia: Biomass (ecology)','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biomass_(ecology)','nature',60)
INSERT INTO questions (id,type,prompt,choices,correct_answer,explanation,source_title,source_url,topic_id,answer_window_seconds)
VALUES
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A hidden token is in one of 16 numbered lockers. Each question may ask membership in any chosen subset and receives a truthful yes/no answer. Minimum worst-case questions?','["5", "7", "3", "4"]','"4"','4 bits distinguish 2^4=16 possibilities; successive halves attain this bound.','Original binary information exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 9 coins exactly one is heavier; all genuine coins weigh equally. With a balance scale, minimum worst-case weighings?','["5", "1", "2", "3"]','"2"','Each weighing has three outcomes. Split into three equal groups repeatedly: 2 weighings distinguish 3^2=9 coins.','Original balance ternary search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A drawer has unlimited socks in 4 colors. Picking blindly, how many socks guarantee a matching color pair?','["4", "5", "6", "8"]','"5"','4 picks could all have different colors; pick 5 repeats a color.','Original pigeonhole socks exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000004','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag has unlimited beads in 4 colors. How many blind picks guarantee three of one color?','["9", "10", "12", "8"]','"9"','At most two per color gives 8 without a triple; the next forces three.','Original pigeonhole triples exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000005','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A club of 7 members plays one game between every unordered pair. How many games?','["22", "24", "20", "21"]','"21"','Choose two members: (7)(6)/2=21.','Original pair counting exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000006','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fence has 6 posts in a straight row, spaced 2 meters apart. Distance from first post to last, in meters?','["13", "9", "10", "11"]','"10"','There are 5 gaps, each 2 meters: 10.','Original inclusive intervals exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000007','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Four cards show 2, 6, Blue and Green. Every card has an integer on one side and exactly one of those colors on the other. To test “if the integer is divisible by 2, the color is Blue,” which shown cards must be turned?','["Neither numbered card nor Green", "2 and 6 and Green", "Blue only", "All four"]','"2 and 6 and Green"','Turn every visible multiple of 2 to check Blue, and Green to exclude a multiple on its reverse. Blue and visible nonmultiples cannot falsify the rule.','Original conditional counterexample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000008','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','All poets in a club are readers. Some club members are poets. What must be true?','["Some club members are readers", "All readers are poets", "No readers exist", "Every member is a poet"]','"Some club members are readers"','At least one poet exists, and every poet is a reader.','Original syllogism exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000009','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','If a file is approved, it is signed. An unsigned file is found. What follows?','["The file is approved", "The file is deleted", "The signature is invisible", "The file is not approved"]','"The file is not approved"','Approved implies signed; not signed implies not approved.','Original implication chains exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000010','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','If an alarm sounds, its light is on. The light is on. What follows about the alarm?','["It must be silent", "The rule is necessarily broken", "It could be sounding or silent", "It must be sounding"]','"It could be sounding or silent"','The rule permits light without sound; affirming the consequence is invalid.','Original conditional diagnosis exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the logical negation of “every box is red”?','["At least one box is red", "At least one box is not red", "Every box is blue", "No box is red"]','"At least one box is not red"','Not all means at least one counterexample.','Original logical negation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A secret code is an integer from 1 through 4. Exactly one statement is true: A says it is even; B says it is divisible by 3. How many possible codes satisfy this?','["3", "4", "6", "2"]','"3"','Count integers in exactly one of the two sets. The valid codes are 2, 3, 4. Equivalently floor(4/2)+floor(4/3)−2floor(4/6)=3.','Original truth assignment exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Exactly four runners finish without ties. Mina finishes before Oren; Paz finishes after Oren; Rui finishes after Paz. Who finishes first?','["Oren", "Paz", "Rui", "Mina"]','"Mina"','The constraints force the order Mina → Oren → Paz → Rui; the first runner is Mina.','Original ordering constraints exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A knockout contest begins with 20 players. Every match eliminates exactly one player; no byes eliminate anyone. Matches needed for one champion?','["22", "18", "19", "20"]','"19"','Exactly 19 players must be eliminated; each match eliminates one.','Original tournament elimination exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000015','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','On a grid, go 4 steps east and 2 steps north in any order, with no other moves. How many distinct step sequences?','["14", "15", "16", "18"]','"15"','Choose the two north-step positions among 6: C(6,2)=15.','Original grid paths exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000016','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','4 different keys must be tried on one lock. Exactly one works; unsuccessful keys are never retried. Maximum trials needed to open it?','["4", "5", "7", "3"]','"4"','The working key can be last among all 4; identifying the last key is not opening the lock.','Original cycle permutation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000017','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A menu has 4 optional toppings; each can be included once or omitted independently. How many topping selections including none?','["17", "19", "15", "16"]','"16"','Each of 4 toppings has two states, so 2^4=16.','Original subset counts exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000018','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose a president and a different secretary from 4 candidates. How many assignments?','["15", "11", "12", "13"]','"12"','4 president choices times 3 secretary choices=12.','Original ordered arrangements exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000019','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 20 students, 9 study art, 7 study music, and 4 study both. How many study at least one?','["11", "12", "13", "15"]','"12"','Add the groups and subtract double-counted overlap: 9+7-4=12.','Original inclusion exclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000020','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A 12-hour clock shows 2:00. What hour number appears 17 hours later?','["7", "8", "10", "6"]','"7"','Add 17 hours and reduce modulo 12, labeling remainder zero as 12.','Original modular clock exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000021','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A board has 11 tiles, initially 2 black and the rest white. A move flips exactly two different tiles. After any number of moves, what is the largest reachable number of black tiles?','["11", "13", "9", "10"]','"10"','Each move preserves black-count parity. The largest count at most 11 with parity 0 is 10; flipping pairs of white tiles attains it.','Original parity invariant exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000022','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','An integer lies from 1 through 15. Each trial guesses one value and reports correct, too low, or too high. Minimum worst-case trials to identify it by a correct guess?','["7", "3", "4", "5"]','"4"','With 4 trials binary search covers at most 2^4-1 values, achieved by guessing midpoints.','Original bounded search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000023','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Two identical test sensors break above an unknown threshold in a 3-level building, and survive at or below it. The threshold is the highest safe floor, from 0 (all floors unsafe) through 3 (all floors safe). Broken sensors cannot be reused. Minimum worst-case drops to find the threshold?','["1", "2", "3", "5"]','"2"','2 drops cover a descending sum from 2 through 1, totaling 2×3/2=3 levels using decreasing gaps; 1 drops cover fewer.','Original two egg threshold exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000024','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the smallest positive integer greater than 24 that is divisible by both 2 and 3?','["30", "31", "33", "29"]','"30"','Common multiples are multiples of 6; the next after 24 is 30.','Original remainders exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000025','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A prize is in exactly one of boxes numbered 1 through 4. Exactly one speaker tells the truth. A says “the box number is even”; B says “the box number is odd”; C says “the box number is divisible by 3.” How many box numbers remain possible?','["4", "6", "2", "3"]','"3"','A and B are complementary, so exactly one already tells the truth. C must be false. Excluding 1 multiples of 3 leaves 4−1=3 possibilities.','Original exactly one speaker exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000001','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 2 times. Probability that every flip is heads?','["1/8", "5/8", "0", "1/4"]','"1/4"','Independence multiplies 2 factors of 1/2: 1/4.','Original all heads exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000002','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is flipped independently 3 times. Probability of at least one head?','["15/16", "0", "7/8", "7/16"]','"7/8"','Take the complement of all tails: 1−(1/2)^3.','Original at least one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000003','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 4 times. Probability of exactly one head?','["0", "1/4", "1/8", "5/8"]','"1/4"','Choose one head position in 4 ways, each sequence has probability 1/16.','Original exactly one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000004','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is tossed independently 5 times. Probability of exactly two heads?','["5/16", "5/32", "21/32", "0"]','"5/16"','C(5,2)=10 qualifying sequences out of 32.','Original binomial two successes exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000005','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag holds 4 red and 3 blue tokens. Every token is equally likely to be drawn once. Probability of red?','["2/7", "11/14", "0", "4/7"]','"4/7"','4 favorable tokens among 7 total.','Original one uniform draw exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000006','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two tokens uniformly without replacement from 4 red and 5 blue. Probability both are red?','["7/12", "0", "1/6", "1/12"]','"1/6"','Multiply 4/9 by 3/8.','Original without replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000007','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw twice uniformly with replacement from 4 red and 6 blue tokens. Probability both red?','["0", "4/25", "2/25", "29/50"]','"4/25"','Replacement makes independent draws with red probability 4/10; square it.','Original with replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000008','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two uniformly without replacement from 5 red and 7 blue tokens. Probability of one of each color?','["35/66", "35/132", "101/132", "0"]','"35/66"','Add red-blue and blue-red: 2×5×7/(12×11).','Original one of each color exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000009','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 24. Probability it is divisible by 3?','["1/6", "2/3", "0", "1/3"]','"1/3"','8 multiples of 3 among 24 integers.','Original uniform integer divisor exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000010','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 24. Probability it is divisible by 2 or 3?','["5/6", "0", "2/3", "1/3"]','"2/3"','12 multiples of 2 plus 8 multiples of 3 minus 4 multiples of 6 gives 16/24.','Original uniform integer union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000011','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Randomly order 4 distinct books, all orders equally likely. Probability a specified book is first?','["0", "1/4", "1/8", "5/8"]','"1/4"','Each book has equal probability of any of the 4 positions.','Original random permutation first exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000012','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Arrange 6 distinct people uniformly in a straight line. Probability two specified people stand next to each other?','["1/3", "1/6", "2/3", "0"]','"1/3"','Treat the pair as a block with two internal orders: 2×(5)!/(6)!=2/6.','Original permutation adjacency exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000013','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A committee of 2 is selected uniformly from 7 distinct people. Probability a specified person is selected?','["1/7", "9/14", "0", "2/7"]','"2/7"','The person belongs to 6 of C(7,2) committees, yielding 2/7.','Original subset inclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000014','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A lottery chooses one winning ticket uniformly from 80 tickets. You own 4 distinct tickets. Probability you win?','["21/40", "0", "1/20", "1/40"]','"1/20"','4 of 80 equally likely tickets are yours.','Original matching lottery exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000015','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/3 and 1/5. Probability both occur?','["0", "1/15", "1/30", "8/15"]','"1/15"','For independent events multiply 1/3 by 1/5.','Original independent intersections exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000016','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/4 and 1/6. Probability at least one occurs?','["3/8", "3/16", "11/16", "0"]','"3/8"','P(A or B)=P(A)+P(B)−P(A)P(B)=3/8.','Original independent union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000017','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A number is chosen uniformly from 1 through 16. Given it is even, probability it is also divisible by 4?','["1/4", "3/4", "0", "1/2"]','"1/2"','There are 8 even values and 4 multiples of 4 in the restricted sample.','Original conditional restricted sample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000018','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A group has 4 engineers and 3 artists. Choose 3 people uniformly without replacement. Probability all are engineers?','["39/70", "0", "4/35", "2/35"]','"4/35"','C(4,3)/C(7,3)=4/35.','Original hypergeometric committee exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000019','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent attempts succeed with probability 1/3 each. Probability the first success is on attempt 2?','["0", "2/9", "1/9", "11/18"]','"2/9"','1 failures followed by success: (2/3)^1×(1/3).','Original geometric first success exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000020','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice, each labeled 1 through 4. Probability their values match?','["1/4", "1/8", "5/8", "0"]','"1/4"','4 matching ordered pairs among 16 equally likely pairs.','Original uniform dice equality exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000021','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 5. Probability the first value is strictly greater than the second?','["1/5", "7/10", "0", "2/5"]','"2/5"','Excluding 5 ties, symmetry halves the remaining 20 ordered pairs: 4/(10).','Original uniform dice ordering exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000022','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 6. Probability at least one shows 6?','["47/72", "0", "11/36", "11/72"]','"11/36"','Complement of neither showing the top face: 1−(5/6)².','Original dice maximum exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000023','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A code has 2 digits chosen independently uniformly from 0–9; leading zeros allowed. Probability all digits are nonzero? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["90.50%", "81.00%", "40.50%", "27.00%"]','"81.00%"','Each digit is nonzero with probability 9/10; multiply 2 factors. This is approximately 81.00%.','Original random digit code exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000024','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Pick one of two bags by fair coin. Bag A has 4 red and 1 blue token; bag B has 1 red and 4 blue. Draw uniformly. Given red, probability bag A was chosen?','["4/5", "2/5", "9/10", "0"]','"4/5"','Bayes: (1/2×4/5)/(1/2×4/5+1/2×1/5)=4/5.','Original Bayes selected bag exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000025','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','2 people have independent birthdays uniform over a fictional 40-day year. Probability all birthdays differ? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["48.75%", "32.50%", "98.75%", "97.50%"]','"97.50%"','Successive birthday choices: 40/40 × 39/40 × ... × 39/40. This is approximately 97.50%.','Original sampling distinct birthdays exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000001','ESTIMATE','For planning, a vehicle covers 68 km at a constant 55 km/h with no stops. Estimate travel time in minutes. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'74','Time = distance/speed × 60 = 68/55×60 minutes. Rounded result: 74.','Original travel time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
