('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000025','ESTIMATE','A solution contains 10.0 grams of dissolved salt in a final volume of 4 liters. Estimate concentration in grams per liter. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'2.5','Concentration = dissolved mass/final volume = 10.0/4 g/L. Rounded result: 2.5.','Original dilution concentration exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000026','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A hidden token is in one of 32 numbered lockers. Each question may ask membership in any chosen subset and receives a truthful yes/no answer. Minimum worst-case questions?','["8", "4", "5", "6"]','"5"','5 bits distinguish 2^5=32 possibilities; successive halves attain this bound.','Original binary information exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000027','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 27 coins exactly one is heavier; all genuine coins weigh equally. With a balance scale, minimum worst-case weighings?','["2", "3", "4", "6"]','"3"','Each weighing has three outcomes. Split into three equal groups repeatedly: 3 weighings distinguish 3^3=27 coins.','Original balance ternary search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000028','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A drawer has unlimited socks in 5 colors. Picking blindly, how many socks guarantee a matching color pair?','["6", "7", "9", "5"]','"6"','5 picks could all have different colors; pick 6 repeats a color.','Original pigeonhole socks exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000029','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag has unlimited beads in 5 colors. How many blind picks guarantee three of one color?','["12", "14", "10", "11"]','"11"','At most two per color gives 10 without a triple; the next forces three.','Original pigeonhole triples exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000030','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A club of 8 members plays one game between every unordered pair. How many games?','["31", "27", "28", "29"]','"28"','Choose two members: (8)(7)/2=28.','Original pair counting exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000031','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fence has 7 posts in a straight row, spaced 3 meters apart. Distance from first post to last, in meters?','["17", "18", "19", "21"]','"18"','There are 6 gaps, each 3 meters: 18.','Original inclusive intervals exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000032','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Four cards show 3, 8, Blue and Green. Every card has an integer on one side and exactly one of those colors on the other. To test “if the integer is divisible by 3, the color is Blue,” which shown cards must be turned?','["3 and Green", "Blue only", "All four", "Neither numbered card nor Green"]','"3 and Green"','Turn every visible multiple of 3 to check Blue, and Green to exclude a multiple on its reverse. Blue and visible nonmultiples cannot falsify the rule.','Original conditional counterexample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000033','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','No wooden object is magnetic. Every token in a tray is wooden. What follows?','["Every magnetic object is a token", "Some tokens are magnetic", "No tokens exist", "No tray token is magnetic"]','"No tray token is magnetic"','Every tray token belongs to the wooden class, which is disjoint from magnetic objects.','Original syllogism exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000034','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','If a parcel is express, it gets a tracking code. If it has a tracking code, it appears in the registry. A parcel is absent from the registry. What follows?','["The parcel was lost", "The parcel never existed", "The parcel is not express", "The parcel is express"]','"The parcel is not express"','Absence rules out a code; lack of a code rules out express status.','Original implication chains exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000035','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','If a code is valid, the door opens. The code is valid. What follows?','["Nothing follows about the door", "The door opens", "The door stays shut", "The code is invalid"]','"The door opens"','Modus ponens applies the stated implication to its true antecedent.','Original conditional diagnosis exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000036','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the logical negation of “some cards are marked”?','["No cards are marked", "Every card is marked", "Some cards are unmarked", "Exactly one card is marked"]','"No cards are marked"','Negating an existential statement gives a universal absence.','Original logical negation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000037','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A secret code is an integer from 1 through 5. Exactly one statement is true: A says it is even; B says it is divisible by 3. How many possible codes satisfy this?','["4", "6", "2", "3"]','"3"','Count integers in exactly one of the two sets. The valid codes are 2, 3, 4. Equivalently floor(5/2)+floor(5/3)−2floor(5/6)=3.','Original truth assignment exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000038','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Exactly four runners finish without ties. Mina finishes before Oren; Rui finishes after Oren; Paz finishes after Rui. Who finishes second?','["Rui", "Paz", "Oren", "Mina"]','"Oren"','The constraints force the order Mina → Oren → Rui → Paz; the second runner is Oren.','Original ordering constraints exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000039','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A knockout contest begins with 25 players. Every match eliminates exactly one player; no byes eliminate anyone. Matches needed for one champion?','["23", "24", "25", "27"]','"24"','Exactly 24 players must be eliminated; each match eliminates one.','Original tournament elimination exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000040','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','On a grid, go 5 steps east and 2 steps north in any order, with no other moves. How many distinct step sequences?','["21", "22", "24", "20"]','"21"','Choose the two north-step positions among 7: C(7,2)=21.','Original grid paths exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000041','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','5 different keys must be tried on one lock. Exactly one works; unsuccessful keys are never retried. Maximum trials needed to open it?','["6", "8", "4", "5"]','"5"','The working key can be last among all 5; identifying the last key is not opening the lock.','Original cycle permutation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000042','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A menu has 5 optional toppings; each can be included once or omitted independently. How many topping selections including none?','["35", "31", "32", "33"]','"32"','Each of 5 toppings has two states, so 2^5=32.','Original subset counts exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000043','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose a president and a different secretary from 5 candidates. How many assignments?','["19", "20", "21", "23"]','"20"','5 president choices times 4 secretary choices=20.','Original ordered arrangements exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000044','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 25 students, 10 study art, 8 study music, and 5 study both. How many study at least one?','["13", "14", "16", "12"]','"13"','Add the groups and subtract double-counted overlap: 10+8-5=13.','Original inclusion exclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000045','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A 12-hour clock shows 3:00. What hour number appears 18 hours later?','["10", "12", "8", "9"]','"9"','Add 18 hours and reduce modulo 12, labeling remainder zero as 12.','Original modular clock exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000046','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A board has 12 tiles, initially 3 black and the rest white. A move flips exactly two different tiles. After any number of moves, what is the largest reachable number of black tiles?','["14", "10", "11", "12"]','"11"','Each move preserves black-count parity. The largest count at most 12 with parity 1 is 11; flipping pairs of white tiles attains it.','Original parity invariant exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000047','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','An integer lies from 1 through 31. Each trial guesses one value and reports correct, too low, or too high. Minimum worst-case trials to identify it by a correct guess?','["4", "5", "6", "8"]','"5"','With 5 trials binary search covers at most 2^5-1 values, achieved by guessing midpoints.','Original bounded search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000048','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Two identical test sensors break above an unknown threshold in a 6-level building, and survive at or below it. The threshold is the highest safe floor, from 0 (all floors unsafe) through 6 (all floors safe). Broken sensors cannot be reused. Minimum worst-case drops to find the threshold?','["3", "4", "6", "2"]','"3"','3 drops cover a descending sum from 3 through 1, totaling 3×4/2=6 levels using decreasing gaps; 2 drops cover fewer.','Original two egg threshold exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000049','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the smallest positive integer greater than 30 that is divisible by both 2 and 3?','["37", "39", "35", "36"]','"36"','Common multiples are multiples of 6; the next after 30 is 36.','Original remainders exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000050','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A prize is in exactly one of boxes numbered 1 through 5. Exactly one speaker tells the truth. A says “the box number is even”; B says “the box number is odd”; C says “the box number is divisible by 3.” How many box numbers remain possible?','["7", "3", "4", "5"]','"4"','A and B are complementary, so exactly one already tells the truth. C must be false. Excluding 1 multiples of 3 leaves 5−1=4 possibilities.','Original exactly one speaker exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000026','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 3 times. Probability that every flip is heads?','["9/16", "0", "1/8", "1/16"]','"1/8"','Independence multiplies 3 factors of 1/2: 1/8.','Original all heads exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000027','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is flipped independently 4 times. Probability of at least one head?','["0", "15/16", "15/32", "31/32"]','"15/16"','Take the complement of all tails: 1−(1/2)^4.','Original at least one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000028','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 5 times. Probability of exactly one head?','["5/32", "5/64", "37/64", "0"]','"5/32"','Choose one head position in 5 ways, each sequence has probability 1/32.','Original exactly one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000029','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is tossed independently 6 times. Probability of exactly two heads?','["15/128", "79/128", "0", "15/64"]','"15/64"','C(6,2)=15 qualifying sequences out of 64.','Original binomial two successes exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000030','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag holds 5 red and 4 blue tokens. Every token is equally likely to be drawn once. Probability of red?','["7/9", "0", "5/9", "5/18"]','"5/9"','5 favorable tokens among 9 total.','Original one uniform draw exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000031','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two tokens uniformly without replacement from 5 red and 6 blue. Probability both are red?','["0", "2/11", "1/11", "13/22"]','"2/11"','Multiply 5/11 by 4/10.','Original without replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000032','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw twice uniformly with replacement from 5 red and 7 blue tokens. Probability both red?','["25/144", "25/288", "169/288", "0"]','"25/144"','Replacement makes independent draws with red probability 5/12; square it.','Original with replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000033','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two uniformly without replacement from 6 red and 8 blue tokens. Probability of one of each color?','["24/91", "139/182", "0", "48/91"]','"48/91"','Add red-blue and blue-red: 2×6×8/(14×13).','Original one of each color exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000034','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 30. Probability it is divisible by 3?','["2/3", "0", "1/3", "1/6"]','"1/3"','10 multiples of 3 among 30 integers.','Original uniform integer divisor exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000035','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 30. Probability it is divisible by 2 or 3?','["0", "2/3", "1/3", "5/6"]','"2/3"','15 multiples of 2 plus 10 multiples of 3 minus 5 multiples of 6 gives 20/30.','Original uniform integer union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000036','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Randomly order 5 distinct books, all orders equally likely. Probability a specified book is first?','["1/5", "1/10", "3/5", "0"]','"1/5"','Each book has equal probability of any of the 5 positions.','Original random permutation first exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000037','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Arrange 7 distinct people uniformly in a straight line. Probability two specified people stand next to each other?','["1/7", "9/14", "0", "2/7"]','"2/7"','Treat the pair as a block with two internal orders: 2×(6)!/(7)!=2/7.','Original permutation adjacency exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000038','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A committee of 2 is selected uniformly from 8 distinct people. Probability a specified person is selected?','["5/8", "0", "1/4", "1/8"]','"1/4"','The person belongs to 7 of C(8,2) committees, yielding 2/8.','Original subset inclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000039','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A lottery chooses one winning ticket uniformly from 100 tickets. You own 5 distinct tickets. Probability you win?','["0", "1/20", "1/40", "21/40"]','"1/20"','5 of 100 equally likely tickets are yours.','Original matching lottery exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000040','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/4 and 1/6. Probability both occur?','["1/24", "1/48", "25/48", "0"]','"1/24"','For independent events multiply 1/4 by 1/6.','Original independent intersections exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000041','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/5 and 1/7. Probability at least one occurs?','["11/70", "23/35", "0", "11/35"]','"11/35"','P(A or B)=P(A)+P(B)−P(A)P(B)=11/35.','Original independent union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000042','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A number is chosen uniformly from 1 through 20. Given it is even, probability it is also divisible by 4?','["3/4", "0", "1/2", "1/4"]','"1/2"','There are 10 even values and 5 multiples of 4 in the restricted sample.','Original conditional restricted sample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000043','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A group has 5 engineers and 3 artists. Choose 3 people uniformly without replacement. Probability all are engineers?','["0", "5/28", "5/56", "33/56"]','"5/28"','C(5,3)/C(8,3)=10/56.','Original hypergeometric committee exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000044','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent attempts succeed with probability 1/3 each. Probability the first success is on attempt 3?','["4/27", "2/27", "31/54", "0"]','"4/27"','2 failures followed by success: (2/3)^2×(1/3).','Original geometric first success exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000045','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice, each labeled 1 through 5. Probability their values match?','["1/10", "3/5", "0", "1/5"]','"1/5"','5 matching ordered pairs among 25 equally likely pairs.','Original uniform dice equality exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000046','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 6. Probability the first value is strictly greater than the second?','["17/24", "0", "5/12", "5/24"]','"5/12"','Excluding 6 ties, symmetry halves the remaining 30 ordered pairs: 5/(12).','Original uniform dice ordering exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000047','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 7. Probability at least one shows 7?','["0", "13/49", "13/98", "31/49"]','"13/49"','Complement of neither showing the top face: 1−(6/7)².','Original dice maximum exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000048','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A code has 3 digits chosen independently uniformly from 0–9; leading zeros allowed. Probability all digits are nonzero? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["72.90%", "36.45%", "24.30%", "86.45%"]','"72.90%"','Each digit is nonzero with probability 9/10; multiply 3 factors. This is approximately 72.90%.','Original random digit code exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000049','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Pick one of two bags by fair coin. Bag A has 5 red and 1 blue token; bag B has 1 red and 5 blue. Draw uniformly. Given red, probability bag A was chosen?','["5/12", "11/12", "0", "5/6"]','"5/6"','Bayes: (1/2×5/6)/(1/2×5/6+1/2×1/6)=5/6.','Original Bayes selected bag exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000050','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','3 people have independent birthdays uniform over a fictional 50-day year. Probability all birthdays differ? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["31.36%", "97.04%", "94.08%", "47.04%"]','"94.08%"','Successive birthday choices: 50/50 × 49/50 × ... × 48/50. This is approximately 94.08%.','Original sampling distinct birthdays exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000026','ESTIMATE','For planning, a vehicle covers 85 km at a constant 65 km/h with no stops. Estimate travel time in minutes. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'78','Time = distance/speed × 60 = 85/65×60 minutes. Rounded result: 78.','Original travel time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000027','ESTIMATE','A car averages 6 liters per 100 km over a 155-km route. Estimate fuel needed in liters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'9.3','Fuel = 155×6/100 liters. Rounded result: 9.3.','Original fuel consumption exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000028','ESTIMATE','A rectangular floor is 5.5 m long and 3.25 m wide. Assuming no waste, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'17.9','Area = length×width = 5.5×3.25 m². Rounded result: 17.9.','Original area coverage exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000029','ESTIMATE','A wall area is 65 square meters. One liter covers 7 square meters per coat. For two coats, estimate liters of paint, ignoring waste. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'18.6','Paint volume = 65×2/7 liters. Rounded result: 18.6.','Original paint demand exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000030','ESTIMATE','A rectangular tank has internal dimensions 50 cm by 30 cm by 35 cm. Estimate capacity in liters; use 1 liter = 1,000 cubic cm. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'52.5','Capacity = 50×30×35/1000 liters. Rounded result: 52.5.','Original water tank exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000031','ESTIMATE','A tap delivers 7 liters per minute at a constant rate. Estimate minutes to fill an empty 135-liter tank. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'19.3','Time = volume/flow = 135/7 minutes. Rounded result: 19.3.','Original flow filling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000032','ESTIMATE','A 175-watt lamp runs for 4 hours. Estimate electricity used in kilowatt-hours. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'0.7','Energy = power×time = 175/1000×4 kWh. Rounded result: 0.7.','Original electric energy exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000033','ESTIMATE','An appliance uses 12.5 kWh. Electricity costs $0.23 per kWh with no fixed fees or taxes. Estimate cost in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'2.88','Cost = 12.5×0.23 dollars. Rounded result: 2.88.','Original energy cost exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000034','ESTIMATE','A walker takes 6170 steps, each 0.72 meters long. Estimate total distance in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'4.4','Distance = 6170×0.72/1000 km. Rounded result: 4.4.','Original walking distance exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000035','ESTIMATE','A sample of 100 identical-sized plots contains 35 trees. Assume the same density over 1,250 plots. Estimate total trees. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'438','Scale sample count by 1250/100: 35×12.5 trees. Rounded result: 438.','Original sample extrapolation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000036','ESTIMATE','A solid block has volume 6.5 cubic centimeters and density 2.7 grams per cubic centimeter. Estimate mass in grams. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'17.6','Mass = density×volume = 2.7×6.5 grams. Rounded result: 17.6.','Original density mass exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000037','ESTIMATE','An exchange model gives exactly 35.5 baht per US dollar, without fees. Estimate baht received for $85. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'3018','Baht = 85×35.5. Rounded result: 3018.','Original currency conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000038','ESTIMATE','A budget is $675. Assume 13% goes to transport. Estimate transport spending in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'87.75','Transport = 675×13/100 dollars. Rounded result: 87.75.','Original percentage budget exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000039','ESTIMATE','A model population starts at 5000 and grows exactly 5% each year for 3 years. Estimate its final population. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'5788','Compound growth: 5000×1.05^3. Rounded result: 5788.','Original compound growth exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000040','ESTIMATE','Equipment initially costs $6000 and retains 85% of its value after each year. Estimate its value after 3 years in dollars. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'3685','Value = 6000×0.85^3 dollars. Rounded result: 3685.','Original compound depreciation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000041','ESTIMATE','A cyclist rides 15 km outward at 12 km/h and the same distance back at 18 km/h. No stops. Estimate overall average speed in km/h. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'14.4','Total distance 30 km / total time (15/12+15/18) h = 14.4 km/h. Rounded result: 14.4.','Original average speed round trip exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000042','ESTIMATE','A class has 5 students scoring 70 and 3 students scoring 90. Estimate their combined mean score in points. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'77.5','Weighted mean = (5×70+3×90)/(5+3) points. Rounded result: 77.5.','Original weighted mean exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000043','ESTIMATE','A circular garden has radius 5.2 meters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'84.9','Area = pi×radius² = 3.1416×5.2² m². Rounded result: 84.9.','Original circular area exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000044','ESTIMATE','A wheel has radius 18.5 centimeters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its circumference in centimeters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'116.2','Circumference = 2×3.1416×18.5 cm. Rounded result: 116.2.','Original circular circumference exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000045','ESTIMATE','A map scale is 1:50,000. A measured route is 8.5 cm on the map. Estimate actual route length in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'4.3','Each map centimeter represents 50,000 cm = 0.5 km; distance = 8.5×0.5 km. Rounded result: 4.3.','Original map scaling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000046','ESTIMATE','A parcel weighs 11.5 pounds. Use exactly 1 pound = 0.45359237 kg. Estimate mass in kilograms. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'5.2','Mass = 11.5×0.45359237 kg. Rounded result: 5.2.','Original unit conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000047','ESTIMATE','A 625-megabyte file downloads at exactly 25 megabits per second. Use 8 bits per byte and decimal units; ignore overhead. Estimate seconds needed. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'200','Time = file size×8/link rate = 625×8/25 seconds. Rounded result: 200.','Original download time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000048','ESTIMATE','A drive offers exactly 50 decimal gigabytes. Each photo occupies exactly 4 decimal megabytes. Ignoring filesystem overhead, estimate how many whole photos fit; 1 GB = 1,000 MB. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'12500','Photo count = 50×1000/4; the result is an integer. Rounded result: 12500.','Original storage capacity exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000049','ESTIMATE','A factory makes 65 items per hour at a constant rate. Estimate items produced during 51 minutes, allowing fractional theoretical output before rounding. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'55','Output = 65×51/60 items. Rounded result: 55.','Original production throughput exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000050','ESTIMATE','A solution contains 12.5 grams of dissolved salt in a final volume of 5 liters. Estimate concentration in grams per liter. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'2.5','Concentration = dissolved mass/final volume = 12.5/5 g/L. Rounded result: 2.5.','Original dilution concentration exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000051','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A hidden token is in one of 64 numbered lockers. Each question may ask membership in any chosen subset and receives a truthful yes/no answer. Minimum worst-case questions?','["5", "6", "7", "9"]','"6"','6 bits distinguish 2^6=64 possibilities; successive halves attain this bound.','Original binary information exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000052','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 81 coins exactly one is heavier; all genuine coins weigh equally. With a balance scale, minimum worst-case weighings?','["4", "5", "7", "3"]','"4"','Each weighing has three outcomes. Split into three equal groups repeatedly: 4 weighings distinguish 3^4=81 coins.','Original balance ternary search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000053','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A drawer has unlimited socks in 6 colors. Picking blindly, how many socks guarantee a matching color pair?','["8", "10", "6", "7"]','"7"','6 picks could all have different colors; pick 7 repeats a color.','Original pigeonhole socks exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000054','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag has unlimited beads in 6 colors. How many blind picks guarantee three of one color?','["16", "12", "13", "14"]','"13"','At most two per color gives 12 without a triple; the next forces three.','Original pigeonhole triples exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000055','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A club of 9 members plays one game between every unordered pair. How many games?','["35", "36", "37", "39"]','"36"','Choose two members: (9)(8)/2=36.','Original pair counting exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000056','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fence has 8 posts in a straight row, spaced 4 meters apart. Distance from first post to last, in meters?','["28", "29", "31", "27"]','"28"','There are 7 gaps, each 4 meters: 28.','Original inclusive intervals exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000057','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Four cards show 4, 10, Blue and Green. Every card has an integer on one side and exactly one of those colors on the other. To test “if the integer is divisible by 4, the color is Blue,” which shown cards must be turned?','["Blue only", "All four", "Neither numbered card nor Green", "4 and Green"]','"4 and Green"','Turn every visible multiple of 4 to check Blue, and Green to exclude a multiple on its reverse. Blue and visible nonmultiples cannot falsify the rule.','Original conditional counterexample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000058','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','All squares are rectangles. Some rectangles are blue. Must some squares be blue?','["Yes, exactly one square is blue", "No rectangle is blue", "No; the blue rectangles may all be nonsquare", "Yes, all squares are blue"]','"No; the blue rectangles may all be nonsquare"','The existential statement need not concern the subset of squares.','Original syllogism exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000059','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Any licensed driver has passed a test. Nia has not passed a test. Which conclusion follows?','["Nia cannot learn to drive", "Nia is not a licensed driver", "Nia is licensed", "Nia owns no car"]','"Nia is not a licensed driver"','This is the contrapositive of licensed implies passed.','Original implication chains exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000060','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','If a cup is full, the scale reads heavy. The cup is not full. Must the scale read light?','["No", "Yes, necessarily", "The cup must be empty", "The scale is broken"]','"No"','The rule gives no conclusion when its antecedent is false.','Original conditional diagnosis exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000061','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the logical negation of “both lamps are on”?','["Both lamps are off", "Exactly one lamp is on", "At least one lamp is on", "At least one lamp is off"]','"At least one lamp is off"','Not(A and B) equals not A or not B.','Original logical negation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000062','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A secret code is an integer from 1 through 6. Exactly one statement is true: A says it is even; B says it is divisible by 3. How many possible codes satisfy this?','["6", "2", "3", "4"]','"3"','Count integers in exactly one of the two sets. The valid codes are 2, 3, 4. Equivalently floor(6/2)+floor(6/3)−2floor(6/6)=3.','Original truth assignment exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000063','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Exactly four runners finish without ties. Mina finishes before Paz; Oren finishes after Paz; Rui finishes after Oren. Who finishes third?','["Rui", "Oren", "Mina", "Paz"]','"Oren"','The constraints force the order Mina → Paz → Oren → Rui; the third runner is Oren.','Original ordering constraints exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000064','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A knockout contest begins with 30 players. Every match eliminates exactly one player; no byes eliminate anyone. Matches needed for one champion?','["29", "30", "32", "28"]','"29"','Exactly 29 players must be eliminated; each match eliminates one.','Original tournament elimination exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000065','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','On a grid, go 6 steps east and 2 steps north in any order, with no other moves. How many distinct step sequences?','["29", "31", "27", "28"]','"28"','Choose the two north-step positions among 8: C(8,2)=28.','Original grid paths exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000066','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','6 different keys must be tried on one lock. Exactly one works; unsuccessful keys are never retried. Maximum trials needed to open it?','["9", "5", "6", "7"]','"6"','The working key can be last among all 6; identifying the last key is not opening the lock.','Original cycle permutation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000067','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A menu has 6 optional toppings; each can be included once or omitted independently. How many topping selections including none?','["63", "64", "65", "67"]','"64"','Each of 6 toppings has two states, so 2^6=64.','Original subset counts exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000068','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose a president and a different secretary from 6 candidates. How many assignments?','["30", "31", "33", "29"]','"30"','6 president choices times 5 secretary choices=30.','Original ordered arrangements exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000069','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 30 students, 11 study art, 9 study music, and 6 study both. How many study at least one?','["15", "17", "13", "14"]','"14"','Add the groups and subtract double-counted overlap: 11+9-6=14.','Original inclusion exclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000070','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A 12-hour clock shows 4:00. What hour number appears 19 hours later?','["14", "10", "11", "12"]','"11"','Add 19 hours and reduce modulo 12, labeling remainder zero as 12.','Original modular clock exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000071','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A board has 13 tiles, initially 4 black and the rest white. A move flips exactly two different tiles. After any number of moves, what is the largest reachable number of black tiles?','["11", "12", "13", "15"]','"12"','Each move preserves black-count parity. The largest count at most 13 with parity 0 is 12; flipping pairs of white tiles attains it.','Original parity invariant exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000072','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','An integer lies from 1 through 63. Each trial guesses one value and reports correct, too low, or too high. Minimum worst-case trials to identify it by a correct guess?','["6", "7", "9", "5"]','"6"','With 6 trials binary search covers at most 2^6-1 values, achieved by guessing midpoints.','Original bounded search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000073','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Two identical test sensors break above an unknown threshold in a 10-level building, and survive at or below it. The threshold is the highest safe floor, from 0 (all floors unsafe) through 10 (all floors safe). Broken sensors cannot be reused. Minimum worst-case drops to find the threshold?','["5", "7", "3", "4"]','"4"','4 drops cover a descending sum from 4 through 1, totaling 4×5/2=10 levels using decreasing gaps; 3 drops cover fewer.','Original two egg threshold exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000074','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the smallest positive integer greater than 36 that is divisible by both 2 and 3?','["45", "41", "42", "43"]','"42"','Common multiples are multiples of 6; the next after 36 is 42.','Original remainders exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000075','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A prize is in exactly one of boxes numbered 1 through 6. Exactly one speaker tells the truth. A says “the box number is even”; B says “the box number is odd”; C says “the box number is divisible by 3.” How many box numbers remain possible?','["3", "4", "5", "7"]','"4"','A and B are complementary, so exactly one already tells the truth. C must be false. Excluding 2 multiples of 3 leaves 6−2=4 possibilities.','Original exactly one speaker exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000051','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 4 times. Probability that every flip is heads?','["0", "1/16", "1/32", "17/32"]','"1/16"','Independence multiplies 4 factors of 1/2: 1/16.','Original all heads exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000052','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is flipped independently 5 times. Probability of at least one head?','["31/32", "31/64", "63/64", "0"]','"31/32"','Take the complement of all tails: 1−(1/2)^5.','Original at least one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000053','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 6 times. Probability of exactly one head?','["3/64", "35/64", "0", "3/32"]','"3/32"','Choose one head position in 6 ways, each sequence has probability 1/64.','Original exactly one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000054','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is tossed independently 7 times. Probability of exactly two heads?','["149/256", "0", "21/128", "21/256"]','"21/128"','C(7,2)=21 qualifying sequences out of 128.','Original binomial two successes exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000055','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag holds 6 red and 5 blue tokens. Every token is equally likely to be drawn once. Probability of red?','["0", "6/11", "3/11", "17/22"]','"6/11"','6 favorable tokens among 11 total.','Original one uniform draw exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000056','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two tokens uniformly without replacement from 6 red and 7 blue. Probability both are red?','["5/26", "5/52", "31/52", "0"]','"5/26"','Multiply 6/13 by 5/12.','Original without replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000057','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw twice uniformly with replacement from 6 red and 8 blue tokens. Probability both red?','["9/98", "29/49", "0", "9/49"]','"9/49"','Replacement makes independent draws with red probability 6/14; square it.','Original with replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000058','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two uniformly without replacement from 7 red and 9 blue tokens. Probability of one of each color?','["61/80", "0", "21/40", "21/80"]','"21/40"','Add red-blue and blue-red: 2×7×9/(16×15).','Original one of each color exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000059','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 36. Probability it is divisible by 3?','["0", "1/3", "1/6", "2/3"]','"1/3"','12 multiples of 3 among 36 integers.','Original uniform integer divisor exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000060','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 36. Probability it is divisible by 2 or 3?','["2/3", "1/3", "5/6", "0"]','"2/3"','18 multiples of 2 plus 12 multiples of 3 minus 6 multiples of 6 gives 24/36.','Original uniform integer union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000061','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Randomly order 6 distinct books, all orders equally likely. Probability a specified book is first?','["1/12", "7/12", "0", "1/6"]','"1/6"','Each book has equal probability of any of the 6 positions.','Original random permutation first exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000062','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Arrange 8 distinct people uniformly in a straight line. Probability two specified people stand next to each other?','["5/8", "0", "1/4", "1/8"]','"1/4"','Treat the pair as a block with two internal orders: 2×(7)!/(8)!=2/8.','Original permutation adjacency exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000063','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A committee of 2 is selected uniformly from 9 distinct people. Probability a specified person is selected?','["0", "2/9", "1/9", "11/18"]','"2/9"','The person belongs to 8 of C(9,2) committees, yielding 2/9.','Original subset inclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000064','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A lottery chooses one winning ticket uniformly from 120 tickets. You own 6 distinct tickets. Probability you win?','["1/20", "1/40", "21/40", "0"]','"1/20"','6 of 120 equally likely tickets are yours.','Original matching lottery exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000065','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/5 and 1/7. Probability both occur?','["1/70", "18/35", "0", "1/35"]','"1/35"','For independent events multiply 1/5 by 1/7.','Original independent intersections exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000066','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/6 and 1/8. Probability at least one occurs?','["61/96", "0", "13/48", "13/96"]','"13/48"','P(A or B)=P(A)+P(B)−P(A)P(B)=13/48.','Original independent union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000067','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A number is chosen uniformly from 1 through 24. Given it is even, probability it is also divisible by 4?','["0", "1/2", "1/4", "3/4"]','"1/2"','There are 12 even values and 6 multiples of 4 in the restricted sample.','Original conditional restricted sample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000068','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A group has 6 engineers and 3 artists. Choose 3 people uniformly without replacement. Probability all are engineers?','["5/21", "5/42", "13/21", "0"]','"5/21"','C(6,3)/C(9,3)=20/84.','Original hypergeometric committee exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000069','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent attempts succeed with probability 1/3 each. Probability the first success is on attempt 4?','["4/81", "89/162", "0", "8/81"]','"8/81"','3 failures followed by success: (2/3)^3×(1/3).','Original geometric first success exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000070','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice, each labeled 1 through 6. Probability their values match?','["7/12", "0", "1/6", "1/12"]','"1/6"','6 matching ordered pairs among 36 equally likely pairs.','Original uniform dice equality exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000071','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 7. Probability the first value is strictly greater than the second?','["0", "3/7", "3/14", "5/7"]','"3/7"','Excluding 7 ties, symmetry halves the remaining 42 ordered pairs: 6/(14).','Original uniform dice ordering exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000072','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 8. Probability at least one shows 8?','["15/64", "15/128", "79/128", "0"]','"15/64"','Complement of neither showing the top face: 1−(7/8)².','Original dice maximum exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000073','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A code has 4 digits chosen independently uniformly from 0–9; leading zeros allowed. Probability all digits are nonzero? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["32.80%", "21.87%", "82.81%", "65.61%"]','"65.61%"','Each digit is nonzero with probability 9/10; multiply 4 factors. This is approximately 65.61%.','Original random digit code exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000074','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Pick one of two bags by fair coin. Bag A has 6 red and 1 blue token; bag B has 1 red and 6 blue. Draw uniformly. Given red, probability bag A was chosen?','["13/14", "0", "6/7", "3/7"]','"6/7"','Bayes: (1/2×6/7)/(1/2×6/7+1/2×1/7)=6/7.','Original Bayes selected bag exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000075','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','4 people have independent birthdays uniform over a fictional 60-day year. Probability all birthdays differ? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["95.15%", "90.30%", "45.15%", "30.10%"]','"90.30%"','Successive birthday choices: 60/60 × 59/60 × ... × 57/60. This is approximately 90.30%.','Original sampling distinct birthdays exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000051','ESTIMATE','For planning, a vehicle covers 102 km at a constant 75 km/h with no stops. Estimate travel time in minutes. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'82','Time = distance/speed × 60 = 102/75×60 minutes. Rounded result: 82.','Original travel time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000052','ESTIMATE','A car averages 7 liters per 100 km over a 186-km route. Estimate fuel needed in liters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'13.0','Fuel = 186×7/100 liters. Rounded result: 13.0.','Original fuel consumption exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000053','ESTIMATE','A rectangular floor is 6.5 m long and 4.25 m wide. Assuming no waste, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'27.6','Area = length×width = 6.5×4.25 m². Rounded result: 27.6.','Original area coverage exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000054','ESTIMATE','A wall area is 78 square meters. One liter covers 7 square meters per coat. For two coats, estimate liters of paint, ignoring waste. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'22.3','Paint volume = 78×2/7 liters. Rounded result: 22.3.','Original paint demand exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000055','ESTIMATE','A rectangular tank has internal dimensions 60 cm by 40 cm by 35 cm. Estimate capacity in liters; use 1 liter = 1,000 cubic cm. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'84.0','Capacity = 60×40×35/1000 liters. Rounded result: 84.0.','Original water tank exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000056','ESTIMATE','A tap delivers 8 liters per minute at a constant rate. Estimate minutes to fill an empty 162-liter tank. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'20.3','Time = volume/flow = 162/8 minutes. Rounded result: 20.3.','Original flow filling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000057','ESTIMATE','A 210-watt lamp runs for 5 hours. Estimate electricity used in kilowatt-hours. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'1.05','Energy = power×time = 210/1000×5 kWh. Rounded result: 1.05.','Original electric energy exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000058','ESTIMATE','An appliance uses 15.0 kWh. Electricity costs $0.23 per kWh with no fixed fees or taxes. Estimate cost in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'3.45','Cost = 15.0×0.23 dollars. Rounded result: 3.45.','Original energy cost exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000059','ESTIMATE','A walker takes 7404 steps, each 0.72 meters long. Estimate total distance in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'5.3','Distance = 7404×0.72/1000 km. Rounded result: 5.3.','Original walking distance exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000060','ESTIMATE','A sample of 100 identical-sized plots contains 42 trees. Assume the same density over 1,250 plots. Estimate total trees. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'525','Scale sample count by 1250/100: 42×12.5 trees. Rounded result: 525.','Original sample extrapolation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000061','ESTIMATE','A solid block has volume 7.8 cubic centimeters and density 2.7 grams per cubic centimeter. Estimate mass in grams. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'21.1','Mass = density×volume = 2.7×7.8 grams. Rounded result: 21.1.','Original density mass exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000062','ESTIMATE','An exchange model gives exactly 35.5 baht per US dollar, without fees. Estimate baht received for $102. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'3621','Baht = 102×35.5. Rounded result: 3621.','Original currency conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000063','ESTIMATE','A budget is $810. Assume 14% goes to transport. Estimate transport spending in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'113.4','Transport = 810×14/100 dollars. Rounded result: 113.4.','Original percentage budget exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000064','ESTIMATE','A model population starts at 6000 and grows exactly 5% each year for 4 years. Estimate its final population. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'7293','Compound growth: 6000×1.05^4. Rounded result: 7293.','Original compound growth exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000065','ESTIMATE','Equipment initially costs $7200 and retains 85% of its value after each year. Estimate its value after 4 years in dollars. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'3758','Value = 7200×0.85^4 dollars. Rounded result: 3758.','Original compound depreciation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000066','ESTIMATE','A cyclist rides 18 km outward at 12 km/h and the same distance back at 18 km/h. No stops. Estimate overall average speed in km/h. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'14.4','Total distance 36 km / total time (18/12+18/18) h = 14.4 km/h. Rounded result: 14.4.','Original average speed round trip exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000067','ESTIMATE','A class has 6 students scoring 70 and 4 students scoring 90. Estimate their combined mean score in points. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'78.0','Weighted mean = (6×70+4×90)/(6+4) points. Rounded result: 78.0.','Original weighted mean exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000068','ESTIMATE','A circular garden has radius 6.2 meters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'120.8','Area = pi×radius² = 3.1416×6.2² m². Rounded result: 120.8.','Original circular area exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000069','ESTIMATE','A wheel has radius 22.2 centimeters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its circumference in centimeters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'139.5','Circumference = 2×3.1416×22.2 cm. Rounded result: 139.5.','Original circular circumference exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000070','ESTIMATE','A map scale is 1:50,000. A measured route is 10.2 cm on the map. Estimate actual route length in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'5.1','Each map centimeter represents 50,000 cm = 0.5 km; distance = 10.2×0.5 km. Rounded result: 5.1.','Original map scaling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000071','ESTIMATE','A parcel weighs 13.8 pounds. Use exactly 1 pound = 0.45359237 kg. Estimate mass in kilograms. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'6.3','Mass = 13.8×0.45359237 kg. Rounded result: 6.3.','Original unit conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000072','ESTIMATE','A 750-megabyte file downloads at exactly 30 megabits per second. Use 8 bits per byte and decimal units; ignore overhead. Estimate seconds needed. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'200','Time = file size×8/link rate = 750×8/30 seconds. Rounded result: 200.','Original download time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000073','ESTIMATE','A drive offers exactly 60 decimal gigabytes. Each photo occupies exactly 4 decimal megabytes. Ignoring filesystem overhead, estimate how many whole photos fit; 1 GB = 1,000 MB. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'15000','Photo count = 60×1000/4; the result is an integer. Rounded result: 15000.','Original storage capacity exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000074','ESTIMATE','A factory makes 78 items per hour at a constant rate. Estimate items produced during 68 minutes, allowing fractional theoretical output before rounding. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'88','Output = 78×68/60 items. Rounded result: 88.','Original production throughput exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000075','ESTIMATE','A solution contains 15.0 grams of dissolved salt in a final volume of 6 liters. Estimate concentration in grams per liter. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'2.5','Concentration = dissolved mass/final volume = 15.0/6 g/L. Rounded result: 2.5.','Original dilution concentration exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000076','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A hidden token is in one of 128 numbered lockers. Each question may ask membership in any chosen subset and receives a truthful yes/no answer. Minimum worst-case questions?','["7", "8", "10", "6"]','"7"','7 bits distinguish 2^7=128 possibilities; successive halves attain this bound.','Original binary information exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000077','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 243 coins exactly one is heavier; all genuine coins weigh equally. With a balance scale, minimum worst-case weighings?','["6", "8", "4", "5"]','"5"','Each weighing has three outcomes. Split into three equal groups repeatedly: 5 weighings distinguish 3^5=243 coins.','Original balance ternary search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000078','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A drawer has unlimited socks in 7 colors. Picking blindly, how many socks guarantee a matching color pair?','["11", "7", "8", "9"]','"8"','7 picks could all have different colors; pick 8 repeats a color.','Original pigeonhole socks exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000079','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag has unlimited beads in 7 colors. How many blind picks guarantee three of one color?','["14", "15", "16", "18"]','"15"','At most two per color gives 14 without a triple; the next forces three.','Original pigeonhole triples exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000080','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A club of 10 members plays one game between every unordered pair. How many games?','["45", "46", "48", "44"]','"45"','Choose two members: (10)(9)/2=45.','Original pair counting exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000081','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fence has 9 posts in a straight row, spaced 5 meters apart. Distance from first post to last, in meters?','["41", "43", "39", "40"]','"40"','There are 8 gaps, each 5 meters: 40.','Original inclusive intervals exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000082','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Four cards show 5, 12, Blue and Green. Every card has an integer on one side and exactly one of those colors on the other. To test “if the integer is divisible by 5, the color is Blue,” which shown cards must be turned?','["All four", "Neither numbered card nor Green", "5 and Green", "Blue only"]','"5 and Green"','Turn every visible multiple of 5 to check Blue, and Green to exclude a multiple on its reverse. Blue and visible nonmultiples cannot falsify the rule.','Original conditional counterexample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000083','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Every dancer is an athlete. No athlete is asleep. Which statement is guaranteed?','["Some dancers are asleep", "No dancer is asleep", "Every awake person dances", "No athletes exist"]','"No dancer is asleep"','Dancers are contained in athletes, and athletes exclude sleeping people.','Original syllogism exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000084','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','If a device is powered, its indicator is lit. If its indicator is lit, its battery is connected. The battery is not connected. What follows?','["The device is not powered", "The device is powered", "The indicator is lit", "The battery is connected"]','"The device is not powered"','Not connected excludes a lit indicator, which excludes being powered.','Original implication chains exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000085','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A lamp is on if and only if the switch is up. The lamp is on. What follows?','["The switch is down", "No switch position follows", "The switch is absent", "The switch is up"]','"The switch is up"','A biconditional supplies both directions, including lamp on implies switch up.','Original conditional diagnosis exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000086','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the logical negation of “at least one door is open”?','["Every door is open", "Some doors are open", "Every door is closed", "Exactly one door is closed"]','"Every door is closed"','With open/closed taken as exhaustive alternatives, not any open means all closed.','Original logical negation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000087','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A secret code is an integer from 1 through 7. Exactly one statement is true: A says it is even; B says it is divisible by 3. How many possible codes satisfy this?','["2", "3", "4", "6"]','"3"','Count integers in exactly one of the two sets. The valid codes are 2, 3, 4. Equivalently floor(7/2)+floor(7/3)−2floor(7/6)=3.','Original truth assignment exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000088','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Exactly four runners finish without ties. Mina finishes before Paz; Rui finishes after Paz; Oren finishes after Rui. Who finishes fourth?','["Oren", "Mina", "Paz", "Rui"]','"Oren"','The constraints force the order Mina → Paz → Rui → Oren; the fourth runner is Oren.','Original ordering constraints exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000089','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A knockout contest begins with 35 players. Every match eliminates exactly one player; no byes eliminate anyone. Matches needed for one champion?','["35", "37", "33", "34"]','"34"','Exactly 34 players must be eliminated; each match eliminates one.','Original tournament elimination exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000090','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','On a grid, go 7 steps east and 2 steps north in any order, with no other moves. How many distinct step sequences?','["39", "35", "36", "37"]','"36"','Choose the two north-step positions among 9: C(9,2)=36.','Original grid paths exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000091','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','7 different keys must be tried on one lock. Exactly one works; unsuccessful keys are never retried. Maximum trials needed to open it?','["6", "7", "8", "10"]','"7"','The working key can be last among all 7; identifying the last key is not opening the lock.','Original cycle permutation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000092','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A menu has 7 optional toppings; each can be included once or omitted independently. How many topping selections including none?','["128", "129", "131", "127"]','"128"','Each of 7 toppings has two states, so 2^7=128.','Original subset counts exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000093','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose a president and a different secretary from 7 candidates. How many assignments?','["43", "45", "41", "42"]','"42"','7 president choices times 6 secretary choices=42.','Original ordered arrangements exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000094','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 35 students, 12 study art, 10 study music, and 7 study both. How many study at least one?','["18", "14", "15", "16"]','"15"','Add the groups and subtract double-counted overlap: 12+10-7=15.','Original inclusion exclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000095','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A 12-hour clock shows 5:00. What hour number appears 20 hours later?','["0", "1", "2", "4"]','"1"','Add 20 hours and reduce modulo 12, labeling remainder zero as 12.','Original modular clock exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000096','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A board has 14 tiles, initially 5 black and the rest white. A move flips exactly two different tiles. After any number of moves, what is the largest reachable number of black tiles?','["13", "14", "16", "12"]','"13"','Each move preserves black-count parity. The largest count at most 14 with parity 1 is 13; flipping pairs of white tiles attains it.','Original parity invariant exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000097','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','An integer lies from 1 through 127. Each trial guesses one value and reports correct, too low, or too high. Minimum worst-case trials to identify it by a correct guess?','["8", "10", "6", "7"]','"7"','With 7 trials binary search covers at most 2^7-1 values, achieved by guessing midpoints.','Original bounded search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000098','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Two identical test sensors break above an unknown threshold in a 15-level building, and survive at or below it. The threshold is the highest safe floor, from 0 (all floors unsafe) through 15 (all floors safe). Broken sensors cannot be reused. Minimum worst-case drops to find the threshold?','["8", "4", "5", "6"]','"5"','5 drops cover a descending sum from 5 through 1, totaling 5×6/2=15 levels using decreasing gaps; 4 drops cover fewer.','Original two egg threshold exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000099','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the smallest positive integer greater than 42 that is divisible by both 2 and 3?','["47", "48", "49", "51"]','"48"','Common multiples are multiples of 6; the next after 42 is 48.','Original remainders exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000100','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A prize is in exactly one of boxes numbered 1 through 7. Exactly one speaker tells the truth. A says “the box number is even”; B says “the box number is odd”; C says “the box number is divisible by 3.” How many box numbers remain possible?','["5", "6", "8", "4"]','"5"','A and B are complementary, so exactly one already tells the truth. C must be false. Excluding 2 multiples of 3 leaves 7−2=5 possibilities.','Original exactly one speaker exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000076','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 5 times. Probability that every flip is heads?','["1/32", "1/64", "33/64", "0"]','"1/32"','Independence multiplies 5 factors of 1/2: 1/32.','Original all heads exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000077','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is flipped independently 6 times. Probability of at least one head?','["63/128", "127/128", "0", "63/64"]','"63/64"','Take the complement of all tails: 1−(1/2)^6.','Original at least one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000078','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 7 times. Probability of exactly one head?','["135/256", "0", "7/128", "7/256"]','"7/128"','Choose one head position in 7 ways, each sequence has probability 1/128.','Original exactly one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000079','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is tossed independently 8 times. Probability of exactly two heads?','["0", "7/64", "7/128", "71/128"]','"7/64"','C(8,2)=28 qualifying sequences out of 256.','Original binomial two successes exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000080','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag holds 7 red and 6 blue tokens. Every token is equally likely to be drawn once. Probability of red?','["7/13", "7/26", "10/13", "0"]','"7/13"','7 favorable tokens among 13 total.','Original one uniform draw exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000081','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two tokens uniformly without replacement from 7 red and 8 blue. Probability both are red?','["1/10", "3/5", "0", "1/5"]','"1/5"','Multiply 7/15 by 6/14.','Original without replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000082','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw twice uniformly with replacement from 7 red and 9 blue tokens. Probability both red?','["305/512", "0", "49/256", "49/512"]','"49/256"','Replacement makes independent draws with red probability 7/16; square it.','Original with replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000083','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two uniformly without replacement from 8 red and 10 blue tokens. Probability of one of each color?','["0", "80/153", "40/153", "233/306"]','"80/153"','Add red-blue and blue-red: 2×8×10/(18×17).','Original one of each color exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000084','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 42. Probability it is divisible by 3?','["1/3", "1/6", "2/3", "0"]','"1/3"','14 multiples of 3 among 42 integers.','Original uniform integer divisor exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000085','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 42. Probability it is divisible by 2 or 3?','["1/3", "5/6", "0", "2/3"]','"2/3"','21 multiples of 2 plus 14 multiples of 3 minus 7 multiples of 6 gives 28/42.','Original uniform integer union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000086','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Randomly order 7 distinct books, all orders equally likely. Probability a specified book is first?','["4/7", "0", "1/7", "1/14"]','"1/7"','Each book has equal probability of any of the 7 positions.','Original random permutation first exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000087','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Arrange 9 distinct people uniformly in a straight line. Probability two specified people stand next to each other?','["0", "2/9", "1/9", "11/18"]','"2/9"','Treat the pair as a block with two internal orders: 2×(8)!/(9)!=2/9.','Original permutation adjacency exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000088','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A committee of 2 is selected uniformly from 10 distinct people. Probability a specified person is selected?','["1/5", "1/10", "3/5", "0"]','"1/5"','The person belongs to 9 of C(10,2) committees, yielding 2/10.','Original subset inclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000089','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A lottery chooses one winning ticket uniformly from 140 tickets. You own 7 distinct tickets. Probability you win?','["1/40", "21/40", "0", "1/20"]','"1/20"','7 of 140 equally likely tickets are yours.','Original matching lottery exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000090','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/6 and 1/8. Probability both occur?','["49/96", "0", "1/48", "1/96"]','"1/48"','For independent events multiply 1/6 by 1/8.','Original independent intersections exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000091','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/7 and 1/9. Probability at least one occurs?','["0", "5/21", "5/42", "13/21"]','"5/21"','P(A or B)=P(A)+P(B)−P(A)P(B)=5/21.','Original independent union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000092','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A number is chosen uniformly from 1 through 28. Given it is even, probability it is also divisible by 4?','["1/2", "1/4", "3/4", "0"]','"1/2"','There are 14 even values and 7 multiples of 4 in the restricted sample.','Original conditional restricted sample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000093','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A group has 7 engineers and 3 artists. Choose 3 people uniformly without replacement. Probability all are engineers?','["7/48", "31/48", "0", "7/24"]','"7/24"','C(7,3)/C(10,3)=35/120.','Original hypergeometric committee exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000094','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent attempts succeed with probability 1/3 each. Probability the first success is on attempt 5?','["259/486", "0", "16/243", "8/243"]','"16/243"','4 failures followed by success: (2/3)^4×(1/3).','Original geometric first success exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000095','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice, each labeled 1 through 7. Probability their values match?','["0", "1/7", "1/14", "4/7"]','"1/7"','7 matching ordered pairs among 49 equally likely pairs.','Original uniform dice equality exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000096','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 8. Probability the first value is strictly greater than the second?','["7/16", "7/32", "23/32", "0"]','"7/16"','Excluding 8 ties, symmetry halves the remaining 56 ordered pairs: 7/(16).','Original uniform dice ordering exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000097','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 9. Probability at least one shows 9?','["17/162", "49/81", "0", "17/81"]','"17/81"','Complement of neither showing the top face: 1−(8/9)².','Original dice maximum exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000098','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A code has 5 digits chosen independently uniformly from 0–9; leading zeros allowed. Probability all digits are nonzero? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["19.68%", "79.52%", "59.05%", "29.52%"]','"59.05%"','Each digit is nonzero with probability 9/10; multiply 5 factors. This is approximately 59.05%.','Original random digit code exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000099','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Pick one of two bags by fair coin. Bag A has 7 red and 1 blue token; bag B has 1 red and 7 blue. Draw uniformly. Given red, probability bag A was chosen?','["0", "7/8", "7/16", "15/16"]','"7/8"','Bayes: (1/2×7/8)/(1/2×7/8+1/2×1/8)=7/8.','Original Bayes selected bag exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000100','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','5 people have independent birthdays uniform over a fictional 70-day year. Probability all birthdays differ? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["86.41%", "43.21%", "28.80%", "93.21%"]','"86.41%"','Successive birthday choices: 70/70 × 69/70 × ... × 66/70. This is approximately 86.41%.','Original sampling distinct birthdays exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000076','ESTIMATE','For planning, a vehicle covers 119 km at a constant 85 km/h with no stops. Estimate travel time in minutes. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'84','Time = distance/speed × 60 = 119/85×60 minutes. Rounded result: 84.','Original travel time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000077','ESTIMATE','A car averages 8 liters per 100 km over a 217-km route. Estimate fuel needed in liters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'17.4','Fuel = 217×8/100 liters. Rounded result: 17.4.','Original fuel consumption exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000078','ESTIMATE','A rectangular floor is 7.5 m long and 5.25 m wide. Assuming no waste, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'39.4','Area = length×width = 7.5×5.25 m². Rounded result: 39.4.','Original area coverage exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000079','ESTIMATE','A wall area is 91 square meters. One liter covers 7 square meters per coat. For two coats, estimate liters of paint, ignoring waste. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'26.0','Paint volume = 91×2/7 liters. Rounded result: 26.0.','Original paint demand exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000080','ESTIMATE','A rectangular tank has internal dimensions 70 cm by 50 cm by 35 cm. Estimate capacity in liters; use 1 liter = 1,000 cubic cm. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'122.5','Capacity = 70×50×35/1000 liters. Rounded result: 122.5.','Original water tank exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000081','ESTIMATE','A tap delivers 9 liters per minute at a constant rate. Estimate minutes to fill an empty 189-liter tank. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'21.0','Time = volume/flow = 189/9 minutes. Rounded result: 21.0.','Original flow filling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000082','ESTIMATE','A 245-watt lamp runs for 6 hours. Estimate electricity used in kilowatt-hours. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'1.47','Energy = power×time = 245/1000×6 kWh. Rounded result: 1.47.','Original electric energy exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000083','ESTIMATE','An appliance uses 17.5 kWh. Electricity costs $0.23 per kWh with no fixed fees or taxes. Estimate cost in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'4.03','Cost = 17.5×0.23 dollars. Rounded result: 4.03.','Original energy cost exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000084','ESTIMATE','A walker takes 8638 steps, each 0.72 meters long. Estimate total distance in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'6.2','Distance = 8638×0.72/1000 km. Rounded result: 6.2.','Original walking distance exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000085','ESTIMATE','A sample of 100 identical-sized plots contains 49 trees. Assume the same density over 1,250 plots. Estimate total trees. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'613','Scale sample count by 1250/100: 49×12.5 trees. Rounded result: 613.','Original sample extrapolation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000086','ESTIMATE','A solid block has volume 9.1 cubic centimeters and density 2.7 grams per cubic centimeter. Estimate mass in grams. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'24.6','Mass = density×volume = 2.7×9.1 grams. Rounded result: 24.6.','Original density mass exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000087','ESTIMATE','An exchange model gives exactly 35.5 baht per US dollar, without fees. Estimate baht received for $119. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'4225','Baht = 119×35.5. Rounded result: 4225.','Original currency conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000088','ESTIMATE','A budget is $945. Assume 15% goes to transport. Estimate transport spending in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'141.75','Transport = 945×15/100 dollars. Rounded result: 141.75.','Original percentage budget exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000089','ESTIMATE','A model population starts at 7000 and grows exactly 5% each year for 5 years. Estimate its final population. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'8934','Compound growth: 7000×1.05^5. Rounded result: 8934.','Original compound growth exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
