('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000091','ESTIMATE','A cyclist rides 21 km outward at 12 km/h and the same distance back at 18 km/h. No stops. Estimate overall average speed in km/h. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'14.4','Total distance 42 km / total time (21/12+21/18) h = 14.4 km/h. Rounded result: 14.4.','Original average speed round trip exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000092','ESTIMATE','A class has 7 students scoring 70 and 5 students scoring 90. Estimate their combined mean score in points. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'78.3','Weighted mean = (7×70+5×90)/(7+5) points. Rounded result: 78.3.','Original weighted mean exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000093','ESTIMATE','A circular garden has radius 7.2 meters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'162.9','Area = pi×radius² = 3.1416×7.2² m². Rounded result: 162.9.','Original circular area exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000094','ESTIMATE','A wheel has radius 25.9 centimeters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its circumference in centimeters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'162.7','Circumference = 2×3.1416×25.9 cm. Rounded result: 162.7.','Original circular circumference exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000095','ESTIMATE','A map scale is 1:50,000. A measured route is 11.9 cm on the map. Estimate actual route length in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'6.0','Each map centimeter represents 50,000 cm = 0.5 km; distance = 11.9×0.5 km. Rounded result: 6.0.','Original map scaling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000096','ESTIMATE','A parcel weighs 16.1 pounds. Use exactly 1 pound = 0.45359237 kg. Estimate mass in kilograms. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'7.3','Mass = 16.1×0.45359237 kg. Rounded result: 7.3.','Original unit conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000097','ESTIMATE','A 875-megabyte file downloads at exactly 35 megabits per second. Use 8 bits per byte and decimal units; ignore overhead. Estimate seconds needed. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'200','Time = file size×8/link rate = 875×8/35 seconds. Rounded result: 200.','Original download time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000098','ESTIMATE','A drive offers exactly 70 decimal gigabytes. Each photo occupies exactly 4 decimal megabytes. Ignoring filesystem overhead, estimate how many whole photos fit; 1 GB = 1,000 MB. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'17500','Photo count = 70×1000/4; the result is an integer. Rounded result: 17500.','Original storage capacity exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000099','ESTIMATE','A factory makes 91 items per hour at a constant rate. Estimate items produced during 85 minutes, allowing fractional theoretical output before rounding. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'129','Output = 91×85/60 items. Rounded result: 129.','Original production throughput exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000100','ESTIMATE','A solution contains 17.5 grams of dissolved salt in a final volume of 7 liters. Estimate concentration in grams per liter. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'2.5','Concentration = dissolved mass/final volume = 17.5/7 g/L. Rounded result: 2.5.','Original dilution concentration exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000101','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A hidden token is in one of 256 numbered lockers. Each question may ask membership in any chosen subset and receives a truthful yes/no answer. Minimum worst-case questions?','["9", "11", "7", "8"]','"8"','8 bits distinguish 2^8=256 possibilities; successive halves attain this bound.','Original binary information exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000102','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 729 coins exactly one is heavier; all genuine coins weigh equally. With a balance scale, minimum worst-case weighings?','["9", "5", "6", "7"]','"6"','Each weighing has three outcomes. Split into three equal groups repeatedly: 6 weighings distinguish 3^6=729 coins.','Original balance ternary search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000103','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A drawer has unlimited socks in 8 colors. Picking blindly, how many socks guarantee a matching color pair?','["8", "9", "10", "12"]','"9"','8 picks could all have different colors; pick 9 repeats a color.','Original pigeonhole socks exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000104','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag has unlimited beads in 8 colors. How many blind picks guarantee three of one color?','["17", "18", "20", "16"]','"17"','At most two per color gives 16 without a triple; the next forces three.','Original pigeonhole triples exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000105','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A club of 11 members plays one game between every unordered pair. How many games?','["56", "58", "54", "55"]','"55"','Choose two members: (11)(10)/2=55.','Original pair counting exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000106','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fence has 10 posts in a straight row, spaced 6 meters apart. Distance from first post to last, in meters?','["57", "53", "54", "55"]','"54"','There are 9 gaps, each 6 meters: 54.','Original inclusive intervals exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000107','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Four cards show 6, 14, Blue and Green. Every card has an integer on one side and exactly one of those colors on the other. To test “if the integer is divisible by 2, the color is Blue,” which shown cards must be turned?','["Neither numbered card nor Green", "6 and 14 and Green", "Blue only", "All four"]','"6 and 14 and Green"','Turn every visible multiple of 2 to check Blue, and Green to exclude a multiple on its reverse. Blue and visible nonmultiples cannot falsify the rule.','Original conditional counterexample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000108','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Some librarians are cyclists. All cyclists wear helmets today. What must be true?','["Some librarians wear helmets today", "All librarians cycle", "No librarian wears a helmet", "All helmet wearers are librarians"]','"Some librarians wear helmets today"','The librarians known to be cyclists inherit the helmet property.','Original syllogism exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000109','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','If a shape is a square, all its sides have equal length. A shape has two unequal sides. What can be inferred?','["It is a circle", "It has five sides", "It is necessarily a rectangle", "It is not a square"]','"It is not a square"','Unequal sides contradict a necessary square property.','Original implication chains exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000110','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','At least one of A and B is selected. A is not selected. What follows?','["Neither is selected", "Both are selected", "B is selected", "B is not selected"]','"B is selected"','An inclusive disjunction with one false term requires the other term to be true.','Original conditional diagnosis exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000111','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the logical negation of “A or B or both is true”?','["A is true", "Neither A nor B is true", "Both A and B are true", "Exactly one is true"]','"Neither A nor B is true"','De Morgan negation of inclusive or makes both terms false.','Original logical negation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000112','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A secret code is an integer from 1 through 8. Exactly one statement is true: A says it is even; B says it is divisible by 3. How many possible codes satisfy this?','["4", "5", "7", "3"]','"4"','Count integers in exactly one of the two sets. The valid codes are 2, 3, 4, 8. Equivalently floor(8/2)+floor(8/3)−2floor(8/6)=4.','Original truth assignment exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000113','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Exactly four runners finish without ties. Mina finishes before Rui; Oren finishes after Rui; Paz finishes after Oren. Who finishes first?','["Rui", "Oren", "Paz", "Mina"]','"Mina"','The constraints force the order Mina → Rui → Oren → Paz; the first runner is Mina.','Original ordering constraints exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000114','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A knockout contest begins with 40 players. Every match eliminates exactly one player; no byes eliminate anyone. Matches needed for one champion?','["42", "38", "39", "40"]','"39"','Exactly 39 players must be eliminated; each match eliminates one.','Original tournament elimination exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000115','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','On a grid, go 8 steps east and 2 steps north in any order, with no other moves. How many distinct step sequences?','["44", "45", "46", "48"]','"45"','Choose the two north-step positions among 10: C(10,2)=45.','Original grid paths exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000116','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','8 different keys must be tried on one lock. Exactly one works; unsuccessful keys are never retried. Maximum trials needed to open it?','["8", "9", "11", "7"]','"8"','The working key can be last among all 8; identifying the last key is not opening the lock.','Original cycle permutation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000117','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A menu has 8 optional toppings; each can be included once or omitted independently. How many topping selections including none?','["257", "259", "255", "256"]','"256"','Each of 8 toppings has two states, so 2^8=256.','Original subset counts exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000118','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose a president and a different secretary from 8 candidates. How many assignments?','["59", "55", "56", "57"]','"56"','8 president choices times 7 secretary choices=56.','Original ordered arrangements exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000119','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 40 students, 13 study art, 11 study music, and 8 study both. How many study at least one?','["15", "16", "17", "19"]','"16"','Add the groups and subtract double-counted overlap: 13+11-8=16.','Original inclusion exclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000120','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A 12-hour clock shows 6:00. What hour number appears 21 hours later?','["3", "4", "6", "2"]','"3"','Add 21 hours and reduce modulo 12, labeling remainder zero as 12.','Original modular clock exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000121','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A board has 15 tiles, initially 6 black and the rest white. A move flips exactly two different tiles. After any number of moves, what is the largest reachable number of black tiles?','["15", "17", "13", "14"]','"14"','Each move preserves black-count parity. The largest count at most 15 with parity 0 is 14; flipping pairs of white tiles attains it.','Original parity invariant exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000122','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','An integer lies from 1 through 255. Each trial guesses one value and reports correct, too low, or too high. Minimum worst-case trials to identify it by a correct guess?','["11", "7", "8", "9"]','"8"','With 8 trials binary search covers at most 2^8-1 values, achieved by guessing midpoints.','Original bounded search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000123','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Two identical test sensors break above an unknown threshold in a 21-level building, and survive at or below it. The threshold is the highest safe floor, from 0 (all floors unsafe) through 21 (all floors safe). Broken sensors cannot be reused. Minimum worst-case drops to find the threshold?','["5", "6", "7", "9"]','"6"','6 drops cover a descending sum from 6 through 1, totaling 6×7/2=21 levels using decreasing gaps; 5 drops cover fewer.','Original two egg threshold exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000124','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the smallest positive integer greater than 48 that is divisible by both 2 and 3?','["54", "55", "57", "53"]','"54"','Common multiples are multiples of 6; the next after 48 is 54.','Original remainders exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000125','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A prize is in exactly one of boxes numbered 1 through 8. Exactly one speaker tells the truth. A says “the box number is even”; B says “the box number is odd”; C says “the box number is divisible by 3.” How many box numbers remain possible?','["7", "9", "5", "6"]','"6"','A and B are complementary, so exactly one already tells the truth. C must be false. Excluding 2 multiples of 3 leaves 8−2=6 possibilities.','Original exactly one speaker exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000101','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 6 times. Probability that every flip is heads?','["1/128", "65/128", "0", "1/64"]','"1/64"','Independence multiplies 6 factors of 1/2: 1/64.','Original all heads exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000102','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is flipped independently 7 times. Probability of at least one head?','["255/256", "0", "127/128", "127/256"]','"127/128"','Take the complement of all tails: 1−(1/2)^7.','Original at least one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000103','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 8 times. Probability of exactly one head?','["0", "1/32", "1/64", "33/64"]','"1/32"','Choose one head position in 8 ways, each sequence has probability 1/256.','Original exactly one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000104','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is tossed independently 9 times. Probability of exactly two heads?','["9/128", "9/256", "137/256", "0"]','"9/128"','C(9,2)=36 qualifying sequences out of 512.','Original binomial two successes exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000105','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag holds 8 red and 7 blue tokens. Every token is equally likely to be drawn once. Probability of red?','["4/15", "23/30", "0", "8/15"]','"8/15"','8 favorable tokens among 15 total.','Original one uniform draw exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000106','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two tokens uniformly without replacement from 8 red and 9 blue. Probability both are red?','["41/68", "0", "7/34", "7/68"]','"7/34"','Multiply 8/17 by 7/16.','Original without replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000107','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw twice uniformly with replacement from 8 red and 10 blue tokens. Probability both red?','["0", "16/81", "8/81", "97/162"]','"16/81"','Replacement makes independent draws with red probability 8/18; square it.','Original with replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000108','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two uniformly without replacement from 9 red and 11 blue tokens. Probability of one of each color?','["99/190", "99/380", "289/380", "0"]','"99/190"','Add red-blue and blue-red: 2×9×11/(20×19).','Original one of each color exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000109','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 48. Probability it is divisible by 3?','["1/6", "2/3", "0", "1/3"]','"1/3"','16 multiples of 3 among 48 integers.','Original uniform integer divisor exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000110','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 48. Probability it is divisible by 2 or 3?','["5/6", "0", "2/3", "1/3"]','"2/3"','24 multiples of 2 plus 16 multiples of 3 minus 8 multiples of 6 gives 32/48.','Original uniform integer union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000111','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Randomly order 8 distinct books, all orders equally likely. Probability a specified book is first?','["0", "1/8", "1/16", "9/16"]','"1/8"','Each book has equal probability of any of the 8 positions.','Original random permutation first exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000112','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Arrange 10 distinct people uniformly in a straight line. Probability two specified people stand next to each other?','["1/5", "1/10", "3/5", "0"]','"1/5"','Treat the pair as a block with two internal orders: 2×(9)!/(10)!=2/10.','Original permutation adjacency exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000113','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A committee of 2 is selected uniformly from 11 distinct people. Probability a specified person is selected?','["1/11", "13/22", "0", "2/11"]','"2/11"','The person belongs to 10 of C(11,2) committees, yielding 2/11.','Original subset inclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000114','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A lottery chooses one winning ticket uniformly from 160 tickets. You own 8 distinct tickets. Probability you win?','["21/40", "0", "1/20", "1/40"]','"1/20"','8 of 160 equally likely tickets are yours.','Original matching lottery exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000115','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/7 and 1/9. Probability both occur?','["0", "1/63", "1/126", "32/63"]','"1/63"','For independent events multiply 1/7 by 1/9.','Original independent intersections exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000116','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/8 and 1/10. Probability at least one occurs?','["17/80", "17/160", "97/160", "0"]','"17/80"','P(A or B)=P(A)+P(B)−P(A)P(B)=17/80.','Original independent union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000117','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A number is chosen uniformly from 1 through 32. Given it is even, probability it is also divisible by 4?','["1/4", "3/4", "0", "1/2"]','"1/2"','There are 16 even values and 8 multiples of 4 in the restricted sample.','Original conditional restricted sample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000118','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A group has 8 engineers and 3 artists. Choose 3 people uniformly without replacement. Probability all are engineers?','["221/330", "0", "56/165", "28/165"]','"56/165"','C(8,3)/C(11,3)=56/165.','Original hypergeometric committee exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000119','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent attempts succeed with probability 1/3 each. Probability the first success is on attempt 6?','["0", "32/729", "16/729", "761/1458"]','"32/729"','5 failures followed by success: (2/3)^5×(1/3).','Original geometric first success exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000120','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice, each labeled 1 through 8. Probability their values match?','["1/8", "1/16", "9/16", "0"]','"1/8"','8 matching ordered pairs among 64 equally likely pairs.','Original uniform dice equality exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000121','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 9. Probability the first value is strictly greater than the second?','["2/9", "13/18", "0", "4/9"]','"4/9"','Excluding 9 ties, symmetry halves the remaining 72 ordered pairs: 8/(18).','Original uniform dice ordering exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000122','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 10. Probability at least one shows 10?','["119/200", "0", "19/100", "19/200"]','"19/100"','Complement of neither showing the top face: 1−(9/10)².','Original dice maximum exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000123','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A code has 6 digits chosen independently uniformly from 0–9; leading zeros allowed. Probability all digits are nonzero? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["76.57%", "53.14%", "26.57%", "17.71%"]','"53.14%"','Each digit is nonzero with probability 9/10; multiply 6 factors. This is approximately 53.14%.','Original random digit code exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000124','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Pick one of two bags by fair coin. Bag A has 8 red and 1 blue token; bag B has 1 red and 8 blue. Draw uniformly. Given red, probability bag A was chosen?','["8/9", "4/9", "17/18", "0"]','"8/9"','Bayes: (1/2×8/9)/(1/2×8/9+1/2×1/9)=8/9.','Original Bayes selected bag exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000125','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','6 people have independent birthdays uniform over a fictional 80-day year. Probability all birthdays differ? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["41.27%", "27.51%", "91.27%", "82.53%"]','"82.53%"','Successive birthday choices: 80/80 × 79/80 × ... × 75/80. This is approximately 82.53%.','Original sampling distinct birthdays exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000101','ESTIMATE','For planning, a vehicle covers 136 km at a constant 95 km/h with no stops. Estimate travel time in minutes. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'86','Time = distance/speed × 60 = 136/95×60 minutes. Rounded result: 86.','Original travel time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000102','ESTIMATE','A car averages 9 liters per 100 km over a 248-km route. Estimate fuel needed in liters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'22.3','Fuel = 248×9/100 liters. Rounded result: 22.3.','Original fuel consumption exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000103','ESTIMATE','A rectangular floor is 8.5 m long and 6.25 m wide. Assuming no waste, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'53.1','Area = length×width = 8.5×6.25 m². Rounded result: 53.1.','Original area coverage exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000104','ESTIMATE','A wall area is 104 square meters. One liter covers 7 square meters per coat. For two coats, estimate liters of paint, ignoring waste. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'29.7','Paint volume = 104×2/7 liters. Rounded result: 29.7.','Original paint demand exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000105','ESTIMATE','A rectangular tank has internal dimensions 80 cm by 60 cm by 35 cm. Estimate capacity in liters; use 1 liter = 1,000 cubic cm. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'168.0','Capacity = 80×60×35/1000 liters. Rounded result: 168.0.','Original water tank exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000106','ESTIMATE','A tap delivers 10 liters per minute at a constant rate. Estimate minutes to fill an empty 216-liter tank. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'21.6','Time = volume/flow = 216/10 minutes. Rounded result: 21.6.','Original flow filling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000107','ESTIMATE','A 280-watt lamp runs for 7 hours. Estimate electricity used in kilowatt-hours. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'1.96','Energy = power×time = 280/1000×7 kWh. Rounded result: 1.96.','Original electric energy exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000108','ESTIMATE','An appliance uses 20.0 kWh. Electricity costs $0.23 per kWh with no fixed fees or taxes. Estimate cost in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'4.6','Cost = 20.0×0.23 dollars. Rounded result: 4.6.','Original energy cost exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000109','ESTIMATE','A walker takes 9872 steps, each 0.72 meters long. Estimate total distance in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'7.1','Distance = 9872×0.72/1000 km. Rounded result: 7.1.','Original walking distance exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000110','ESTIMATE','A sample of 100 identical-sized plots contains 56 trees. Assume the same density over 1,250 plots. Estimate total trees. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'700','Scale sample count by 1250/100: 56×12.5 trees. Rounded result: 700.','Original sample extrapolation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000111','ESTIMATE','A solid block has volume 10.4 cubic centimeters and density 2.7 grams per cubic centimeter. Estimate mass in grams. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'28.1','Mass = density×volume = 2.7×10.4 grams. Rounded result: 28.1.','Original density mass exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000112','ESTIMATE','An exchange model gives exactly 35.5 baht per US dollar, without fees. Estimate baht received for $136. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'4828','Baht = 136×35.5. Rounded result: 4828.','Original currency conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000113','ESTIMATE','A budget is $1080. Assume 16% goes to transport. Estimate transport spending in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'172.8','Transport = 1080×16/100 dollars. Rounded result: 172.8.','Original percentage budget exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000114','ESTIMATE','A model population starts at 8000 and grows exactly 5% each year for 6 years. Estimate its final population. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'10721','Compound growth: 8000×1.05^6. Rounded result: 10721.','Original compound growth exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000115','ESTIMATE','Equipment initially costs $9600 and retains 85% of its value after each year. Estimate its value after 6 years in dollars. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'3621','Value = 9600×0.85^6 dollars. Rounded result: 3621.','Original compound depreciation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000116','ESTIMATE','A cyclist rides 24 km outward at 12 km/h and the same distance back at 18 km/h. No stops. Estimate overall average speed in km/h. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'14.4','Total distance 48 km / total time (24/12+24/18) h = 14.4 km/h. Rounded result: 14.4.','Original average speed round trip exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000117','ESTIMATE','A class has 8 students scoring 70 and 6 students scoring 90. Estimate their combined mean score in points. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'78.6','Weighted mean = (8×70+6×90)/(8+6) points. Rounded result: 78.6.','Original weighted mean exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000118','ESTIMATE','A circular garden has radius 8.2 meters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'211.2','Area = pi×radius² = 3.1416×8.2² m². Rounded result: 211.2.','Original circular area exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000119','ESTIMATE','A wheel has radius 29.6 centimeters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its circumference in centimeters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'186.0','Circumference = 2×3.1416×29.6 cm. Rounded result: 186.0.','Original circular circumference exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000120','ESTIMATE','A map scale is 1:50,000. A measured route is 13.6 cm on the map. Estimate actual route length in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'6.8','Each map centimeter represents 50,000 cm = 0.5 km; distance = 13.6×0.5 km. Rounded result: 6.8.','Original map scaling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000121','ESTIMATE','A parcel weighs 18.4 pounds. Use exactly 1 pound = 0.45359237 kg. Estimate mass in kilograms. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'8.3','Mass = 18.4×0.45359237 kg. Rounded result: 8.3.','Original unit conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000122','ESTIMATE','A 1000-megabyte file downloads at exactly 40 megabits per second. Use 8 bits per byte and decimal units; ignore overhead. Estimate seconds needed. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'200','Time = file size×8/link rate = 1000×8/40 seconds. Rounded result: 200.','Original download time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000123','ESTIMATE','A drive offers exactly 80 decimal gigabytes. Each photo occupies exactly 4 decimal megabytes. Ignoring filesystem overhead, estimate how many whole photos fit; 1 GB = 1,000 MB. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'20000','Photo count = 80×1000/4; the result is an integer. Rounded result: 20000.','Original storage capacity exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000124','ESTIMATE','A factory makes 104 items per hour at a constant rate. Estimate items produced during 102 minutes, allowing fractional theoretical output before rounding. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'177','Output = 104×102/60 items. Rounded result: 177.','Original production throughput exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000125','ESTIMATE','A solution contains 20.0 grams of dissolved salt in a final volume of 8 liters. Estimate concentration in grams per liter. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'2.5','Concentration = dissolved mass/final volume = 20.0/8 g/L. Rounded result: 2.5.','Original dilution concentration exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000126','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A hidden token is in one of 512 numbered lockers. Each question may ask membership in any chosen subset and receives a truthful yes/no answer. Minimum worst-case questions?','["12", "8", "9", "10"]','"9"','9 bits distinguish 2^9=512 possibilities; successive halves attain this bound.','Original binary information exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000127','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 2187 coins exactly one is heavier; all genuine coins weigh equally. With a balance scale, minimum worst-case weighings?','["6", "7", "8", "10"]','"7"','Each weighing has three outcomes. Split into three equal groups repeatedly: 7 weighings distinguish 3^7=2187 coins.','Original balance ternary search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000128','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A drawer has unlimited socks in 9 colors. Picking blindly, how many socks guarantee a matching color pair?','["10", "11", "13", "9"]','"10"','9 picks could all have different colors; pick 10 repeats a color.','Original pigeonhole socks exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000129','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag has unlimited beads in 9 colors. How many blind picks guarantee three of one color?','["20", "22", "18", "19"]','"19"','At most two per color gives 18 without a triple; the next forces three.','Original pigeonhole triples exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000130','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A club of 12 members plays one game between every unordered pair. How many games?','["69", "65", "66", "67"]','"66"','Choose two members: (12)(11)/2=66.','Original pair counting exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000131','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fence has 11 posts in a straight row, spaced 7 meters apart. Distance from first post to last, in meters?','["69", "70", "71", "73"]','"70"','There are 10 gaps, each 7 meters: 70.','Original inclusive intervals exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000132','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Four cards show 7, 16, Blue and Green. Every card has an integer on one side and exactly one of those colors on the other. To test “if the integer is divisible by 3, the color is Blue,” which shown cards must be turned?','["Green", "Blue only", "All four", "Neither numbered card nor Green"]','"Green"','Turn every visible multiple of 3 to check Blue, and Green to exclude a multiple on its reverse. Blue and visible nonmultiples cannot falsify the rule.','Original conditional counterexample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000133','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Every amber bead is glass. Every glass bead is fragile. Which conclusion follows?','["Every fragile bead is amber", "No glass bead is amber", "All beads are fragile", "Every amber bead is fragile"]','"Every amber bead is fragile"','Subset containment is transitive: amber beads are glass beads and hence fragile.','Original syllogism exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000134','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','All members must wear badges. If someone wears a badge, the scanner records them. Tom is a member. What follows?','["Tom is not recorded", "Everyone recorded is a member", "The scanner records Tom", "Tom has no badge"]','"The scanner records Tom"','Membership implies a badge, and a badge implies a scanner record.','Original implication chains exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000135','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Exactly one of A and B is selected. A is selected. What follows?','["No conclusion follows", "B is not selected", "B is selected", "Neither is selected"]','"B is not selected"','Exclusive selection rules out selecting both.','Original conditional diagnosis exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000136','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the logical negation of “if A then B”?','["A is true and B is false", "A is false and B is true", "If B then A", "A and B are both false"]','"A is true and B is false"','Only a true antecedent and false consequent falsifies an implication.','Original logical negation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000137','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A secret code is an integer from 1 through 9. Exactly one statement is true: A says it is even; B says it is divisible by 3. How many possible codes satisfy this?','["6", "8", "4", "5"]','"5"','Count integers in exactly one of the two sets. The valid codes are 2, 3, 4, 8, 9. Equivalently floor(9/2)+floor(9/3)−2floor(9/6)=5.','Original truth assignment exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000138','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Exactly four runners finish without ties. Mina finishes before Rui; Paz finishes after Rui; Oren finishes after Paz. Who finishes second?','["Paz", "Oren", "Rui", "Mina"]','"Rui"','The constraints force the order Mina → Rui → Paz → Oren; the second runner is Rui.','Original ordering constraints exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000139','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A knockout contest begins with 45 players. Every match eliminates exactly one player; no byes eliminate anyone. Matches needed for one champion?','["43", "44", "45", "47"]','"44"','Exactly 44 players must be eliminated; each match eliminates one.','Original tournament elimination exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000140','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','On a grid, go 9 steps east and 2 steps north in any order, with no other moves. How many distinct step sequences?','["55", "56", "58", "54"]','"55"','Choose the two north-step positions among 11: C(11,2)=55.','Original grid paths exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000141','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','9 different keys must be tried on one lock. Exactly one works; unsuccessful keys are never retried. Maximum trials needed to open it?','["10", "12", "8", "9"]','"9"','The working key can be last among all 9; identifying the last key is not opening the lock.','Original cycle permutation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000142','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A menu has 9 optional toppings; each can be included once or omitted independently. How many topping selections including none?','["515", "511", "512", "513"]','"512"','Each of 9 toppings has two states, so 2^9=512.','Original subset counts exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000143','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose a president and a different secretary from 9 candidates. How many assignments?','["71", "72", "73", "75"]','"72"','9 president choices times 8 secretary choices=72.','Original ordered arrangements exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000144','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 45 students, 14 study art, 12 study music, and 9 study both. How many study at least one?','["17", "18", "20", "16"]','"17"','Add the groups and subtract double-counted overlap: 14+12-9=17.','Original inclusion exclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000145','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A 12-hour clock shows 7:00. What hour number appears 22 hours later?','["6", "8", "4", "5"]','"5"','Add 22 hours and reduce modulo 12, labeling remainder zero as 12.','Original modular clock exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000146','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A board has 16 tiles, initially 7 black and the rest white. A move flips exactly two different tiles. After any number of moves, what is the largest reachable number of black tiles?','["18", "14", "15", "16"]','"15"','Each move preserves black-count parity. The largest count at most 16 with parity 1 is 15; flipping pairs of white tiles attains it.','Original parity invariant exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000147','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','An integer lies from 1 through 511. Each trial guesses one value and reports correct, too low, or too high. Minimum worst-case trials to identify it by a correct guess?','["8", "9", "10", "12"]','"9"','With 9 trials binary search covers at most 2^9-1 values, achieved by guessing midpoints.','Original bounded search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000148','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Two identical test sensors break above an unknown threshold in a 28-level building, and survive at or below it. The threshold is the highest safe floor, from 0 (all floors unsafe) through 28 (all floors safe). Broken sensors cannot be reused. Minimum worst-case drops to find the threshold?','["7", "8", "10", "6"]','"7"','7 drops cover a descending sum from 7 through 1, totaling 7×8/2=28 levels using decreasing gaps; 6 drops cover fewer.','Original two egg threshold exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000149','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the smallest positive integer greater than 54 that is divisible by both 2 and 3?','["61", "63", "59", "60"]','"60"','Common multiples are multiples of 6; the next after 54 is 60.','Original remainders exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000150','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A prize is in exactly one of boxes numbered 1 through 9. Exactly one speaker tells the truth. A says “the box number is even”; B says “the box number is odd”; C says “the box number is divisible by 3.” How many box numbers remain possible?','["9", "5", "6", "7"]','"6"','A and B are complementary, so exactly one already tells the truth. C must be false. Excluding 3 multiples of 3 leaves 9−3=6 possibilities.','Original exactly one speaker exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000126','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 7 times. Probability that every flip is heads?','["129/256", "0", "1/128", "1/256"]','"1/128"','Independence multiplies 7 factors of 1/2: 1/128.','Original all heads exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000127','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is flipped independently 8 times. Probability of at least one head?','["0", "255/256", "255/512", "511/512"]','"255/256"','Take the complement of all tails: 1−(1/2)^8.','Original at least one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000128','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 9 times. Probability of exactly one head?','["9/512", "9/1024", "521/1024", "0"]','"9/512"','Choose one head position in 9 ways, each sequence has probability 1/512.','Original exactly one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000129','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is tossed independently 10 times. Probability of exactly two heads?','["45/2048", "1069/2048", "0", "45/1024"]','"45/1024"','C(10,2)=45 qualifying sequences out of 1024.','Original binomial two successes exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000130','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag holds 9 red and 8 blue tokens. Every token is equally likely to be drawn once. Probability of red?','["13/17", "0", "9/17", "9/34"]','"9/17"','9 favorable tokens among 17 total.','Original one uniform draw exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000131','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two tokens uniformly without replacement from 9 red and 10 blue. Probability both are red?','["0", "4/19", "2/19", "23/38"]','"4/19"','Multiply 9/19 by 8/18.','Original without replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000132','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw twice uniformly with replacement from 9 red and 11 blue tokens. Probability both red?','["81/400", "81/800", "481/800", "0"]','"81/400"','Replacement makes independent draws with red probability 9/20; square it.','Original with replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000133','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two uniformly without replacement from 10 red and 12 blue tokens. Probability of one of each color?','["20/77", "117/154", "0", "40/77"]','"40/77"','Add red-blue and blue-red: 2×10×12/(22×21).','Original one of each color exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000134','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 54. Probability it is divisible by 3?','["2/3", "0", "1/3", "1/6"]','"1/3"','18 multiples of 3 among 54 integers.','Original uniform integer divisor exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000135','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 54. Probability it is divisible by 2 or 3?','["0", "2/3", "1/3", "5/6"]','"2/3"','27 multiples of 2 plus 18 multiples of 3 minus 9 multiples of 6 gives 36/54.','Original uniform integer union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000136','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Randomly order 9 distinct books, all orders equally likely. Probability a specified book is first?','["1/9", "1/18", "5/9", "0"]','"1/9"','Each book has equal probability of any of the 9 positions.','Original random permutation first exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000137','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Arrange 11 distinct people uniformly in a straight line. Probability two specified people stand next to each other?','["1/11", "13/22", "0", "2/11"]','"2/11"','Treat the pair as a block with two internal orders: 2×(10)!/(11)!=2/11.','Original permutation adjacency exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000138','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A committee of 2 is selected uniformly from 12 distinct people. Probability a specified person is selected?','["7/12", "0", "1/6", "1/12"]','"1/6"','The person belongs to 11 of C(12,2) committees, yielding 2/12.','Original subset inclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000139','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A lottery chooses one winning ticket uniformly from 180 tickets. You own 9 distinct tickets. Probability you win?','["0", "1/20", "1/40", "21/40"]','"1/20"','9 of 180 equally likely tickets are yours.','Original matching lottery exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000140','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/8 and 1/10. Probability both occur?','["1/80", "1/160", "81/160", "0"]','"1/80"','For independent events multiply 1/8 by 1/10.','Original independent intersections exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000141','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/9 and 1/11. Probability at least one occurs?','["19/198", "59/99", "0", "19/99"]','"19/99"','P(A or B)=P(A)+P(B)−P(A)P(B)=19/99.','Original independent union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000142','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A number is chosen uniformly from 1 through 36. Given it is even, probability it is also divisible by 4?','["3/4", "0", "1/2", "1/4"]','"1/2"','There are 18 even values and 9 multiples of 4 in the restricted sample.','Original conditional restricted sample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000143','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A group has 9 engineers and 3 artists. Choose 3 people uniformly without replacement. Probability all are engineers?','["0", "21/55", "21/110", "38/55"]','"21/55"','C(9,3)/C(12,3)=84/220.','Original hypergeometric committee exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000144','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent attempts succeed with probability 1/3 each. Probability the first success is on attempt 7?','["64/2187", "32/2187", "2251/4374", "0"]','"64/2187"','6 failures followed by success: (2/3)^6×(1/3).','Original geometric first success exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000145','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice, each labeled 1 through 9. Probability their values match?','["1/18", "5/9", "0", "1/9"]','"1/9"','9 matching ordered pairs among 81 equally likely pairs.','Original uniform dice equality exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000146','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 10. Probability the first value is strictly greater than the second?','["29/40", "0", "9/20", "9/40"]','"9/20"','Excluding 10 ties, symmetry halves the remaining 90 ordered pairs: 9/(20).','Original uniform dice ordering exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000147','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 11. Probability at least one shows 11?','["0", "21/121", "21/242", "71/121"]','"21/121"','Complement of neither showing the top face: 1−(10/11)².','Original dice maximum exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000148','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A code has 7 digits chosen independently uniformly from 0–9; leading zeros allowed. Probability all digits are nonzero? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["47.83%", "23.91%", "15.94%", "73.91%"]','"47.83%"','Each digit is nonzero with probability 9/10; multiply 7 factors. This is approximately 47.83%.','Original random digit code exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000149','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Pick one of two bags by fair coin. Bag A has 9 red and 1 blue token; bag B has 1 red and 9 blue. Draw uniformly. Given red, probability bag A was chosen?','["9/20", "19/20", "0", "9/10"]','"9/10"','Bayes: (1/2×9/10)/(1/2×9/10+1/2×1/10)=9/10.','Original Bayes selected bag exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000150','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','7 people have independent birthdays uniform over a fictional 90-day year. Probability all birthdays differ? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["26.24%", "89.36%", "78.73%", "39.36%"]','"78.73%"','Successive birthday choices: 90/90 × 89/90 × ... × 84/90. This is approximately 78.73%.','Original sampling distinct birthdays exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000126','ESTIMATE','For planning, a vehicle covers 153 km at a constant 105 km/h with no stops. Estimate travel time in minutes. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'87','Time = distance/speed × 60 = 153/105×60 minutes. Rounded result: 87.','Original travel time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000127','ESTIMATE','A car averages 10 liters per 100 km over a 279-km route. Estimate fuel needed in liters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'27.9','Fuel = 279×10/100 liters. Rounded result: 27.9.','Original fuel consumption exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000128','ESTIMATE','A rectangular floor is 9.5 m long and 7.25 m wide. Assuming no waste, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'68.9','Area = length×width = 9.5×7.25 m². Rounded result: 68.9.','Original area coverage exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000129','ESTIMATE','A wall area is 117 square meters. One liter covers 7 square meters per coat. For two coats, estimate liters of paint, ignoring waste. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'33.4','Paint volume = 117×2/7 liters. Rounded result: 33.4.','Original paint demand exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000130','ESTIMATE','A rectangular tank has internal dimensions 90 cm by 70 cm by 35 cm. Estimate capacity in liters; use 1 liter = 1,000 cubic cm. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'220.5','Capacity = 90×70×35/1000 liters. Rounded result: 220.5.','Original water tank exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000131','ESTIMATE','A tap delivers 11 liters per minute at a constant rate. Estimate minutes to fill an empty 243-liter tank. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'22.1','Time = volume/flow = 243/11 minutes. Rounded result: 22.1.','Original flow filling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000132','ESTIMATE','A 315-watt lamp runs for 8 hours. Estimate electricity used in kilowatt-hours. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'2.52','Energy = power×time = 315/1000×8 kWh. Rounded result: 2.52.','Original electric energy exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000133','ESTIMATE','An appliance uses 22.5 kWh. Electricity costs $0.23 per kWh with no fixed fees or taxes. Estimate cost in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'5.18','Cost = 22.5×0.23 dollars. Rounded result: 5.18.','Original energy cost exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000134','ESTIMATE','A walker takes 11106 steps, each 0.72 meters long. Estimate total distance in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'8.0','Distance = 11106×0.72/1000 km. Rounded result: 8.0.','Original walking distance exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000135','ESTIMATE','A sample of 100 identical-sized plots contains 63 trees. Assume the same density over 1,250 plots. Estimate total trees. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'788','Scale sample count by 1250/100: 63×12.5 trees. Rounded result: 788.','Original sample extrapolation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000136','ESTIMATE','A solid block has volume 11.7 cubic centimeters and density 2.7 grams per cubic centimeter. Estimate mass in grams. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'31.6','Mass = density×volume = 2.7×11.7 grams. Rounded result: 31.6.','Original density mass exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000137','ESTIMATE','An exchange model gives exactly 35.5 baht per US dollar, without fees. Estimate baht received for $153. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'5432','Baht = 153×35.5. Rounded result: 5432.','Original currency conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000138','ESTIMATE','A budget is $1215. Assume 17% goes to transport. Estimate transport spending in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'206.55','Transport = 1215×17/100 dollars. Rounded result: 206.55.','Original percentage budget exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000139','ESTIMATE','A model population starts at 9000 and grows exactly 5% each year for 7 years. Estimate its final population. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'12664','Compound growth: 9000×1.05^7. Rounded result: 12664.','Original compound growth exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000140','ESTIMATE','Equipment initially costs $10800 and retains 85% of its value after each year. Estimate its value after 7 years in dollars. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'3462','Value = 10800×0.85^7 dollars. Rounded result: 3462.','Original compound depreciation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000141','ESTIMATE','A cyclist rides 27 km outward at 12 km/h and the same distance back at 18 km/h. No stops. Estimate overall average speed in km/h. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'14.4','Total distance 54 km / total time (27/12+27/18) h = 14.4 km/h. Rounded result: 14.4.','Original average speed round trip exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000142','ESTIMATE','A class has 9 students scoring 70 and 7 students scoring 90. Estimate their combined mean score in points. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'78.8','Weighted mean = (9×70+7×90)/(9+7) points. Rounded result: 78.8.','Original weighted mean exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000143','ESTIMATE','A circular garden has radius 9.2 meters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'265.9','Area = pi×radius² = 3.1416×9.2² m². Rounded result: 265.9.','Original circular area exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000144','ESTIMATE','A wheel has radius 33.3 centimeters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its circumference in centimeters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'209.2','Circumference = 2×3.1416×33.3 cm. Rounded result: 209.2.','Original circular circumference exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000145','ESTIMATE','A map scale is 1:50,000. A measured route is 15.3 cm on the map. Estimate actual route length in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'7.7','Each map centimeter represents 50,000 cm = 0.5 km; distance = 15.3×0.5 km. Rounded result: 7.7.','Original map scaling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000146','ESTIMATE','A parcel weighs 20.7 pounds. Use exactly 1 pound = 0.45359237 kg. Estimate mass in kilograms. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'9.4','Mass = 20.7×0.45359237 kg. Rounded result: 9.4.','Original unit conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000147','ESTIMATE','A 1125-megabyte file downloads at exactly 45 megabits per second. Use 8 bits per byte and decimal units; ignore overhead. Estimate seconds needed. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'200','Time = file size×8/link rate = 1125×8/45 seconds. Rounded result: 200.','Original download time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000148','ESTIMATE','A drive offers exactly 90 decimal gigabytes. Each photo occupies exactly 4 decimal megabytes. Ignoring filesystem overhead, estimate how many whole photos fit; 1 GB = 1,000 MB. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'22500','Photo count = 90×1000/4; the result is an integer. Rounded result: 22500.','Original storage capacity exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000149','ESTIMATE','A factory makes 117 items per hour at a constant rate. Estimate items produced during 119 minutes, allowing fractional theoretical output before rounding. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'232','Output = 117×119/60 items. Rounded result: 232.','Original production throughput exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000150','ESTIMATE','A solution contains 22.5 grams of dissolved salt in a final volume of 9 liters. Estimate concentration in grams per liter. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'2.5','Concentration = dissolved mass/final volume = 22.5/9 g/L. Rounded result: 2.5.','Original dilution concentration exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000151','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A hidden token is in one of 1024 numbered lockers. Each question may ask membership in any chosen subset and receives a truthful yes/no answer. Minimum worst-case questions?','["9", "10", "11", "13"]','"10"','10 bits distinguish 2^10=1024 possibilities; successive halves attain this bound.','Original binary information exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000152','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 6561 coins exactly one is heavier; all genuine coins weigh equally. With a balance scale, minimum worst-case weighings?','["8", "9", "11", "7"]','"8"','Each weighing has three outcomes. Split into three equal groups repeatedly: 8 weighings distinguish 3^8=6561 coins.','Original balance ternary search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000153','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A drawer has unlimited socks in 10 colors. Picking blindly, how many socks guarantee a matching color pair?','["12", "14", "10", "11"]','"11"','10 picks could all have different colors; pick 11 repeats a color.','Original pigeonhole socks exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000154','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag has unlimited beads in 10 colors. How many blind picks guarantee three of one color?','["24", "20", "21", "22"]','"21"','At most two per color gives 20 without a triple; the next forces three.','Original pigeonhole triples exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000155','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A club of 13 members plays one game between every unordered pair. How many games?','["77", "78", "79", "81"]','"78"','Choose two members: (13)(12)/2=78.','Original pair counting exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000156','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fence has 12 posts in a straight row, spaced 8 meters apart. Distance from first post to last, in meters?','["88", "89", "91", "87"]','"88"','There are 11 gaps, each 8 meters: 88.','Original inclusive intervals exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000157','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Four cards show 8, 18, Blue and Green. Every card has an integer on one side and exactly one of those colors on the other. To test “if the integer is divisible by 4, the color is Blue,” which shown cards must be turned?','["Blue only", "All four", "Neither numbered card nor Green", "8 and Green"]','"8 and Green"','Turn every visible multiple of 4 to check Blue, and Green to exclude a multiple on its reverse. Blue and visible nonmultiples cannot falsify the rule.','Original conditional counterexample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000158','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','No reptile is a mammal. Some pets are reptiles. What follows?','["All pets are reptiles", "Some reptiles are mammals", "Some pets are not mammals", "No pet is a mammal"]','"Some pets are not mammals"','The pet reptiles are witnesses to pets outside the mammal class.','Original syllogism exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000159','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','If it rains, the path is wet. If the path is wet, the sign is raised. It is raining. What follows?','["Every raised sign means rain", "The sign is raised", "The sign is lowered", "The path is dry"]','"The sign is raised"','Forward implication through both rules yields the raised sign.','Original implication chains exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000160','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','If A is true, B is true. If B is true, A is true. B is false. What follows?','["A is false", "A is true", "Both are true", "No conclusion follows"]','"A is false"','The first rule forbids true A with false B.','Original conditional diagnosis exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000161','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A finite set has exactly three members. What negates that statement?','["Its size is four", "Its size is less than three", "Its size is positive", "Its size is not three"]','"Its size is not three"','The complement includes zero, one, two, and every size above three.','Original logical negation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000162','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A secret code is an integer from 1 through 10. Exactly one statement is true: A says it is even; B says it is divisible by 3. How many possible codes satisfy this?','["9", "5", "6", "7"]','"6"','Count integers in exactly one of the two sets. The valid codes are 2, 3, 4, 8, 9, 10. Equivalently floor(10/2)+floor(10/3)−2floor(10/6)=6.','Original truth assignment exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000163','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Exactly four runners finish without ties. Oren finishes before Mina; Paz finishes after Mina; Rui finishes after Paz. Who finishes third?','["Rui", "Paz", "Oren", "Mina"]','"Paz"','The constraints force the order Oren → Mina → Paz → Rui; the third runner is Paz.','Original ordering constraints exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000164','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A knockout contest begins with 50 players. Every match eliminates exactly one player; no byes eliminate anyone. Matches needed for one champion?','["49", "50", "52", "48"]','"49"','Exactly 49 players must be eliminated; each match eliminates one.','Original tournament elimination exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000165','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','On a grid, go 10 steps east and 2 steps north in any order, with no other moves. How many distinct step sequences?','["67", "69", "65", "66"]','"66"','Choose the two north-step positions among 12: C(12,2)=66.','Original grid paths exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000166','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','10 different keys must be tried on one lock. Exactly one works; unsuccessful keys are never retried. Maximum trials needed to open it?','["13", "9", "10", "11"]','"10"','The working key can be last among all 10; identifying the last key is not opening the lock.','Original cycle permutation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000167','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A menu has 10 optional toppings; each can be included once or omitted independently. How many topping selections including none?','["1023", "1024", "1025", "1027"]','"1024"','Each of 10 toppings has two states, so 2^10=1024.','Original subset counts exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000168','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose a president and a different secretary from 10 candidates. How many assignments?','["90", "91", "93", "89"]','"90"','10 president choices times 9 secretary choices=90.','Original ordered arrangements exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000169','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 50 students, 15 study art, 13 study music, and 10 study both. How many study at least one?','["19", "21", "17", "18"]','"18"','Add the groups and subtract double-counted overlap: 15+13-10=18.','Original inclusion exclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000170','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A 12-hour clock shows 8:00. What hour number appears 23 hours later?','["10", "6", "7", "8"]','"7"','Add 23 hours and reduce modulo 12, labeling remainder zero as 12.','Original modular clock exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000171','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A board has 17 tiles, initially 8 black and the rest white. A move flips exactly two different tiles. After any number of moves, what is the largest reachable number of black tiles?','["15", "16", "17", "19"]','"16"','Each move preserves black-count parity. The largest count at most 17 with parity 0 is 16; flipping pairs of white tiles attains it.','Original parity invariant exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000172','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','An integer lies from 1 through 1023. Each trial guesses one value and reports correct, too low, or too high. Minimum worst-case trials to identify it by a correct guess?','["10", "11", "13", "9"]','"10"','With 10 trials binary search covers at most 2^10-1 values, achieved by guessing midpoints.','Original bounded search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000173','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Two identical test sensors break above an unknown threshold in a 36-level building, and survive at or below it. The threshold is the highest safe floor, from 0 (all floors unsafe) through 36 (all floors safe). Broken sensors cannot be reused. Minimum worst-case drops to find the threshold?','["9", "11", "7", "8"]','"8"','8 drops cover a descending sum from 8 through 1, totaling 8×9/2=36 levels using decreasing gaps; 7 drops cover fewer.','Original two egg threshold exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000174','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the smallest positive integer greater than 60 that is divisible by both 2 and 3?','["69", "65", "66", "67"]','"66"','Common multiples are multiples of 6; the next after 60 is 66.','Original remainders exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000175','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A prize is in exactly one of boxes numbered 1 through 10. Exactly one speaker tells the truth. A says “the box number is even”; B says “the box number is odd”; C says “the box number is divisible by 3.” How many box numbers remain possible?','["6", "7", "8", "10"]','"7"','A and B are complementary, so exactly one already tells the truth. C must be false. Excluding 3 multiples of 3 leaves 10−3=7 possibilities.','Original exactly one speaker exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000151','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 8 times. Probability that every flip is heads?','["0", "1/256", "1/512", "257/512"]','"1/256"','Independence multiplies 8 factors of 1/2: 1/256.','Original all heads exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000152','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is flipped independently 9 times. Probability of at least one head?','["511/512", "511/1024", "1023/1024", "0"]','"511/512"','Take the complement of all tails: 1−(1/2)^9.','Original at least one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000153','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 10 times. Probability of exactly one head?','["5/1024", "517/1024", "0", "5/512"]','"5/512"','Choose one head position in 10 ways, each sequence has probability 1/1024.','Original exactly one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000154','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is tossed independently 11 times. Probability of exactly two heads?','["2103/4096", "0", "55/2048", "55/4096"]','"55/2048"','C(11,2)=55 qualifying sequences out of 2048.','Original binomial two successes exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000155','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag holds 10 red and 9 blue tokens. Every token is equally likely to be drawn once. Probability of red?','["0", "10/19", "5/19", "29/38"]','"10/19"','10 favorable tokens among 19 total.','Original one uniform draw exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000156','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two tokens uniformly without replacement from 10 red and 11 blue. Probability both are red?','["3/14", "3/28", "17/28", "0"]','"3/14"','Multiply 10/21 by 9/20.','Original without replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000157','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw twice uniformly with replacement from 10 red and 12 blue tokens. Probability both red?','["25/242", "73/121", "0", "25/121"]','"25/121"','Replacement makes independent draws with red probability 10/22; square it.','Original with replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000158','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two uniformly without replacement from 11 red and 13 blue tokens. Probability of one of each color?','["419/552", "0", "143/276", "143/552"]','"143/276"','Add red-blue and blue-red: 2×11×13/(24×23).','Original one of each color exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000159','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 60. Probability it is divisible by 3?','["0", "1/3", "1/6", "2/3"]','"1/3"','20 multiples of 3 among 60 integers.','Original uniform integer divisor exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000160','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 60. Probability it is divisible by 2 or 3?','["2/3", "1/3", "5/6", "0"]','"2/3"','30 multiples of 2 plus 20 multiples of 3 minus 10 multiples of 6 gives 40/60.','Original uniform integer union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000161','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Randomly order 10 distinct books, all orders equally likely. Probability a specified book is first?','["1/20", "11/20", "0", "1/10"]','"1/10"','Each book has equal probability of any of the 10 positions.','Original random permutation first exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000162','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Arrange 12 distinct people uniformly in a straight line. Probability two specified people stand next to each other?','["7/12", "0", "1/6", "1/12"]','"1/6"','Treat the pair as a block with two internal orders: 2×(11)!/(12)!=2/12.','Original permutation adjacency exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000163','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A committee of 2 is selected uniformly from 13 distinct people. Probability a specified person is selected?','["0", "2/13", "1/13", "15/26"]','"2/13"','The person belongs to 12 of C(13,2) committees, yielding 2/13.','Original subset inclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000164','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A lottery chooses one winning ticket uniformly from 200 tickets. You own 10 distinct tickets. Probability you win?','["1/20", "1/40", "21/40", "0"]','"1/20"','10 of 200 equally likely tickets are yours.','Original matching lottery exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000165','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/9 and 1/11. Probability both occur?','["1/198", "50/99", "0", "1/99"]','"1/99"','For independent events multiply 1/9 by 1/11.','Original independent intersections exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000166','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/10 and 1/12. Probability at least one occurs?','["47/80", "0", "7/40", "7/80"]','"7/40"','P(A or B)=P(A)+P(B)−P(A)P(B)=7/40.','Original independent union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000167','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A number is chosen uniformly from 1 through 40. Given it is even, probability it is also divisible by 4?','["0", "1/2", "1/4", "3/4"]','"1/2"','There are 20 even values and 10 multiples of 4 in the restricted sample.','Original conditional restricted sample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000168','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A group has 10 engineers and 3 artists. Choose 3 people uniformly without replacement. Probability all are engineers?','["60/143", "30/143", "203/286", "0"]','"60/143"','C(10,3)/C(13,3)=120/286.','Original hypergeometric committee exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000169','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent attempts succeed with probability 1/3 each. Probability the first success is on attempt 8?','["64/6561", "6689/13122", "0", "128/6561"]','"128/6561"','7 failures followed by success: (2/3)^7×(1/3).','Original geometric first success exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000170','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice, each labeled 1 through 10. Probability their values match?','["11/20", "0", "1/10", "1/20"]','"1/10"','10 matching ordered pairs among 100 equally likely pairs.','Original uniform dice equality exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000171','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 11. Probability the first value is strictly greater than the second?','["0", "5/11", "5/22", "8/11"]','"5/11"','Excluding 11 ties, symmetry halves the remaining 110 ordered pairs: 10/(22).','Original uniform dice ordering exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000172','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 12. Probability at least one shows 12?','["23/144", "23/288", "167/288", "0"]','"23/144"','Complement of neither showing the top face: 1−(11/12)².','Original dice maximum exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000173','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A code has 8 digits chosen independently uniformly from 0–9; leading zeros allowed. Probability all digits are nonzero? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["21.52%", "14.35%", "71.52%", "43.05%"]','"43.05%"','Each digit is nonzero with probability 9/10; multiply 8 factors. This is approximately 43.05%.','Original random digit code exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000174','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Pick one of two bags by fair coin. Bag A has 10 red and 1 blue token; bag B has 1 red and 10 blue. Draw uniformly. Given red, probability bag A was chosen?','["21/22", "0", "10/11", "5/11"]','"10/11"','Bayes: (1/2×10/11)/(1/2×10/11+1/2×1/11)=10/11.','Original Bayes selected bag exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000175','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','8 people have independent birthdays uniform over a fictional 100-day year. Probability all birthdays differ? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["87.52%", "75.03%", "37.52%", "25.01%"]','"75.03%"','Successive birthday choices: 100/100 × 99/100 × ... × 93/100. This is approximately 75.03%.','Original sampling distinct birthdays exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000151','ESTIMATE','For planning, a vehicle covers 170 km at a constant 115 km/h with no stops. Estimate travel time in minutes. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'89','Time = distance/speed × 60 = 170/115×60 minutes. Rounded result: 89.','Original travel time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000152','ESTIMATE','A car averages 11 liters per 100 km over a 310-km route. Estimate fuel needed in liters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'34.1','Fuel = 310×11/100 liters. Rounded result: 34.1.','Original fuel consumption exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000153','ESTIMATE','A rectangular floor is 10.5 m long and 8.25 m wide. Assuming no waste, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'86.6','Area = length×width = 10.5×8.25 m². Rounded result: 86.6.','Original area coverage exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000154','ESTIMATE','A wall area is 130 square meters. One liter covers 7 square meters per coat. For two coats, estimate liters of paint, ignoring waste. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'37.1','Paint volume = 130×2/7 liters. Rounded result: 37.1.','Original paint demand exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000155','ESTIMATE','A rectangular tank has internal dimensions 100 cm by 80 cm by 35 cm. Estimate capacity in liters; use 1 liter = 1,000 cubic cm. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'280.0','Capacity = 100×80×35/1000 liters. Rounded result: 280.0.','Original water tank exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000156','ESTIMATE','A tap delivers 12 liters per minute at a constant rate. Estimate minutes to fill an empty 270-liter tank. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'22.5','Time = volume/flow = 270/12 minutes. Rounded result: 22.5.','Original flow filling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000157','ESTIMATE','A 350-watt lamp runs for 9 hours. Estimate electricity used in kilowatt-hours. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'3.15','Energy = power×time = 350/1000×9 kWh. Rounded result: 3.15.','Original electric energy exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000158','ESTIMATE','An appliance uses 25.0 kWh. Electricity costs $0.23 per kWh with no fixed fees or taxes. Estimate cost in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'5.75','Cost = 25.0×0.23 dollars. Rounded result: 5.75.','Original energy cost exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000159','ESTIMATE','A walker takes 12340 steps, each 0.72 meters long. Estimate total distance in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'8.9','Distance = 12340×0.72/1000 km. Rounded result: 8.9.','Original walking distance exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000160','ESTIMATE','A sample of 100 identical-sized plots contains 70 trees. Assume the same density over 1,250 plots. Estimate total trees. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'875','Scale sample count by 1250/100: 70×12.5 trees. Rounded result: 875.','Original sample extrapolation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000161','ESTIMATE','A solid block has volume 13.0 cubic centimeters and density 2.7 grams per cubic centimeter. Estimate mass in grams. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'35.1','Mass = density×volume = 2.7×13.0 grams. Rounded result: 35.1.','Original density mass exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000162','ESTIMATE','An exchange model gives exactly 35.5 baht per US dollar, without fees. Estimate baht received for $170. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'6035','Baht = 170×35.5. Rounded result: 6035.','Original currency conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000163','ESTIMATE','A budget is $1350. Assume 18% goes to transport. Estimate transport spending in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'243.0','Transport = 1350×18/100 dollars. Rounded result: 243.0.','Original percentage budget exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000164','ESTIMATE','A model population starts at 10000 and grows exactly 5% each year for 8 years. Estimate its final population. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'14775','Compound growth: 10000×1.05^8. Rounded result: 14775.','Original compound growth exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000165','ESTIMATE','Equipment initially costs $12000 and retains 85% of its value after each year. Estimate its value after 8 years in dollars. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'3270','Value = 12000×0.85^8 dollars. Rounded result: 3270.','Original compound depreciation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000166','ESTIMATE','A cyclist rides 30 km outward at 12 km/h and the same distance back at 18 km/h. No stops. Estimate overall average speed in km/h. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'14.4','Total distance 60 km / total time (30/12+30/18) h = 14.4 km/h. Rounded result: 14.4.','Original average speed round trip exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000167','ESTIMATE','A class has 10 students scoring 70 and 8 students scoring 90. Estimate their combined mean score in points. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'78.9','Weighted mean = (10×70+8×90)/(10+8) points. Rounded result: 78.9.','Original weighted mean exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000168','ESTIMATE','A circular garden has radius 10.2 meters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'326.9','Area = pi×radius² = 3.1416×10.2² m². Rounded result: 326.9.','Original circular area exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000169','ESTIMATE','A wheel has radius 37.0 centimeters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its circumference in centimeters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'232.5','Circumference = 2×3.1416×37.0 cm. Rounded result: 232.5.','Original circular circumference exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000170','ESTIMATE','A map scale is 1:50,000. A measured route is 17.0 cm on the map. Estimate actual route length in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'8.5','Each map centimeter represents 50,000 cm = 0.5 km; distance = 17.0×0.5 km. Rounded result: 8.5.','Original map scaling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000171','ESTIMATE','A parcel weighs 23.0 pounds. Use exactly 1 pound = 0.45359237 kg. Estimate mass in kilograms. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'10.4','Mass = 23.0×0.45359237 kg. Rounded result: 10.4.','Original unit conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000172','ESTIMATE','A 1250-megabyte file downloads at exactly 50 megabits per second. Use 8 bits per byte and decimal units; ignore overhead. Estimate seconds needed. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'200','Time = file size×8/link rate = 1250×8/50 seconds. Rounded result: 200.','Original download time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000173','ESTIMATE','A drive offers exactly 100 decimal gigabytes. Each photo occupies exactly 4 decimal megabytes. Ignoring filesystem overhead, estimate how many whole photos fit; 1 GB = 1,000 MB. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'25000','Photo count = 100×1000/4; the result is an integer. Rounded result: 25000.','Original storage capacity exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000174','ESTIMATE','A factory makes 130 items per hour at a constant rate. Estimate items produced during 136 minutes, allowing fractional theoretical output before rounding. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'295','Output = 130×136/60 items. Rounded result: 295.','Original production throughput exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000175','ESTIMATE','A solution contains 25.0 grams of dissolved salt in a final volume of 10 liters. Estimate concentration in grams per liter. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'2.5','Concentration = dissolved mass/final volume = 25.0/10 g/L. Rounded result: 2.5.','Original dilution concentration exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000176','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A hidden token is in one of 2048 numbered lockers. Each question may ask membership in any chosen subset and receives a truthful yes/no answer. Minimum worst-case questions?','["11", "12", "14", "10"]','"11"','11 bits distinguish 2^11=2048 possibilities; successive halves attain this bound.','Original binary information exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000177','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 19683 coins exactly one is heavier; all genuine coins weigh equally. With a balance scale, minimum worst-case weighings?','["10", "12", "8", "9"]','"9"','Each weighing has three outcomes. Split into three equal groups repeatedly: 9 weighings distinguish 3^9=19683 coins.','Original balance ternary search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000178','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A drawer has unlimited socks in 11 colors. Picking blindly, how many socks guarantee a matching color pair?','["15", "11", "12", "13"]','"12"','11 picks could all have different colors; pick 12 repeats a color.','Original pigeonhole socks exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000179','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag has unlimited beads in 11 colors. How many blind picks guarantee three of one color?','["22", "23", "24", "26"]','"23"','At most two per color gives 22 without a triple; the next forces three.','Original pigeonhole triples exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000180','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A club of 14 members plays one game between every unordered pair. How many games?','["91", "92", "94", "90"]','"91"','Choose two members: (14)(13)/2=91.','Original pair counting exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000181','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fence has 13 posts in a straight row, spaced 9 meters apart. Distance from first post to last, in meters?','["109", "111", "107", "108"]','"108"','There are 12 gaps, each 9 meters: 108.','Original inclusive intervals exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000182','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Four cards show 9, 20, Blue and Green. Every card has an integer on one side and exactly one of those colors on the other. To test “if the integer is divisible by 5, the color is Blue,” which shown cards must be turned?','["All four", "Neither numbered card nor Green", "20 and Green", "Blue only"]','"20 and Green"','Turn every visible multiple of 5 to check Blue, and Green to exclude a multiple on its reverse. Blue and visible nonmultiples cannot falsify the rule.','Original conditional counterexample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000183','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','All maps in a drawer are folded. No folded item is framed. A framed map lies on a desk. What follows?','["All desk items are framed", "That map is not in the drawer", "The drawer is empty", "The map is folded"]','"That map is not in the drawer"','A drawer map would be folded and hence unframed, contradicting its framed status.','Original syllogism exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000184','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','If a number is divisible by 4, it is even. An integer is odd. What follows?','["It is not divisible by 4", "It is divisible by 4", "It is prime", "It is divisible by 3"]','"It is not divisible by 4"','Odd means not even, which rules out a multiple of 4.','Original implication chains exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000185','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A and B cannot both be true. A is true. What follows?','["B is true", "A is false", "Both are false", "B is false"]','"B is false"','The no-both condition rules out B when A holds.','Original conditional diagnosis exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000186','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the logical negation of “no coin is silver”?','["Some coins are not silver", "Exactly two coins are silver", "At least one coin is silver", "All coins are silver"]','"At least one coin is silver"','Negating a universal absence asserts an existing example.','Original logical negation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000187','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A secret code is an integer from 1 through 11. Exactly one statement is true: A says it is even; B says it is divisible by 3. How many possible codes satisfy this?','["5", "6", "7", "9"]','"6"','Count integers in exactly one of the two sets. The valid codes are 2, 3, 4, 8, 9, 10. Equivalently floor(11/2)+floor(11/3)−2floor(11/6)=6.','Original truth assignment exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000188','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Exactly four runners finish without ties. Oren finishes before Mina; Rui finishes after Mina; Paz finishes after Rui. Who finishes fourth?','["Paz", "Oren", "Mina", "Rui"]','"Paz"','The constraints force the order Oren → Mina → Rui → Paz; the fourth runner is Paz.','Original ordering constraints exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000189','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A knockout contest begins with 55 players. Every match eliminates exactly one player; no byes eliminate anyone. Matches needed for one champion?','["55", "57", "53", "54"]','"54"','Exactly 54 players must be eliminated; each match eliminates one.','Original tournament elimination exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000190','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','On a grid, go 11 steps east and 2 steps north in any order, with no other moves. How many distinct step sequences?','["81", "77", "78", "79"]','"78"','Choose the two north-step positions among 13: C(13,2)=78.','Original grid paths exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000191','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','11 different keys must be tried on one lock. Exactly one works; unsuccessful keys are never retried. Maximum trials needed to open it?','["10", "11", "12", "14"]','"11"','The working key can be last among all 11; identifying the last key is not opening the lock.','Original cycle permutation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000192','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A menu has 11 optional toppings; each can be included once or omitted independently. How many topping selections including none?','["2048", "2049", "2051", "2047"]','"2048"','Each of 11 toppings has two states, so 2^11=2048.','Original subset counts exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000193','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose a president and a different secretary from 11 candidates. How many assignments?','["111", "113", "109", "110"]','"110"','11 president choices times 10 secretary choices=110.','Original ordered arrangements exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000194','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 55 students, 16 study art, 14 study music, and 11 study both. How many study at least one?','["22", "18", "19", "20"]','"19"','Add the groups and subtract double-counted overlap: 16+14-11=19.','Original inclusion exclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000195','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A 12-hour clock shows 9:00. What hour number appears 24 hours later?','["8", "9", "10", "12"]','"9"','Add 24 hours and reduce modulo 12, labeling remainder zero as 12.','Original modular clock exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000196','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A board has 18 tiles, initially 9 black and the rest white. A move flips exactly two different tiles. After any number of moves, what is the largest reachable number of black tiles?','["17", "18", "20", "16"]','"17"','Each move preserves black-count parity. The largest count at most 18 with parity 1 is 17; flipping pairs of white tiles attains it.','Original parity invariant exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000197','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','An integer lies from 1 through 2047. Each trial guesses one value and reports correct, too low, or too high. Minimum worst-case trials to identify it by a correct guess?','["12", "14", "10", "11"]','"11"','With 11 trials binary search covers at most 2^11-1 values, achieved by guessing midpoints.','Original bounded search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000198','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Two identical test sensors break above an unknown threshold in a 45-level building, and survive at or below it. The threshold is the highest safe floor, from 0 (all floors unsafe) through 45 (all floors safe). Broken sensors cannot be reused. Minimum worst-case drops to find the threshold?','["12", "8", "9", "10"]','"9"','9 drops cover a descending sum from 9 through 1, totaling 9×10/2=45 levels using decreasing gaps; 8 drops cover fewer.','Original two egg threshold exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000199','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the smallest positive integer greater than 66 that is divisible by both 2 and 3?','["71", "72", "73", "75"]','"72"','Common multiples are multiples of 6; the next after 66 is 72.','Original remainders exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000200','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A prize is in exactly one of boxes numbered 1 through 11. Exactly one speaker tells the truth. A says “the box number is even”; B says “the box number is odd”; C says “the box number is divisible by 3.” How many box numbers remain possible?','["8", "9", "11", "7"]','"8"','A and B are complementary, so exactly one already tells the truth. C must be false. Excluding 3 multiples of 3 leaves 11−3=8 possibilities.','Original exactly one speaker exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000176','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 9 times. Probability that every flip is heads?','["1/512", "1/1024", "513/1024", "0"]','"1/512"','Independence multiplies 9 factors of 1/2: 1/512.','Original all heads exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000177','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is flipped independently 10 times. Probability of at least one head?','["1023/2048", "2047/2048", "0", "1023/1024"]','"1023/1024"','Take the complement of all tails: 1−(1/2)^10.','Original at least one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000178','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 11 times. Probability of exactly one head?','["2059/4096", "0", "11/2048", "11/4096"]','"11/2048"','Choose one head position in 11 ways, each sequence has probability 1/2048.','Original exactly one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000179','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is tossed independently 12 times. Probability of exactly two heads?','["0", "33/2048", "33/4096", "2081/4096"]','"33/2048"','C(12,2)=66 qualifying sequences out of 4096.','Original binomial two successes exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000180','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag holds 11 red and 10 blue tokens. Every token is equally likely to be drawn once. Probability of red?','["11/21", "11/42", "16/21", "0"]','"11/21"','11 favorable tokens among 21 total.','Original one uniform draw exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000181','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two tokens uniformly without replacement from 11 red and 12 blue. Probability both are red?','["5/46", "14/23", "0", "5/23"]','"5/23"','Multiply 11/23 by 10/22.','Original without replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000182','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw twice uniformly with replacement from 11 red and 13 blue tokens. Probability both red?','["697/1152", "0", "121/576", "121/1152"]','"121/576"','Replacement makes independent draws with red probability 11/24; square it.','Original with replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000183','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two uniformly without replacement from 12 red and 14 blue tokens. Probability of one of each color?','["0", "168/325", "84/325", "493/650"]','"168/325"','Add red-blue and blue-red: 2×12×14/(26×25).','Original one of each color exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000184','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 66. Probability it is divisible by 3?','["1/3", "1/6", "2/3", "0"]','"1/3"','22 multiples of 3 among 66 integers.','Original uniform integer divisor exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000185','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 66. Probability it is divisible by 2 or 3?','["1/3", "5/6", "0", "2/3"]','"2/3"','33 multiples of 2 plus 22 multiples of 3 minus 11 multiples of 6 gives 44/66.','Original uniform integer union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000186','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Randomly order 11 distinct books, all orders equally likely. Probability a specified book is first?','["6/11", "0", "1/11", "1/22"]','"1/11"','Each book has equal probability of any of the 11 positions.','Original random permutation first exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000187','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Arrange 13 distinct people uniformly in a straight line. Probability two specified people stand next to each other?','["0", "2/13", "1/13", "15/26"]','"2/13"','Treat the pair as a block with two internal orders: 2×(12)!/(13)!=2/13.','Original permutation adjacency exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000188','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A committee of 2 is selected uniformly from 14 distinct people. Probability a specified person is selected?','["1/7", "1/14", "4/7", "0"]','"1/7"','The person belongs to 13 of C(14,2) committees, yielding 2/14.','Original subset inclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000189','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A lottery chooses one winning ticket uniformly from 220 tickets. You own 11 distinct tickets. Probability you win?','["1/40", "21/40", "0", "1/20"]','"1/20"','11 of 220 equally likely tickets are yours.','Original matching lottery exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000190','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/10 and 1/12. Probability both occur?','["121/240", "0", "1/120", "1/240"]','"1/120"','For independent events multiply 1/10 by 1/12.','Original independent intersections exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000191','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/11 and 1/13. Probability at least one occurs?','["0", "23/143", "23/286", "83/143"]','"23/143"','P(A or B)=P(A)+P(B)−P(A)P(B)=23/143.','Original independent union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000192','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A number is chosen uniformly from 1 through 44. Given it is even, probability it is also divisible by 4?','["1/2", "1/4", "3/4", "0"]','"1/2"','There are 22 even values and 11 multiples of 4 in the restricted sample.','Original conditional restricted sample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000193','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A group has 11 engineers and 3 artists. Choose 3 people uniformly without replacement. Probability all are engineers?','["165/728", "529/728", "0", "165/364"]','"165/364"','C(11,3)/C(14,3)=165/364.','Original hypergeometric committee exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000194','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent attempts succeed with probability 1/3 each. Probability the first success is on attempt 9?','["19939/39366", "0", "256/19683", "128/19683"]','"256/19683"','8 failures followed by success: (2/3)^8×(1/3).','Original geometric first success exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000195','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice, each labeled 1 through 11. Probability their values match?','["0", "1/11", "1/22", "6/11"]','"1/11"','11 matching ordered pairs among 121 equally likely pairs.','Original uniform dice equality exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000196','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 12. Probability the first value is strictly greater than the second?','["11/24", "11/48", "35/48", "0"]','"11/24"','Excluding 12 ties, symmetry halves the remaining 132 ordered pairs: 11/(24).','Original uniform dice ordering exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000197','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 13. Probability at least one shows 13?','["25/338", "97/169", "0", "25/169"]','"25/169"','Complement of neither showing the top face: 1−(12/13)².','Original dice maximum exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000198','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A code has 9 digits chosen independently uniformly from 0–9; leading zeros allowed. Probability all digits are nonzero? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["12.91%", "69.37%", "38.74%", "19.37%"]','"38.74%"','Each digit is nonzero with probability 9/10; multiply 9 factors. This is approximately 38.74%.','Original random digit code exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000199','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Pick one of two bags by fair coin. Bag A has 11 red and 1 blue token; bag B has 1 red and 11 blue. Draw uniformly. Given red, probability bag A was chosen?','["0", "11/12", "11/24", "23/24"]','"11/12"','Bayes: (1/2×11/12)/(1/2×11/12+1/2×1/12)=11/12.','Original Bayes selected bag exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000200','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','9 people have independent birthdays uniform over a fictional 110-day year. Probability all birthdays differ? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["71.46%", "35.73%", "23.82%", "85.73%"]','"71.46%"','Successive birthday choices: 110/110 × 109/110 × ... × 102/110. This is approximately 71.46%.','Original sampling distinct birthdays exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000176','ESTIMATE','For planning, a vehicle covers 187 km at a constant 125 km/h with no stops. Estimate travel time in minutes. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'90','Time = distance/speed × 60 = 187/125×60 minutes. Rounded result: 90.','Original travel time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
