('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000200','ESTIMATE','A solution contains 27.5 grams of dissolved salt in a final volume of 11 liters. Estimate concentration in grams per liter. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'2.5','Concentration = dissolved mass/final volume = 27.5/11 g/L. Rounded result: 2.5.','Original dilution concentration exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000201','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A hidden token is in one of 4096 numbered lockers. Each question may ask membership in any chosen subset and receives a truthful yes/no answer. Minimum worst-case questions?','["13", "15", "11", "12"]','"12"','12 bits distinguish 2^12=4096 possibilities; successive halves attain this bound.','Original binary information exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000202','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 59049 coins exactly one is heavier; all genuine coins weigh equally. With a balance scale, minimum worst-case weighings?','["13", "9", "10", "11"]','"10"','Each weighing has three outcomes. Split into three equal groups repeatedly: 10 weighings distinguish 3^10=59049 coins.','Original balance ternary search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000203','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A drawer has unlimited socks in 12 colors. Picking blindly, how many socks guarantee a matching color pair?','["12", "13", "14", "16"]','"13"','12 picks could all have different colors; pick 13 repeats a color.','Original pigeonhole socks exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000204','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag has unlimited beads in 12 colors. How many blind picks guarantee three of one color?','["25", "26", "28", "24"]','"25"','At most two per color gives 24 without a triple; the next forces three.','Original pigeonhole triples exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000205','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A club of 15 members plays one game between every unordered pair. How many games?','["106", "108", "104", "105"]','"105"','Choose two members: (15)(14)/2=105.','Original pair counting exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000206','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fence has 14 posts in a straight row, spaced 10 meters apart. Distance from first post to last, in meters?','["133", "129", "130", "131"]','"130"','There are 13 gaps, each 10 meters: 130.','Original inclusive intervals exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000207','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Four cards show 10, 22, Blue and Green. Every card has an integer on one side and exactly one of those colors on the other. To test “if the integer is divisible by 2, the color is Blue,” which shown cards must be turned?','["Neither numbered card nor Green", "10 and 22 and Green", "Blue only", "All four"]','"10 and 22 and Green"','Turn every visible multiple of 2 to check Blue, and Green to exclude a multiple on its reverse. Blue and visible nonmultiples cannot falsify the rule.','Original conditional counterexample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000208','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Within a survey, some musicians are engineers. Every engineer in the survey is employed. Which inference is valid?','["Some surveyed musicians are employed", "All musicians are engineers", "All employed people are musicians", "No engineers make music"]','"Some surveyed musicians are employed"','Interpreting both premises within the survey, the engineer-musicians are employed.','Original syllogism exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000209','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','If a server is offline, requests fail. A request succeeds. Given the rule, what follows?','["The server is offline", "All future requests succeed", "The server has no requests", "The server is not offline"]','"The server is not offline"','A successful request falsifies the consequence of offline status.','Original implication chains exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000210','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A or B or both is true. A is true. Is B determined?','["Yes, B is false", "The premises conflict", "No", "Yes, B is true"]','"No"','With A true, the disjunction permits either truth value for B.','Original conditional diagnosis exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000211','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the logical negation of “every student passed both tests”?','["No student took the tests", "Some student failed at least one test", "All students failed both tests", "Some student passed one test"]','"Some student failed at least one test"','Negate the outer all and the inner conjunction: exists a student with not first or not second.','Original logical negation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000212','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A secret code is an integer from 1 through 12. Exactly one statement is true: A says it is even; B says it is divisible by 3. How many possible codes satisfy this?','["6", "7", "9", "5"]','"6"','Count integers in exactly one of the two sets. The valid codes are 2, 3, 4, 8, 9, 10. Equivalently floor(12/2)+floor(12/3)−2floor(12/6)=6.','Original truth assignment exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000213','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Exactly four runners finish without ties. Oren finishes before Paz; Mina finishes after Paz; Rui finishes after Mina. Who finishes first?','["Paz", "Mina", "Rui", "Oren"]','"Oren"','The constraints force the order Oren → Paz → Mina → Rui; the first runner is Oren.','Original ordering constraints exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000214','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A knockout contest begins with 60 players. Every match eliminates exactly one player; no byes eliminate anyone. Matches needed for one champion?','["62", "58", "59", "60"]','"59"','Exactly 59 players must be eliminated; each match eliminates one.','Original tournament elimination exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000215','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','On a grid, go 12 steps east and 2 steps north in any order, with no other moves. How many distinct step sequences?','["90", "91", "92", "94"]','"91"','Choose the two north-step positions among 14: C(14,2)=91.','Original grid paths exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000216','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','12 different keys must be tried on one lock. Exactly one works; unsuccessful keys are never retried. Maximum trials needed to open it?','["12", "13", "15", "11"]','"12"','The working key can be last among all 12; identifying the last key is not opening the lock.','Original cycle permutation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000217','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A menu has 12 optional toppings; each can be included once or omitted independently. How many topping selections including none?','["4097", "4099", "4095", "4096"]','"4096"','Each of 12 toppings has two states, so 2^12=4096.','Original subset counts exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000218','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose a president and a different secretary from 12 candidates. How many assignments?','["135", "131", "132", "133"]','"132"','12 president choices times 11 secretary choices=132.','Original ordered arrangements exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000219','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 60 students, 17 study art, 15 study music, and 12 study both. How many study at least one?','["19", "20", "21", "23"]','"20"','Add the groups and subtract double-counted overlap: 17+15-12=20.','Original inclusion exclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000220','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A 12-hour clock shows 10:00. What hour number appears 25 hours later?','["11", "12", "14", "10"]','"11"','Add 25 hours and reduce modulo 12, labeling remainder zero as 12.','Original modular clock exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000221','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A board has 19 tiles, initially 10 black and the rest white. A move flips exactly two different tiles. After any number of moves, what is the largest reachable number of black tiles?','["19", "21", "17", "18"]','"18"','Each move preserves black-count parity. The largest count at most 19 with parity 0 is 18; flipping pairs of white tiles attains it.','Original parity invariant exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000222','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','An integer lies from 1 through 4095. Each trial guesses one value and reports correct, too low, or too high. Minimum worst-case trials to identify it by a correct guess?','["15", "11", "12", "13"]','"12"','With 12 trials binary search covers at most 2^12-1 values, achieved by guessing midpoints.','Original bounded search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000223','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Two identical test sensors break above an unknown threshold in a 55-level building, and survive at or below it. The threshold is the highest safe floor, from 0 (all floors unsafe) through 55 (all floors safe). Broken sensors cannot be reused. Minimum worst-case drops to find the threshold?','["9", "10", "11", "13"]','"10"','10 drops cover a descending sum from 10 through 1, totaling 10×11/2=55 levels using decreasing gaps; 9 drops cover fewer.','Original two egg threshold exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000224','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the smallest positive integer greater than 72 that is divisible by both 2 and 3?','["78", "79", "81", "77"]','"78"','Common multiples are multiples of 6; the next after 72 is 78.','Original remainders exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000225','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A prize is in exactly one of boxes numbered 1 through 12. Exactly one speaker tells the truth. A says “the box number is even”; B says “the box number is odd”; C says “the box number is divisible by 3.” How many box numbers remain possible?','["9", "11", "7", "8"]','"8"','A and B are complementary, so exactly one already tells the truth. C must be false. Excluding 4 multiples of 3 leaves 12−4=8 possibilities.','Original exactly one speaker exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000201','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 10 times. Probability that every flip is heads?','["1/2048", "1025/2048", "0", "1/1024"]','"1/1024"','Independence multiplies 10 factors of 1/2: 1/1024.','Original all heads exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000202','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is flipped independently 11 times. Probability of at least one head?','["4095/4096", "0", "2047/2048", "2047/4096"]','"2047/2048"','Take the complement of all tails: 1−(1/2)^11.','Original at least one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000203','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 12 times. Probability of exactly one head?','["0", "3/1024", "3/2048", "1027/2048"]','"3/1024"','Choose one head position in 12 ways, each sequence has probability 1/4096.','Original exactly one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000204','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is tossed independently 13 times. Probability of exactly two heads?','["39/4096", "39/8192", "4135/8192", "0"]','"39/4096"','C(13,2)=78 qualifying sequences out of 8192.','Original binomial two successes exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000205','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag holds 12 red and 11 blue tokens. Every token is equally likely to be drawn once. Probability of red?','["6/23", "35/46", "0", "12/23"]','"12/23"','12 favorable tokens among 23 total.','Original one uniform draw exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000206','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two tokens uniformly without replacement from 12 red and 13 blue. Probability both are red?','["61/100", "0", "11/50", "11/100"]','"11/50"','Multiply 12/25 by 11/24.','Original without replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000207','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw twice uniformly with replacement from 12 red and 14 blue tokens. Probability both red?','["0", "36/169", "18/169", "205/338"]','"36/169"','Replacement makes independent draws with red probability 12/26; square it.','Original with replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000208','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two uniformly without replacement from 13 red and 15 blue tokens. Probability of one of each color?','["65/126", "65/252", "191/252", "0"]','"65/126"','Add red-blue and blue-red: 2×13×15/(28×27).','Original one of each color exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000209','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 72. Probability it is divisible by 3?','["1/6", "2/3", "0", "1/3"]','"1/3"','24 multiples of 3 among 72 integers.','Original uniform integer divisor exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000210','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 72. Probability it is divisible by 2 or 3?','["5/6", "0", "2/3", "1/3"]','"2/3"','36 multiples of 2 plus 24 multiples of 3 minus 12 multiples of 6 gives 48/72.','Original uniform integer union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000211','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Randomly order 12 distinct books, all orders equally likely. Probability a specified book is first?','["0", "1/12", "1/24", "13/24"]','"1/12"','Each book has equal probability of any of the 12 positions.','Original random permutation first exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000212','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Arrange 14 distinct people uniformly in a straight line. Probability two specified people stand next to each other?','["1/7", "1/14", "4/7", "0"]','"1/7"','Treat the pair as a block with two internal orders: 2×(13)!/(14)!=2/14.','Original permutation adjacency exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000213','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A committee of 2 is selected uniformly from 15 distinct people. Probability a specified person is selected?','["1/15", "17/30", "0", "2/15"]','"2/15"','The person belongs to 14 of C(15,2) committees, yielding 2/15.','Original subset inclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000214','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A lottery chooses one winning ticket uniformly from 240 tickets. You own 12 distinct tickets. Probability you win?','["21/40", "0", "1/20", "1/40"]','"1/20"','12 of 240 equally likely tickets are yours.','Original matching lottery exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000215','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/11 and 1/13. Probability both occur?','["0", "1/143", "1/286", "72/143"]','"1/143"','For independent events multiply 1/11 by 1/13.','Original independent intersections exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000216','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/12 and 1/14. Probability at least one occurs?','["25/168", "25/336", "193/336", "0"]','"25/168"','P(A or B)=P(A)+P(B)−P(A)P(B)=25/168.','Original independent union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000217','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A number is chosen uniformly from 1 through 48. Given it is even, probability it is also divisible by 4?','["1/4", "3/4", "0", "1/2"]','"1/2"','There are 24 even values and 12 multiples of 4 in the restricted sample.','Original conditional restricted sample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000218','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A group has 12 engineers and 3 artists. Choose 3 people uniformly without replacement. Probability all are engineers?','["135/182", "0", "44/91", "22/91"]','"44/91"','C(12,3)/C(15,3)=220/455.','Original hypergeometric committee exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000219','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent attempts succeed with probability 1/3 each. Probability the first success is on attempt 10?','["0", "512/59049", "256/59049", "59561/118098"]','"512/59049"','9 failures followed by success: (2/3)^9×(1/3).','Original geometric first success exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000220','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice, each labeled 1 through 12. Probability their values match?','["1/12", "1/24", "13/24", "0"]','"1/12"','12 matching ordered pairs among 144 equally likely pairs.','Original uniform dice equality exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000221','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 13. Probability the first value is strictly greater than the second?','["3/13", "19/26", "0", "6/13"]','"6/13"','Excluding 13 ties, symmetry halves the remaining 156 ordered pairs: 12/(26).','Original uniform dice ordering exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000222','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 14. Probability at least one shows 14?','["223/392", "0", "27/196", "27/392"]','"27/196"','Complement of neither showing the top face: 1−(13/14)².','Original dice maximum exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000223','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A code has 10 digits chosen independently uniformly from 0–9; leading zeros allowed. Probability all digits are nonzero? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["67.43%", "34.87%", "17.43%", "11.62%"]','"34.87%"','Each digit is nonzero with probability 9/10; multiply 10 factors. This is approximately 34.87%.','Original random digit code exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000224','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Pick one of two bags by fair coin. Bag A has 12 red and 1 blue token; bag B has 1 red and 12 blue. Draw uniformly. Given red, probability bag A was chosen?','["12/13", "6/13", "25/26", "0"]','"12/13"','Bayes: (1/2×12/13)/(1/2×12/13+1/2×1/13)=12/13.','Original Bayes selected bag exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000225','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','10 people have independent birthdays uniform over a fictional 120-day year. Probability all birthdays differ? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["34.01%", "22.67%", "84.01%", "68.02%"]','"68.02%"','Successive birthday choices: 120/120 × 119/120 × ... × 111/120. This is approximately 68.02%.','Original sampling distinct birthdays exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000201','ESTIMATE','For planning, a vehicle covers 204 km at a constant 135 km/h with no stops. Estimate travel time in minutes. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'91','Time = distance/speed × 60 = 204/135×60 minutes. Rounded result: 91.','Original travel time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000202','ESTIMATE','A car averages 13 liters per 100 km over a 372-km route. Estimate fuel needed in liters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'48.4','Fuel = 372×13/100 liters. Rounded result: 48.4.','Original fuel consumption exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000203','ESTIMATE','A rectangular floor is 12.5 m long and 10.25 m wide. Assuming no waste, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'128.1','Area = length×width = 12.5×10.25 m². Rounded result: 128.1.','Original area coverage exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000204','ESTIMATE','A wall area is 156 square meters. One liter covers 7 square meters per coat. For two coats, estimate liters of paint, ignoring waste. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'44.6','Paint volume = 156×2/7 liters. Rounded result: 44.6.','Original paint demand exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000205','ESTIMATE','A rectangular tank has internal dimensions 120 cm by 100 cm by 35 cm. Estimate capacity in liters; use 1 liter = 1,000 cubic cm. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'420.0','Capacity = 120×100×35/1000 liters. Rounded result: 420.0.','Original water tank exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000206','ESTIMATE','A tap delivers 14 liters per minute at a constant rate. Estimate minutes to fill an empty 324-liter tank. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'23.1','Time = volume/flow = 324/14 minutes. Rounded result: 23.1.','Original flow filling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000207','ESTIMATE','A 420-watt lamp runs for 11 hours. Estimate electricity used in kilowatt-hours. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'4.62','Energy = power×time = 420/1000×11 kWh. Rounded result: 4.62.','Original electric energy exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000208','ESTIMATE','An appliance uses 30.0 kWh. Electricity costs $0.23 per kWh with no fixed fees or taxes. Estimate cost in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'6.9','Cost = 30.0×0.23 dollars. Rounded result: 6.9.','Original energy cost exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000209','ESTIMATE','A walker takes 14808 steps, each 0.72 meters long. Estimate total distance in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'10.7','Distance = 14808×0.72/1000 km. Rounded result: 10.7.','Original walking distance exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000210','ESTIMATE','A sample of 100 identical-sized plots contains 84 trees. Assume the same density over 1,250 plots. Estimate total trees. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'1050','Scale sample count by 1250/100: 84×12.5 trees. Rounded result: 1050.','Original sample extrapolation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000211','ESTIMATE','A solid block has volume 15.6 cubic centimeters and density 2.7 grams per cubic centimeter. Estimate mass in grams. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'42.1','Mass = density×volume = 2.7×15.6 grams. Rounded result: 42.1.','Original density mass exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000212','ESTIMATE','An exchange model gives exactly 35.5 baht per US dollar, without fees. Estimate baht received for $204. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'7242','Baht = 204×35.5. Rounded result: 7242.','Original currency conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000213','ESTIMATE','A budget is $1620. Assume 20% goes to transport. Estimate transport spending in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'324.0','Transport = 1620×20/100 dollars. Rounded result: 324.0.','Original percentage budget exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000214','ESTIMATE','A model population starts at 12000 and grows exactly 5% each year for 10 years. Estimate its final population. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'19547','Compound growth: 12000×1.05^10. Rounded result: 19547.','Original compound growth exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000215','ESTIMATE','Equipment initially costs $14400 and retains 85% of its value after each year. Estimate its value after 10 years in dollars. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'2835','Value = 14400×0.85^10 dollars. Rounded result: 2835.','Original compound depreciation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000216','ESTIMATE','A cyclist rides 36 km outward at 12 km/h and the same distance back at 18 km/h. No stops. Estimate overall average speed in km/h. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'14.4','Total distance 72 km / total time (36/12+36/18) h = 14.4 km/h. Rounded result: 14.4.','Original average speed round trip exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000217','ESTIMATE','A class has 12 students scoring 70 and 10 students scoring 90. Estimate their combined mean score in points. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'79.1','Weighted mean = (12×70+10×90)/(12+10) points. Rounded result: 79.1.','Original weighted mean exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000218','ESTIMATE','A circular garden has radius 12.2 meters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'467.6','Area = pi×radius² = 3.1416×12.2² m². Rounded result: 467.6.','Original circular area exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000219','ESTIMATE','A wheel has radius 44.4 centimeters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its circumference in centimeters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'279.0','Circumference = 2×3.1416×44.4 cm. Rounded result: 279.0.','Original circular circumference exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000220','ESTIMATE','A map scale is 1:50,000. A measured route is 20.4 cm on the map. Estimate actual route length in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'10.2','Each map centimeter represents 50,000 cm = 0.5 km; distance = 20.4×0.5 km. Rounded result: 10.2.','Original map scaling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000221','ESTIMATE','A parcel weighs 27.6 pounds. Use exactly 1 pound = 0.45359237 kg. Estimate mass in kilograms. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'12.5','Mass = 27.6×0.45359237 kg. Rounded result: 12.5.','Original unit conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000222','ESTIMATE','A 1500-megabyte file downloads at exactly 60 megabits per second. Use 8 bits per byte and decimal units; ignore overhead. Estimate seconds needed. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'200','Time = file size×8/link rate = 1500×8/60 seconds. Rounded result: 200.','Original download time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000223','ESTIMATE','A drive offers exactly 120 decimal gigabytes. Each photo occupies exactly 4 decimal megabytes. Ignoring filesystem overhead, estimate how many whole photos fit; 1 GB = 1,000 MB. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'30000','Photo count = 120×1000/4; the result is an integer. Rounded result: 30000.','Original storage capacity exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000224','ESTIMATE','A factory makes 156 items per hour at a constant rate. Estimate items produced during 170 minutes, allowing fractional theoretical output before rounding. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'442','Output = 156×170/60 items. Rounded result: 442.','Original production throughput exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000225','ESTIMATE','A solution contains 30.0 grams of dissolved salt in a final volume of 12 liters. Estimate concentration in grams per liter. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'2.5','Concentration = dissolved mass/final volume = 30.0/12 g/L. Rounded result: 2.5.','Original dilution concentration exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000226','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A hidden token is in one of 8192 numbered lockers. Each question may ask membership in any chosen subset and receives a truthful yes/no answer. Minimum worst-case questions?','["16", "12", "13", "14"]','"13"','13 bits distinguish 2^13=8192 possibilities; successive halves attain this bound.','Original binary information exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000227','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 177147 coins exactly one is heavier; all genuine coins weigh equally. With a balance scale, minimum worst-case weighings?','["10", "11", "12", "14"]','"11"','Each weighing has three outcomes. Split into three equal groups repeatedly: 11 weighings distinguish 3^11=177147 coins.','Original balance ternary search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000228','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A drawer has unlimited socks in 13 colors. Picking blindly, how many socks guarantee a matching color pair?','["14", "15", "17", "13"]','"14"','13 picks could all have different colors; pick 14 repeats a color.','Original pigeonhole socks exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000229','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag has unlimited beads in 13 colors. How many blind picks guarantee three of one color?','["28", "30", "26", "27"]','"27"','At most two per color gives 26 without a triple; the next forces three.','Original pigeonhole triples exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000230','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A club of 16 members plays one game between every unordered pair. How many games?','["123", "119", "120", "121"]','"120"','Choose two members: (16)(15)/2=120.','Original pair counting exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000231','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fence has 15 posts in a straight row, spaced 11 meters apart. Distance from first post to last, in meters?','["153", "154", "155", "157"]','"154"','There are 14 gaps, each 11 meters: 154.','Original inclusive intervals exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000232','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Four cards show 11, 24, Blue and Green. Every card has an integer on one side and exactly one of those colors on the other. To test “if the integer is divisible by 3, the color is Blue,” which shown cards must be turned?','["24 and Green", "Blue only", "All four", "Neither numbered card nor Green"]','"24 and Green"','Turn every visible multiple of 3 to check Blue, and Green to exclude a multiple on its reverse. Blue and visible nonmultiples cannot falsify the rule.','Original conditional counterexample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000233','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','All members of group A belong to B, and all members of B belong to C. Can an object belong to A but not C?','["Yes, if it is not in B", "Yes, always", "Only if C is empty", "No"]','"No"','An A member must also be in B and therefore C.','Original syllogism exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000234','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','If a pass is valid, the gate opens. If the gate opens, the counter increases. The counter did not increase. What follows?','["The gate opened", "The counter increased twice", "The pass is not valid", "The pass is valid"]','"The pass is not valid"','No counter increase means no gate opening, which means no valid pass.','Original implication chains exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000235','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','If A is false, B is true. B is false. What follows?','["Nothing follows", "A is true", "A is false", "B is true"]','"A is true"','The contrapositive of not A implies B is not B implies A.','Original conditional diagnosis exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000236','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the logical negation of “exactly one of A and B is true”?','["A and B have the same truth value", "At least one is true", "Both are false only", "A is true only"]','"A and B have the same truth value"','Exclusive or is false when both terms are true or both false.','Original logical negation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000237','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A secret code is an integer from 1 through 13. Exactly one statement is true: A says it is even; B says it is divisible by 3. How many possible codes satisfy this?','["7", "9", "5", "6"]','"6"','Count integers in exactly one of the two sets. The valid codes are 2, 3, 4, 8, 9, 10. Equivalently floor(13/2)+floor(13/3)−2floor(13/6)=6.','Original truth assignment exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000238','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Exactly four runners finish without ties. Oren finishes before Paz; Rui finishes after Paz; Mina finishes after Rui. Who finishes second?','["Rui", "Mina", "Paz", "Oren"]','"Paz"','The constraints force the order Oren → Paz → Rui → Mina; the second runner is Paz.','Original ordering constraints exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000239','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A knockout contest begins with 65 players. Every match eliminates exactly one player; no byes eliminate anyone. Matches needed for one champion?','["63", "64", "65", "67"]','"64"','Exactly 64 players must be eliminated; each match eliminates one.','Original tournament elimination exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000240','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','On a grid, go 13 steps east and 2 steps north in any order, with no other moves. How many distinct step sequences?','["105", "106", "108", "104"]','"105"','Choose the two north-step positions among 15: C(15,2)=105.','Original grid paths exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000241','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','13 different keys must be tried on one lock. Exactly one works; unsuccessful keys are never retried. Maximum trials needed to open it?','["14", "16", "12", "13"]','"13"','The working key can be last among all 13; identifying the last key is not opening the lock.','Original cycle permutation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000242','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A menu has 13 optional toppings; each can be included once or omitted independently. How many topping selections including none?','["8195", "8191", "8192", "8193"]','"8192"','Each of 13 toppings has two states, so 2^13=8192.','Original subset counts exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000243','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose a president and a different secretary from 13 candidates. How many assignments?','["155", "156", "157", "159"]','"156"','13 president choices times 12 secretary choices=156.','Original ordered arrangements exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000244','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 65 students, 18 study art, 16 study music, and 13 study both. How many study at least one?','["21", "22", "24", "20"]','"21"','Add the groups and subtract double-counted overlap: 18+16-13=21.','Original inclusion exclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000245','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A 12-hour clock shows 11:00. What hour number appears 26 hours later?','["2", "4", "0", "1"]','"1"','Add 26 hours and reduce modulo 12, labeling remainder zero as 12.','Original modular clock exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000246','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A board has 20 tiles, initially 11 black and the rest white. A move flips exactly two different tiles. After any number of moves, what is the largest reachable number of black tiles?','["22", "18", "19", "20"]','"19"','Each move preserves black-count parity. The largest count at most 20 with parity 1 is 19; flipping pairs of white tiles attains it.','Original parity invariant exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000247','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','An integer lies from 1 through 8191. Each trial guesses one value and reports correct, too low, or too high. Minimum worst-case trials to identify it by a correct guess?','["12", "13", "14", "16"]','"13"','With 13 trials binary search covers at most 2^13-1 values, achieved by guessing midpoints.','Original bounded search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000248','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Two identical test sensors break above an unknown threshold in a 66-level building, and survive at or below it. The threshold is the highest safe floor, from 0 (all floors unsafe) through 66 (all floors safe). Broken sensors cannot be reused. Minimum worst-case drops to find the threshold?','["11", "12", "14", "10"]','"11"','11 drops cover a descending sum from 11 through 1, totaling 11×12/2=66 levels using decreasing gaps; 10 drops cover fewer.','Original two egg threshold exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000249','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the smallest positive integer greater than 78 that is divisible by both 2 and 3?','["85", "87", "83", "84"]','"84"','Common multiples are multiples of 6; the next after 78 is 84.','Original remainders exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000250','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A prize is in exactly one of boxes numbered 1 through 13. Exactly one speaker tells the truth. A says “the box number is even”; B says “the box number is odd”; C says “the box number is divisible by 3.” How many box numbers remain possible?','["12", "8", "9", "10"]','"9"','A and B are complementary, so exactly one already tells the truth. C must be false. Excluding 4 multiples of 3 leaves 13−4=9 possibilities.','Original exactly one speaker exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000226','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 11 times. Probability that every flip is heads?','["2049/4096", "0", "1/2048", "1/4096"]','"1/2048"','Independence multiplies 11 factors of 1/2: 1/2048.','Original all heads exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000227','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is flipped independently 12 times. Probability of at least one head?','["0", "4095/4096", "4095/8192", "8191/8192"]','"4095/4096"','Take the complement of all tails: 1−(1/2)^12.','Original at least one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000228','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 13 times. Probability of exactly one head?','["13/8192", "13/16384", "8205/16384", "0"]','"13/8192"','Choose one head position in 13 ways, each sequence has probability 1/8192.','Original exactly one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000229','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is tossed independently 14 times. Probability of exactly two heads?','["91/32768", "16475/32768", "0", "91/16384"]','"91/16384"','C(14,2)=91 qualifying sequences out of 16384.','Original binomial two successes exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000230','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag holds 13 red and 12 blue tokens. Every token is equally likely to be drawn once. Probability of red?','["19/25", "0", "13/25", "13/50"]','"13/25"','13 favorable tokens among 25 total.','Original one uniform draw exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000231','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two tokens uniformly without replacement from 13 red and 14 blue. Probability both are red?','["0", "2/9", "1/9", "11/18"]','"2/9"','Multiply 13/27 by 12/26.','Original without replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000232','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw twice uniformly with replacement from 13 red and 15 blue tokens. Probability both red?','["169/784", "169/1568", "953/1568", "0"]','"169/784"','Replacement makes independent draws with red probability 13/28; square it.','Original with replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000233','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two uniformly without replacement from 14 red and 16 blue tokens. Probability of one of each color?','["112/435", "659/870", "0", "224/435"]','"224/435"','Add red-blue and blue-red: 2×14×16/(30×29).','Original one of each color exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000234','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 78. Probability it is divisible by 3?','["2/3", "0", "1/3", "1/6"]','"1/3"','26 multiples of 3 among 78 integers.','Original uniform integer divisor exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000235','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 78. Probability it is divisible by 2 or 3?','["0", "2/3", "1/3", "5/6"]','"2/3"','39 multiples of 2 plus 26 multiples of 3 minus 13 multiples of 6 gives 52/78.','Original uniform integer union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000236','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Randomly order 13 distinct books, all orders equally likely. Probability a specified book is first?','["1/13", "1/26", "7/13", "0"]','"1/13"','Each book has equal probability of any of the 13 positions.','Original random permutation first exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000237','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Arrange 15 distinct people uniformly in a straight line. Probability two specified people stand next to each other?','["1/15", "17/30", "0", "2/15"]','"2/15"','Treat the pair as a block with two internal orders: 2×(14)!/(15)!=2/15.','Original permutation adjacency exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000238','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A committee of 2 is selected uniformly from 16 distinct people. Probability a specified person is selected?','["9/16", "0", "1/8", "1/16"]','"1/8"','The person belongs to 15 of C(16,2) committees, yielding 2/16.','Original subset inclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000239','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A lottery chooses one winning ticket uniformly from 260 tickets. You own 13 distinct tickets. Probability you win?','["0", "1/20", "1/40", "21/40"]','"1/20"','13 of 260 equally likely tickets are yours.','Original matching lottery exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000240','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/12 and 1/14. Probability both occur?','["1/168", "1/336", "169/336", "0"]','"1/168"','For independent events multiply 1/12 by 1/14.','Original independent intersections exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000241','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/13 and 1/15. Probability at least one occurs?','["9/130", "37/65", "0", "9/65"]','"9/65"','P(A or B)=P(A)+P(B)−P(A)P(B)=9/65.','Original independent union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000242','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A number is chosen uniformly from 1 through 52. Given it is even, probability it is also divisible by 4?','["3/4", "0", "1/2", "1/4"]','"1/2"','There are 26 even values and 13 multiples of 4 in the restricted sample.','Original conditional restricted sample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000243','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A group has 13 engineers and 3 artists. Choose 3 people uniformly without replacement. Probability all are engineers?','["0", "143/280", "143/560", "423/560"]','"143/280"','C(13,3)/C(16,3)=286/560.','Original hypergeometric committee exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000244','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent attempts succeed with probability 1/3 each. Probability the first success is on attempt 11?','["1024/177147", "512/177147", "178171/354294", "0"]','"1024/177147"','10 failures followed by success: (2/3)^10×(1/3).','Original geometric first success exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000245','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice, each labeled 1 through 13. Probability their values match?','["1/26", "7/13", "0", "1/13"]','"1/13"','13 matching ordered pairs among 169 equally likely pairs.','Original uniform dice equality exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000246','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 14. Probability the first value is strictly greater than the second?','["41/56", "0", "13/28", "13/56"]','"13/28"','Excluding 14 ties, symmetry halves the remaining 182 ordered pairs: 13/(28).','Original uniform dice ordering exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000247','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 15. Probability at least one shows 15?','["0", "29/225", "29/450", "127/225"]','"29/225"','Complement of neither showing the top face: 1−(14/15)².','Original dice maximum exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000248','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A code has 11 digits chosen independently uniformly from 0–9; leading zeros allowed. Probability all digits are nonzero? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["31.38%", "15.69%", "10.46%", "65.69%"]','"31.38%"','Each digit is nonzero with probability 9/10; multiply 11 factors. This is approximately 31.38%.','Original random digit code exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000249','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Pick one of two bags by fair coin. Bag A has 13 red and 1 blue token; bag B has 1 red and 13 blue. Draw uniformly. Given red, probability bag A was chosen?','["13/28", "27/28", "0", "13/14"]','"13/14"','Bayes: (1/2×13/14)/(1/2×13/14+1/2×1/14)=13/14.','Original Bayes selected bag exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000250','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','11 people have independent birthdays uniform over a fictional 130-day year. Probability all birthdays differ? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["21.58%", "82.36%", "64.73%", "32.36%"]','"64.73%"','Successive birthday choices: 130/130 × 129/130 × ... × 120/130. This is approximately 64.73%.','Original sampling distinct birthdays exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000226','ESTIMATE','For planning, a vehicle covers 221 km at a constant 145 km/h with no stops. Estimate travel time in minutes. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'91','Time = distance/speed × 60 = 221/145×60 minutes. Rounded result: 91.','Original travel time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000227','ESTIMATE','A car averages 14 liters per 100 km over a 403-km route. Estimate fuel needed in liters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'56.4','Fuel = 403×14/100 liters. Rounded result: 56.4.','Original fuel consumption exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000228','ESTIMATE','A rectangular floor is 13.5 m long and 11.25 m wide. Assuming no waste, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'151.9','Area = length×width = 13.5×11.25 m². Rounded result: 151.9.','Original area coverage exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000229','ESTIMATE','A wall area is 169 square meters. One liter covers 7 square meters per coat. For two coats, estimate liters of paint, ignoring waste. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'48.3','Paint volume = 169×2/7 liters. Rounded result: 48.3.','Original paint demand exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000230','ESTIMATE','A rectangular tank has internal dimensions 130 cm by 110 cm by 35 cm. Estimate capacity in liters; use 1 liter = 1,000 cubic cm. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'500.5','Capacity = 130×110×35/1000 liters. Rounded result: 500.5.','Original water tank exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000231','ESTIMATE','A tap delivers 15 liters per minute at a constant rate. Estimate minutes to fill an empty 351-liter tank. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'23.4','Time = volume/flow = 351/15 minutes. Rounded result: 23.4.','Original flow filling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000232','ESTIMATE','A 455-watt lamp runs for 12 hours. Estimate electricity used in kilowatt-hours. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'5.46','Energy = power×time = 455/1000×12 kWh. Rounded result: 5.46.','Original electric energy exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000233','ESTIMATE','An appliance uses 32.5 kWh. Electricity costs $0.23 per kWh with no fixed fees or taxes. Estimate cost in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'7.48','Cost = 32.5×0.23 dollars. Rounded result: 7.48.','Original energy cost exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000234','ESTIMATE','A walker takes 16042 steps, each 0.72 meters long. Estimate total distance in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'11.6','Distance = 16042×0.72/1000 km. Rounded result: 11.6.','Original walking distance exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000235','ESTIMATE','A sample of 100 identical-sized plots contains 91 trees. Assume the same density over 1,250 plots. Estimate total trees. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'1138','Scale sample count by 1250/100: 91×12.5 trees. Rounded result: 1138.','Original sample extrapolation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000236','ESTIMATE','A solid block has volume 16.9 cubic centimeters and density 2.7 grams per cubic centimeter. Estimate mass in grams. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'45.6','Mass = density×volume = 2.7×16.9 grams. Rounded result: 45.6.','Original density mass exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000237','ESTIMATE','An exchange model gives exactly 35.5 baht per US dollar, without fees. Estimate baht received for $221. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'7846','Baht = 221×35.5. Rounded result: 7846.','Original currency conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000238','ESTIMATE','A budget is $1755. Assume 21% goes to transport. Estimate transport spending in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'368.55','Transport = 1755×21/100 dollars. Rounded result: 368.55.','Original percentage budget exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000239','ESTIMATE','A model population starts at 13000 and grows exactly 5% each year for 11 years. Estimate its final population. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'22234','Compound growth: 13000×1.05^11. Rounded result: 22234.','Original compound growth exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000240','ESTIMATE','Equipment initially costs $15600 and retains 85% of its value after each year. Estimate its value after 11 years in dollars. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'2611','Value = 15600×0.85^11 dollars. Rounded result: 2611.','Original compound depreciation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000241','ESTIMATE','A cyclist rides 39 km outward at 12 km/h and the same distance back at 18 km/h. No stops. Estimate overall average speed in km/h. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'14.4','Total distance 78 km / total time (39/12+39/18) h = 14.4 km/h. Rounded result: 14.4.','Original average speed round trip exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000242','ESTIMATE','A class has 13 students scoring 70 and 11 students scoring 90. Estimate their combined mean score in points. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'79.2','Weighted mean = (13×70+11×90)/(13+11) points. Rounded result: 79.2.','Original weighted mean exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000243','ESTIMATE','A circular garden has radius 13.2 meters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'547.4','Area = pi×radius² = 3.1416×13.2² m². Rounded result: 547.4.','Original circular area exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000244','ESTIMATE','A wheel has radius 48.1 centimeters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its circumference in centimeters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'302.2','Circumference = 2×3.1416×48.1 cm. Rounded result: 302.2.','Original circular circumference exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000245','ESTIMATE','A map scale is 1:50,000. A measured route is 22.1 cm on the map. Estimate actual route length in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'11.1','Each map centimeter represents 50,000 cm = 0.5 km; distance = 22.1×0.5 km. Rounded result: 11.1.','Original map scaling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000246','ESTIMATE','A parcel weighs 29.9 pounds. Use exactly 1 pound = 0.45359237 kg. Estimate mass in kilograms. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'13.6','Mass = 29.9×0.45359237 kg. Rounded result: 13.6.','Original unit conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000247','ESTIMATE','A 1625-megabyte file downloads at exactly 65 megabits per second. Use 8 bits per byte and decimal units; ignore overhead. Estimate seconds needed. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'200','Time = file size×8/link rate = 1625×8/65 seconds. Rounded result: 200.','Original download time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000248','ESTIMATE','A drive offers exactly 130 decimal gigabytes. Each photo occupies exactly 4 decimal megabytes. Ignoring filesystem overhead, estimate how many whole photos fit; 1 GB = 1,000 MB. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'32500','Photo count = 130×1000/4; the result is an integer. Rounded result: 32500.','Original storage capacity exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000249','ESTIMATE','A factory makes 169 items per hour at a constant rate. Estimate items produced during 187 minutes, allowing fractional theoretical output before rounding. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'527','Output = 169×187/60 items. Rounded result: 527.','Original production throughput exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000250','ESTIMATE','A solution contains 32.5 grams of dissolved salt in a final volume of 13 liters. Estimate concentration in grams per liter. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'2.5','Concentration = dissolved mass/final volume = 32.5/13 g/L. Rounded result: 2.5.','Original dilution concentration exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000251','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A hidden token is in one of 16384 numbered lockers. Each question may ask membership in any chosen subset and receives a truthful yes/no answer. Minimum worst-case questions?','["13", "14", "15", "17"]','"14"','14 bits distinguish 2^14=16384 possibilities; successive halves attain this bound.','Original binary information exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000252','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 531441 coins exactly one is heavier; all genuine coins weigh equally. With a balance scale, minimum worst-case weighings?','["12", "13", "15", "11"]','"12"','Each weighing has three outcomes. Split into three equal groups repeatedly: 12 weighings distinguish 3^12=531441 coins.','Original balance ternary search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000253','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A drawer has unlimited socks in 14 colors. Picking blindly, how many socks guarantee a matching color pair?','["16", "18", "14", "15"]','"15"','14 picks could all have different colors; pick 15 repeats a color.','Original pigeonhole socks exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000254','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag has unlimited beads in 14 colors. How many blind picks guarantee three of one color?','["32", "28", "29", "30"]','"29"','At most two per color gives 28 without a triple; the next forces three.','Original pigeonhole triples exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000255','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A club of 17 members plays one game between every unordered pair. How many games?','["135", "136", "137", "139"]','"136"','Choose two members: (17)(16)/2=136.','Original pair counting exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000256','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fence has 16 posts in a straight row, spaced 12 meters apart. Distance from first post to last, in meters?','["180", "181", "183", "179"]','"180"','There are 15 gaps, each 12 meters: 180.','Original inclusive intervals exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000257','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Four cards show 12, 26, Blue and Green. Every card has an integer on one side and exactly one of those colors on the other. To test “if the integer is divisible by 4, the color is Blue,” which shown cards must be turned?','["Blue only", "All four", "Neither numbered card nor Green", "12 and Green"]','"12 and Green"','Turn every visible multiple of 4 to check Blue, and Green to exclude a multiple on its reverse. Blue and visible nonmultiples cannot falsify the rule.','Original conditional counterexample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000258','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','No silk fabric is waterproof. All red fabrics in a sample are waterproof. What follows?','["No silk fabric exists", "All red fabrics are silk", "No red fabric in the sample is silk", "All waterproof fabrics are red"]','"No red fabric in the sample is silk"','A red silk fabric would be both waterproof and not waterproof.','Original syllogism exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000259','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','If a specimen is a fish, it has gills. If it has gills, the label reads G. The label does not read G. What follows under these rules?','["The label must read G", "The specimen is not a fish", "The specimen is a fish", "The specimen has gills"]','"The specimen is not a fish"','Not G rules out gills, and therefore rules out fish.','Original implication chains exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000260','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Either A or B is true, but not both. Neither is true. Can both statements be satisfied?','["No", "Yes, always", "Yes, only when A is false", "Yes, only when B is false"]','"No"','Exclusive or requires one true term, contradicted by neither being true.','Original conditional diagnosis exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000261','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the logical negation of “at least five tickets remain”?','["Exactly five remain", "At least six remain", "No tickets remain only", "Fewer than five tickets remain"]','"Fewer than five tickets remain"','The complement of a count at least five is a count at most four.','Original logical negation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000262','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A secret code is an integer from 1 through 14. Exactly one statement is true: A says it is even; B says it is divisible by 3. How many possible codes satisfy this?','["10", "6", "7", "8"]','"7"','Count integers in exactly one of the two sets. The valid codes are 2, 3, 4, 8, 9, 10, 14. Equivalently floor(14/2)+floor(14/3)−2floor(14/6)=7.','Original truth assignment exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000263','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Exactly four runners finish without ties. Oren finishes before Rui; Mina finishes after Rui; Paz finishes after Mina. Who finishes third?','["Paz", "Mina", "Oren", "Rui"]','"Mina"','The constraints force the order Oren → Rui → Mina → Paz; the third runner is Mina.','Original ordering constraints exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000264','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A knockout contest begins with 70 players. Every match eliminates exactly one player; no byes eliminate anyone. Matches needed for one champion?','["69", "70", "72", "68"]','"69"','Exactly 69 players must be eliminated; each match eliminates one.','Original tournament elimination exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000265','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','On a grid, go 14 steps east and 2 steps north in any order, with no other moves. How many distinct step sequences?','["121", "123", "119", "120"]','"120"','Choose the two north-step positions among 16: C(16,2)=120.','Original grid paths exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000266','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','14 different keys must be tried on one lock. Exactly one works; unsuccessful keys are never retried. Maximum trials needed to open it?','["17", "13", "14", "15"]','"14"','The working key can be last among all 14; identifying the last key is not opening the lock.','Original cycle permutation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000267','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A menu has 14 optional toppings; each can be included once or omitted independently. How many topping selections including none?','["16383", "16384", "16385", "16387"]','"16384"','Each of 14 toppings has two states, so 2^14=16384.','Original subset counts exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000268','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose a president and a different secretary from 14 candidates. How many assignments?','["182", "183", "185", "181"]','"182"','14 president choices times 13 secretary choices=182.','Original ordered arrangements exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000269','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Among 70 students, 19 study art, 17 study music, and 14 study both. How many study at least one?','["23", "25", "21", "22"]','"22"','Add the groups and subtract double-counted overlap: 19+17-14=22.','Original inclusion exclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000270','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A 12-hour clock shows 12:00. What hour number appears 27 hours later?','["6", "2", "3", "4"]','"3"','Add 27 hours and reduce modulo 12, labeling remainder zero as 12.','Original modular clock exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000271','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A board has 21 tiles, initially 12 black and the rest white. A move flips exactly two different tiles. After any number of moves, what is the largest reachable number of black tiles?','["19", "20", "21", "23"]','"20"','Each move preserves black-count parity. The largest count at most 21 with parity 0 is 20; flipping pairs of white tiles attains it.','Original parity invariant exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000272','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','An integer lies from 1 through 16383. Each trial guesses one value and reports correct, too low, or too high. Minimum worst-case trials to identify it by a correct guess?','["14", "15", "17", "13"]','"14"','With 14 trials binary search covers at most 2^14-1 values, achieved by guessing midpoints.','Original bounded search exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000273','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Two identical test sensors break above an unknown threshold in a 78-level building, and survive at or below it. The threshold is the highest safe floor, from 0 (all floors unsafe) through 78 (all floors safe). Broken sensors cannot be reused. Minimum worst-case drops to find the threshold?','["13", "15", "11", "12"]','"12"','12 drops cover a descending sum from 12 through 1, totaling 12×13/2=78 levels using decreasing gaps; 11 drops cover fewer.','Original two egg threshold exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000274','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','What is the smallest positive integer greater than 84 that is divisible by both 2 and 3?','["93", "89", "90", "91"]','"90"','Common multiples are multiples of 6; the next after 84 is 90.','Original remainders exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000275','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A prize is in exactly one of boxes numbered 1 through 14. Exactly one speaker tells the truth. A says “the box number is even”; B says “the box number is odd”; C says “the box number is divisible by 3.” How many box numbers remain possible?','["9", "10", "11", "13"]','"10"','A and B are complementary, so exactly one already tells the truth. C must be false. Excluding 4 multiples of 3 leaves 14−4=10 possibilities.','Original exactly one speaker exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/contemporary-mathematics/pages/2-introduction','logic',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000251','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 12 times. Probability that every flip is heads?','["0", "1/4096", "1/8192", "4097/8192"]','"1/4096"','Independence multiplies 12 factors of 1/2: 1/4096.','Original all heads exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000252','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is flipped independently 13 times. Probability of at least one head?','["8191/8192", "8191/16384", "16383/16384", "0"]','"8191/8192"','Take the complement of all tails: 1−(1/2)^13.','Original at least one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000253','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Flip a fair coin independently 14 times. Probability of exactly one head?','["7/16384", "8199/16384", "0", "7/8192"]','"7/8192"','Choose one head position in 14 ways, each sequence has probability 1/16384.','Original exactly one head exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000254','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A fair coin is tossed independently 15 times. Probability of exactly two heads?','["32873/65536", "0", "105/32768", "105/65536"]','"105/32768"','C(15,2)=105 qualifying sequences out of 32768.','Original binomial two successes exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000255','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A bag holds 14 red and 13 blue tokens. Every token is equally likely to be drawn once. Probability of red?','["0", "14/27", "7/27", "41/54"]','"14/27"','14 favorable tokens among 27 total.','Original one uniform draw exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000256','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two tokens uniformly without replacement from 14 red and 15 blue. Probability both are red?','["13/58", "13/116", "71/116", "0"]','"13/58"','Multiply 14/29 by 13/28.','Original without replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000257','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw twice uniformly with replacement from 14 red and 16 blue tokens. Probability both red?','["49/450", "137/225", "0", "49/225"]','"49/225"','Replacement makes independent draws with red probability 14/30; square it.','Original with replacement both red exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000258','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Draw two uniformly without replacement from 15 red and 17 blue tokens. Probability of one of each color?','["751/992", "0", "255/496", "255/992"]','"255/496"','Add red-blue and blue-red: 2×15×17/(32×31).','Original one of each color exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000259','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 84. Probability it is divisible by 3?','["0", "1/3", "1/6", "2/3"]','"1/3"','28 multiples of 3 among 84 integers.','Original uniform integer divisor exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000260','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Choose an integer uniformly from 1 through 84. Probability it is divisible by 2 or 3?','["2/3", "1/3", "5/6", "0"]','"2/3"','42 multiples of 2 plus 28 multiples of 3 minus 14 multiples of 6 gives 56/84.','Original uniform integer union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000261','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Randomly order 14 distinct books, all orders equally likely. Probability a specified book is first?','["1/28", "15/28", "0", "1/14"]','"1/14"','Each book has equal probability of any of the 14 positions.','Original random permutation first exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000262','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Arrange 16 distinct people uniformly in a straight line. Probability two specified people stand next to each other?','["9/16", "0", "1/8", "1/16"]','"1/8"','Treat the pair as a block with two internal orders: 2×(15)!/(16)!=2/16.','Original permutation adjacency exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000263','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A committee of 2 is selected uniformly from 17 distinct people. Probability a specified person is selected?','["0", "2/17", "1/17", "19/34"]','"2/17"','The person belongs to 16 of C(17,2) committees, yielding 2/17.','Original subset inclusion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000264','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A lottery chooses one winning ticket uniformly from 280 tickets. You own 14 distinct tickets. Probability you win?','["1/20", "1/40", "21/40", "0"]','"1/20"','14 of 280 equally likely tickets are yours.','Original matching lottery exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000265','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/13 and 1/15. Probability both occur?','["1/390", "98/195", "0", "1/195"]','"1/195"','For independent events multiply 1/13 by 1/15.','Original independent intersections exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000266','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent events A and B have probabilities 1/14 and 1/16. Probability at least one occurs?','["253/448", "0", "29/224", "29/448"]','"29/224"','P(A or B)=P(A)+P(B)−P(A)P(B)=29/224.','Original independent union exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000267','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A number is chosen uniformly from 1 through 56. Given it is even, probability it is also divisible by 4?','["0", "1/2", "1/4", "3/4"]','"1/2"','There are 28 even values and 14 multiples of 4 in the restricted sample.','Original conditional restricted sample exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000268','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A group has 14 engineers and 3 artists. Choose 3 people uniformly without replacement. Probability all are engineers?','["91/170", "91/340", "261/340", "0"]','"91/170"','C(14,3)/C(17,3)=364/680.','Original hypergeometric committee exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000269','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Independent attempts succeed with probability 1/3 each. Probability the first success is on attempt 12?','["1024/531441", "533489/1062882", "0", "2048/531441"]','"2048/531441"','11 failures followed by success: (2/3)^11×(1/3).','Original geometric first success exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000270','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice, each labeled 1 through 14. Probability their values match?','["15/28", "0", "1/14", "1/28"]','"1/14"','14 matching ordered pairs among 196 equally likely pairs.','Original uniform dice equality exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000271','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 15. Probability the first value is strictly greater than the second?','["0", "7/15", "7/30", "11/15"]','"7/15"','Excluding 15 ties, symmetry halves the remaining 210 ordered pairs: 14/(30).','Original uniform dice ordering exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000272','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Roll two independent fair dice labeled 1 through 16. Probability at least one shows 16?','["31/256", "31/512", "287/512", "0"]','"31/256"','Complement of neither showing the top face: 1−(15/16)².','Original dice maximum exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000273','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','A code has 12 digits chosen independently uniformly from 0–9; leading zeros allowed. Probability all digits are nonzero? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["14.12%", "9.41%", "64.12%", "28.24%"]','"28.24%"','Each digit is nonzero with probability 9/10; multiply 12 factors. This is approximately 28.24%.','Original random digit code exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000274','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Pick one of two bags by fair coin. Bag A has 14 red and 1 blue token; bag B has 1 red and 14 blue. Draw uniformly. Given red, probability bag A was chosen?','["29/30", "0", "14/15", "7/15"]','"14/15"','Bayes: (1/2×14/15)/(1/2×14/15+1/2×1/15)=14/15.','Original Bayes selected bag exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8002-000000000275','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','12 people have independent birthdays uniform over a fictional 140-day year. Probability all birthdays differ? Choose the percentage rounded to two decimal places.','["80.79%", "61.58%", "30.79%", "20.53%"]','"61.58%"','Successive birthday choices: 140/140 × 139/140 × ... × 129/140. This is approximately 61.58%.','Original sampling distinct birthdays exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-introduction','probability',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000251','ESTIMATE','For planning, a vehicle covers 238 km at a constant 155 km/h with no stops. Estimate travel time in minutes. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'92','Time = distance/speed × 60 = 238/155×60 minutes. Rounded result: 92.','Original travel time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000252','ESTIMATE','A car averages 15 liters per 100 km over a 434-km route. Estimate fuel needed in liters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'65.1','Fuel = 434×15/100 liters. Rounded result: 65.1.','Original fuel consumption exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000253','ESTIMATE','A rectangular floor is 14.5 m long and 12.25 m wide. Assuming no waste, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'177.6','Area = length×width = 14.5×12.25 m². Rounded result: 177.6.','Original area coverage exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000254','ESTIMATE','A wall area is 182 square meters. One liter covers 7 square meters per coat. For two coats, estimate liters of paint, ignoring waste. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'52.0','Paint volume = 182×2/7 liters. Rounded result: 52.0.','Original paint demand exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000255','ESTIMATE','A rectangular tank has internal dimensions 140 cm by 120 cm by 35 cm. Estimate capacity in liters; use 1 liter = 1,000 cubic cm. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'588.0','Capacity = 140×120×35/1000 liters. Rounded result: 588.0.','Original water tank exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000256','ESTIMATE','A tap delivers 16 liters per minute at a constant rate. Estimate minutes to fill an empty 378-liter tank. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'23.6','Time = volume/flow = 378/16 minutes. Rounded result: 23.6.','Original flow filling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000257','ESTIMATE','A 490-watt lamp runs for 13 hours. Estimate electricity used in kilowatt-hours. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'6.37','Energy = power×time = 490/1000×13 kWh. Rounded result: 6.37.','Original electric energy exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000258','ESTIMATE','An appliance uses 35.0 kWh. Electricity costs $0.23 per kWh with no fixed fees or taxes. Estimate cost in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'8.05','Cost = 35.0×0.23 dollars. Rounded result: 8.05.','Original energy cost exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000259','ESTIMATE','A walker takes 17276 steps, each 0.72 meters long. Estimate total distance in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'12.4','Distance = 17276×0.72/1000 km. Rounded result: 12.4.','Original walking distance exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000260','ESTIMATE','A sample of 100 identical-sized plots contains 98 trees. Assume the same density over 1,250 plots. Estimate total trees. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'1225','Scale sample count by 1250/100: 98×12.5 trees. Rounded result: 1225.','Original sample extrapolation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000261','ESTIMATE','A solid block has volume 18.2 cubic centimeters and density 2.7 grams per cubic centimeter. Estimate mass in grams. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'49.1','Mass = density×volume = 2.7×18.2 grams. Rounded result: 49.1.','Original density mass exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000262','ESTIMATE','An exchange model gives exactly 35.5 baht per US dollar, without fees. Estimate baht received for $238. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'8449','Baht = 238×35.5. Rounded result: 8449.','Original currency conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000263','ESTIMATE','A budget is $1890. Assume 22% goes to transport. Estimate transport spending in dollars. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'415.8','Transport = 1890×22/100 dollars. Rounded result: 415.8.','Original percentage budget exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000264','ESTIMATE','A model population starts at 14000 and grows exactly 5% each year for 12 years. Estimate its final population. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'25142','Compound growth: 14000×1.05^12. Rounded result: 25142.','Original compound growth exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000265','ESTIMATE','Equipment initially costs $16800 and retains 85% of its value after each year. Estimate its value after 12 years in dollars. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'2390','Value = 16800×0.85^12 dollars. Rounded result: 2390.','Original compound depreciation exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000266','ESTIMATE','A cyclist rides 42 km outward at 12 km/h and the same distance back at 18 km/h. No stops. Estimate overall average speed in km/h. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'14.4','Total distance 84 km / total time (42/12+42/18) h = 14.4 km/h. Rounded result: 14.4.','Original average speed round trip exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000267','ESTIMATE','A class has 14 students scoring 70 and 12 students scoring 90. Estimate their combined mean score in points. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'79.2','Weighted mean = (14×70+12×90)/(14+12) points. Rounded result: 79.2.','Original weighted mean exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000268','ESTIMATE','A circular garden has radius 14.2 meters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its area in square meters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'633.5','Area = pi×radius² = 3.1416×14.2² m². Rounded result: 633.5.','Original circular area exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000269','ESTIMATE','A wheel has radius 51.8 centimeters. Using pi = 3.1416, estimate its circumference in centimeters. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'325.5','Circumference = 2×3.1416×51.8 cm. Rounded result: 325.5.','Original circular circumference exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000270','ESTIMATE','A map scale is 1:50,000. A measured route is 23.8 cm on the map. Estimate actual route length in kilometers. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'11.9','Each map centimeter represents 50,000 cm = 0.5 km; distance = 23.8×0.5 km. Rounded result: 11.9.','Original map scaling exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000271','ESTIMATE','A parcel weighs 32.2 pounds. Use exactly 1 pound = 0.45359237 kg. Estimate mass in kilograms. Round to 1 decimal place. Enter only the number.',NULL,'14.6','Mass = 32.2×0.45359237 kg. Rounded result: 14.6.','Original unit conversion exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000272','ESTIMATE','A 1750-megabyte file downloads at exactly 70 megabits per second. Use 8 bits per byte and decimal units; ignore overhead. Estimate seconds needed. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'200','Time = file size×8/link rate = 1750×8/70 seconds. Rounded result: 200.','Original download time exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000273','ESTIMATE','A drive offers exactly 140 decimal gigabytes. Each photo occupies exactly 4 decimal megabytes. Ignoring filesystem overhead, estimate how many whole photos fit; 1 GB = 1,000 MB. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'35000','Photo count = 140×1000/4; the result is an integer. Rounded result: 35000.','Original storage capacity exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000274','ESTIMATE','A factory makes 182 items per hour at a constant rate. Estimate items produced during 204 minutes, allowing fractional theoretical output before rounding. Round to the nearest whole number. Enter only the number.',NULL,'619','Output = 182×204/60 items. Rounded result: 619.','Original production throughput exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60),
('43000000-0000-4000-8003-000000000275','ESTIMATE','A solution contains 35.0 grams of dissolved salt in a final volume of 14 liters. Estimate concentration in grams per liter. Round to 2 decimal places. Enter only the number.',NULL,'2.5','Concentration = dissolved mass/final volume = 35.0/14 g/L. Rounded result: 2.5.','Original dilution concentration exercise; worked solution in explanation. OpenStax: underlying mathematical methods','https://openstax.org/books/prealgebra-2e/pages/1-introduction','estimate',60)
INSERT INTO questions (id,type,prompt,choices,correct_answer,explanation,source_title,source_url,topic_id,answer_window_seconds)
VALUES
('43000000-0000-4000-8001-000000000001','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Two ropes burn unevenly along their lengths. Rope A takes 20 minutes to burn from one end and rope B takes 30. At time zero light A at both ends and B at one end. As soon as A disappears, light B at its other end. How many minutes after time zero does B disappear?','["20", "21", "19", "22"]','"20"','A finishes after 10 minutes. B then has 20 minutes of one-ended burning left; burning from both ends halves that remainder. Total = 10 + (30-10)/2 = 20. Unevenness of the rope does not affect this timing.','Original uneven-rope timing puzzle; worked solution in explanation. Background: Wikipedia Mathematical puzzle','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_puzzle','lateral',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8001-000000000002','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Two ropes burn unevenly along their lengths. Rope A takes 24 minutes to burn from one end and rope B takes 40. At time zero light A at both ends and B at one end. As soon as A disappears, light B at its other end. How many minutes after time zero does B disappear?','["27", "26", "25", "28"]','"26"','A finishes after 12 minutes. B then has 28 minutes of one-ended burning left; burning from both ends halves that remainder. Total = 12 + (40-12)/2 = 26. Unevenness of the rope does not affect this timing.','Original uneven-rope timing puzzle; worked solution in explanation. Background: Wikipedia Mathematical puzzle','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_puzzle','lateral',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8001-000000000003','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Two ropes burn unevenly along their lengths. Rope A takes 32 minutes to burn from one end and rope B takes 28. At time zero light A at both ends and B at one end. As soon as A disappears, light B at its other end. How many minutes after time zero does B disappear?','["23", "21", "22", "24"]','"22"','A finishes after 16 minutes. B then has 12 minutes of one-ended burning left; burning from both ends halves that remainder. Total = 16 + (28-16)/2 = 22. Unevenness of the rope does not affect this timing.','Original uneven-rope timing puzzle; worked solution in explanation. Background: Wikipedia Mathematical puzzle','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_puzzle','lateral',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8001-000000000004','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Two ropes burn unevenly along their lengths. Rope A takes 40 minutes to burn from one end and rope B takes 34. At time zero light A at both ends and B at one end. As soon as A disappears, light B at its other end. How many minutes after time zero does B disappear?','["28", "26", "29", "27"]','"27"','A finishes after 20 minutes. B then has 14 minutes of one-ended burning left; burning from both ends halves that remainder. Total = 20 + (34-20)/2 = 27. Unevenness of the rope does not affect this timing.','Original uneven-rope timing puzzle; worked solution in explanation. Background: Wikipedia Mathematical puzzle','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_puzzle','lateral',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8001-000000000005','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Two ropes burn unevenly along their lengths. Rope A takes 48 minutes to burn from one end and rope B takes 50. At time zero light A at both ends and B at one end. As soon as A disappears, light B at its other end. How many minutes after time zero does B disappear?','["37", "38", "36", "39"]','"37"','A finishes after 24 minutes. B then has 26 minutes of one-ended burning left; burning from both ends halves that remainder. Total = 24 + (50-24)/2 = 37. Unevenness of the rope does not affect this timing.','Original uneven-rope timing puzzle; worked solution in explanation. Background: Wikipedia Mathematical puzzle','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_puzzle','lateral',180),
