('43000000-0000-4000-8001-000000000033','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','Four people take 5, 6, 18 and 30 minutes to cross a bridge. At most two cross together, at the slower person''s pace. It is dark: every crossing needs the one torch, which must be carried back as needed. All start on the near side. Minimum minutes to get everyone across?','["53", "54", "52", "55"]','"53"','One optimal schedule is out: 5,6; back: 5; out: 18,30; back: 6; out: 5,6, totaling 53 minutes. Enumerating all bank/torch states and selecting the least-cost path proves minimality; a return trip cannot be omitted.','Original bridge-and-torch planning puzzle; worked solution in explanation. Background: Wikipedia Mathematical puzzle','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_puzzle','lateral',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8001-000000000034','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','8 people must cross a river in one boat carrying at most 2 people. Everybody can row; nobody swims. The boat starts with them, and all people and the boat must finish on the other bank. What is the minimum number of one-way crossings?','["14", "13", "12", "15"]','"13"','Every return carries at least one person. With r returns, there are r+1 outward trips, so at most (r+1)*2-r = 2+r*1 people finish across. Thus r must be at least 6. Send full outward boats and return one rower to attain that bound: 13 crossings.','Original return-trip capacity puzzle; worked solution in explanation. Background: Wikipedia Mathematical puzzle','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_puzzle','lateral',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8001-000000000035','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','9 people must cross a river in one boat carrying at most 3 people. Everybody can row; nobody swims. The boat starts with them, and all people and the boat must finish on the other bank. What is the minimum number of one-way crossings?','["8", "6", "7", "9"]','"7"','Every return carries at least one person. With r returns, there are r+1 outward trips, so at most (r+1)*3-r = 3+r*2 people finish across. Thus r must be at least 3. Send full outward boats and return one rower to attain that bound: 7 crossings.','Original return-trip capacity puzzle; worked solution in explanation. Background: Wikipedia Mathematical puzzle','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_puzzle','lateral',180),
('43000000-0000-4000-8001-000000000036','MULTIPLE_CHOICE','12 people must cross a river in one boat carrying at most 4 people. Everybody can row; nobody swims. The boat starts with them, and all people and the boat must finish on the other bank. What is the minimum number of one-way crossings?','["8", "6", "9", "7"]','"7"','Every return carries at least one person. With r returns, there are r+1 outward trips, so at most (r+1)*4-r = 4+r*3 people finish across. Thus r must be at least 3. Send full outward boats and return one rower to attain that bound: 7 crossings.','Original return-trip capacity puzzle; worked solution in explanation. Background: Wikipedia Mathematical puzzle','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_puzzle','lateral',180),
